      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>NO P mtr it R. E CROP A u</head>
        <p>NO P mtr it R. E CROP A u</p>
        <p>There is no surplus labour to deal with laid crops, and every Farmer should rely on standing crops by sowing only</p>
        <p>Stiff- Ga t 4 ns Strawed Oats</p>
        <p>which include our NEW REGENERATED BREEDS for 1916 of The Record, The Yielder, Tartar King, Supreme, etc.</p>
        <p>Thousands of pounds have been lost in past years through laid crops. Then, labour was plentiful and laid crops were harvested by hand without great trouble or inconvenience. Now, however, it is an impossibility t 6 deal with laid crops, and every farmer must see that he assures for himself a straight- forward harvest by sowing only those varieties which can be relied upon to stand up to the time of cutting. Send for Now Illustrated Catalogue, with full particulars. to</p>
        <p>GARTONS SEED (Ltd.), EXPERTS, Warrington</p>
        <p>and nail each one to the n-aIL When the plants begin to grow in the spring, each of these shoots will throw out tendrils, which will cling to the wall, and there will be no more need of any nailing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>STRAWBERRIES.</head>
        <p>STRAWBERRIES.</p>
        <p>Many people on the score of tidiness may at this season be seen busy digging between their lines of strawberries. No greater mistake than this can be made, and there is nothing that so prejudices the coming crop. This digging destroys the surface roots, which should receive every encouragement in the way of rich top.. dressings of rotten manure. The manure should be well worked in round the crown of each plant—not burying the crown, but so placing the manure that the roots may be encouraged to take hold of .it, and thus help the plants when sending up the bloom spikes, and swelling off the fruit. Many people, too, cut off the old leaves with a scythe. These had better be left, as they shelter the crowns, the young leaves gradually pushing their way up. and during the time .this is going on they are protected, until they become hardened and impervious to injury from late frosts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>YELLOW-FLESHED TURNIPS.</head>
        <p>YELLOW-FLESHED TURNIPS.</p>
        <p>It is very strange to find the yellow-fleshed turnip such a favourite in the North, while it is but seldom grown in the South. . When cooked there can be no question as to the superior quality of the yellow-fleshed turnip, the flesh being far firmer, of better flavour, and when served,- to my mind, more attractive. I have often heard it said that in the South the flavour of the yellow-fleshed turnip is strong, but when it is given good culture in the way of deep, well-manured soil, and grown on without a check, I have never noticed this. The culti- vation has often a great deal to do with the flavour, and very often it happens that the white turnips are not only hard but bitter, and for this reason very objectionable. A friend of mine grows only the yellow-fleshed turnips, and finds that in the kitchen they are usually pre- ferred to the white-fleshed varieties.—" Wick," in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>ANKLE LIKE RAW MEAT.</head>
        <p>ANKLE LIKE RAW MEAT.</p>
        <p>Wonderful Zam-Buk Averts Operation.</p>
        <p>With foot and ankle like raw meat and help- lessly crippled, Mrs. M. J. Shepherd was faced with a dreaded surgical operation. Zam-Buk, however, came to her aid, and Mrs. Shepherd's foot is now quite healthy and strong again. At her home, 50, Basil-street, Vavasour-street, Rochdale, the happy mother said to a local reporter:— "I ran a rusty nail in my right leg ten years ago. Blood-poison set in, and try as I would I couldn't get the sore place to heal. A year ago a scratch on my right ankle started a very bad festering. • The whole leg became terribly swollen, and my ankle and foot were just like raw meat.</p>
        <p>"There were six or seven different sores, which later formed into one big raw place. I could not get a shoe on, and was helplessly crippled. All night long I was kept awake 'by the awful pain. My leg and foot were in a really terrible state, and I was advised by several doctors to go into the Infirmary for an operation. "I had practically made up my mind to be operated on when my eldest daughter urged me to try. Zam-Buk, which she felt sure would heal my awful sores. "So I got some Zam-Buk from the drug stores and persevered carefully with the balm. To my great joy, the pain gradually died away, and Zam-Buk was so soothing that I got plenty of sleep. The sores, too, stopped discharging and began to heal up. "I got more Zam-Buk and dressed my foot every day with it. Soon I found myself able to do my own housework, and later I went back to my work at the mill. Thanks to Zam-Buk, all the sores are now cleansed and healed. A new skin has grown over my leg and foot." Nothing else can heal so quickly or so cleanly as Zam-Buk. Whether the case is one of eczeng, ringworm, bad legs, piles, sore head, poisoned wounds or simple cuts, bruises, burn or scald, Zam-Buk can be depended upon to do good.</p>
        <p>A Permanent Blush.</p>
        <p>One can now have a little blush tattooed on her face that is warranted "not to come out in the wash" or to yield to the weather, says a woman's magazine. It will not come and go, of course, like the delicate flush with which novelists so conveniently endow their heroines, and one is not sure whether it is not better to trust to Providence for an occasional blush than to wear it continually and never be able to pale on occasion. If one did naturally blush, moreover, with the "healthy flush ", already placed there by artificiality's artful aid, good- ness alone, knows what the effect would be. Our last state of rosiness might be worse than our first of pallor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE MAIZE CROP.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries have received the following information from the International Agricultural Institute:— The production of maize in the United States is estimated at 1,526,826,000 cwts., or 14.3 per cent. above the 1914 crop. _</p>
        <p>Including the new data on the Canadian crop, the total production of wheat in 1915 (Northern Hemisphere) and 1915-16 (Southern Hemisphere) in the following countries amounts to 2,146,845,000 cwts., compared with 1,710,500,000 cwts. for the previous year, or 25.5 per cent. above the latter amount :—Hun- gary, Bulgaria, Denmark, Spain, France, Great Britain and Ireland, Italy, Luxemburg, Nor- way, Netherlands, Roumania, Russia in Europe, Switzerland, Canada, United States, India, Japan, Russia in Asia, Egypt, Tunis, Argentina and Australia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FOOT NOTES.</head>
        <p>FOOT NOTES.</p>
        <p>The young horse's hoofs should receive care. for neglect of the hoofs is the cause of many nnsoundnesses in the animal when he grows older. Some of the troubles that are caused by this means are ringbones and sidebones. The animal should be kept standing well on his feet. The tendency in the young hoofs, espe- cially on pasture, is for the toes to grow long, thereby throwing too great a strain on the ligaments and soft tissue in the hoof. The toes of the hoof should therefore be trimmed down at frequent intervals, and the rest of the hoof wall trimmed to prevent the breaking off of large pieces, a- thing which leads to cracks in the hoof walls. Horses tend to knuckle over when tired from overdriving, or it may be due to lack of proper shoeing, or to standing idle on a board floor. Drive or work the horse lightly every day. Allow him a box stall in the 'stable. Have him shod once a month. Hand rub the joints and back tendons of the weak legs each time he comes into the stable, and then snugly bandage with flannel.—" Farm Journal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ARE PHEASANTS DESTRUCTIVE?</head>
        <p>ARE PHEASANTS DESTRUCTIVE?</p>
        <p>"Gareth," in the "Referee," writes :—One of the leading authorities on game from the sport- ing and natural history points of view is Mr. Tom Speedy, who had an article in the "Scotsman" not long since inquiring into the question whether pheasants and partridges were inimical to agriculture? He was requested to give expert evidence in a case dealing with the alleged destruction of farm produce by phea- sants, and, desiring to do the thing thoroughly, he wrote to a number of landowners, factors, and gamekeepers all over Scotland, asking them to send him the crops of pheasants. The result was that he received twenty crops. Scarcely two of them, he states, contained the same kind of food, and though it was seed time, in only two of them did he find any grain. In a Ber- wickshire bird there were seventeen grains of barley, in another from Perthshire 33 grains of oats, and he was convinced that the pheasants were not guilty of the destruction alleged; that they had, in fact, merely picked up the seeds on the surface; because pheasants do not use their feet to scape with as domestic poultry do. A couple of years ago, Mr. Speedy relates,, a farmer complained that pheasants were "play- ing the deuce" with his young wheat, which had then sprouted an inch or so above the ground. A number of the birds were shot and no trace of wheat was found in any one of them. They had discovered food which they evidently liked much better—roots, &amp;c. Alto- gether, Mr. Speedy thoroughly acquits the pheasants of the charges which were brought against them on these occasions, though he does not pretend that they never eat grain or injure farm produce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>IVY ON THE HOUSE WALLS.</p>
        <p>Many people have an idea that the dampness on walls overgrown by ivy is caused by the ivy This is a mistake. The trouble is really aue to allowing the growths of the ivy to invade the gutters, thus choking them, and causing the water to overflow down the walls. The same thing happens which Ampelopsis Veitchi is allowed to choke up the spouts. Ivy to be kept in good condition should be cut hard into the wall every year, the month of April being the best time. The plants after the cutting in will at once start into growth, and fresh green leaves will cover the front of the house. The cutting in also allows of all the dead leaves and rubbish, including sparrows' nests, which are very often found under the eaves, being cleared away. As ivy can always be had in pots, it can be planted at any time with very little risk of failure. When planting, it is always well to add some loam and a little manure, and as the soil close up to the wall of a house is, as a rule, very dry, a good soaking of water helps considerably in the re-establish- ing of the plants. After the plants are placed in position, carefully entwine all the shoots,,</p>
      </div>
