      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>BUSINESS</head>
        <p>BUSINESS</p>
        <p>le&amp;141-11S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 5, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 5, 1916.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS IN THE POULTRY YARD.</p>
        <p>Lessons Of Experience.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Brown, F.L.S., in an elaborate review of the poultry industry in 1915, when we had a full year of war conditions, during which a world-wide revolution in the matter of production and consumption, generally, paints to the lessons taught by experience for poultry-keepers in particular. A New Era.</p>
        <p>The year 1915 will, I venture to suggest, mark a new era in this branch of production so far as the United Kingdom is concerned. The present is the twenty-first of these annual re- views that I have been permitted to issue. At no previous period have opportunities for our own people, whether as to demand met with and prices obtainable for eggs and poultry, been so great as now. Will they rise to the occasion? is the question, affording a wide field for discussion. My immediate purpose, how- ever, is to deal with the year just ended, and to learn how far its experience can help to- wards future development. First, I desire to bring under notice a few striking facts, commending these to careful consideration. The imports of eggs in 1915 show a reduction of ninety-four thousand four hun- dred and thirty-three tons from 1913—that is, the last complete year prior to the war; and the imports of poultry show a reduction of six thousand one hundred -and one tons be- tween the same periods. Quantities of eggs in 1915 were practically the same as in 1890, the values of poultry (comparative quantities are not available) as in 1898. So far as eggs are concerned, it is suggestive to show the quan- tities and values in the two years named Quantities. Values. Great hundreds. 1890 ... • 10,291,246 ... 3,428,802 1915 ... 10,247,960 ... 6,122,970</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>43,286 dee.</head>
        <p>43,286 dee.</p>
        <p>£3,205,832 inc.</p>
        <p>Nearly twice as much money had to lac paid in 1915 for about the same volume of eggs twenty-five years before.</p>
        <p>Cost of Foods.</p>
        <p>It is evident that large numbers of fowls were killed off or sold in the winter of 1914-1915. We have no figures for Britain, but: Irish returns show a reduction Of more than 3 per cent. The owners feared an increase in the cost of feeding stuffs, which anticipation has been justified, mainly due to .enormous advances in freight. ages. In not a few instances half the present prices of imported corn represent shipment charges. When that is the case, home-grown grain increases in value to the same extent. What poultry-keepers did not expect was that the sale prices of eggs and poultry—the former especially—would advance to the same or a greater degree. My view is that the increased cost of feeding stuffs will be an ultimate gain. We have been extravagant in feeding. Now the business is to reconsider the whole Question, getting rid of many notions and predilections. Not what is most easily obtained or handled, bid what is economically the best should be sought for. Unfortunately we have no insti- tution devoted to research or expetiment to help In finding a solution of this question. To Meet Increased Home Demand.</p>
        <p>Much has been done to impress upon pro- ducers of all grades the importance of incras- ing home supplies. The three Boards of 'Agri- culture have constantly urged the keeping f more and better poultry, and results are mani- festing themselves. Some local authorities have loyally responded, and one can hope ,all will, be brought into line following upon the formation of War .Food Committees. Early in the year the writer undertook, at the request of the English Board of Agriculture, an inquiry into the poultry industry of Wales. The report issued in October is already bearing fruit. Steps are being taken to encourage and develop poultry-keeping on improved and extended lines in the Principality. A considerable number of breeding stations have been opened, and a couple of hatching stations established. In- creased teaching facilities will shortly be avail- able in different parts of Britain. That is all to the good, and necessary. What has greater significance is the increased demand for stock birds, indicative of a movement among the people at large. Testimony is to hand that never before have breeders of utility stock had so great a sale as during this autumn and winter, confirmed by the keen competition at auctions in market towns. In this direction I hope farmers have realised the mistake made by them last year in reducing their flocks of poultry, and are now redressing the balance. In that respect the War Food Committees formed in each county, on the suggestion of Lord Selborne, President of the English Board of Agriculture, ought to exert a wide influence.</p>
        <p>Backyard Poultry. Much has been done, especially by the Press, to advocate what may -he termed backyard poultry-keeping in urban areas. It would be of great service if multitudes of consumers could provide for their households in this way. If a hundred thousand additional householders did that and no more, they could produce a hundred million of eggs and a million chickens annually at small cost, the food value of which would be upwards of .R 500,000 yearly. Oppor- tunity is often denied, yet multitudes could profitably use such space as they already have: There are, however, dangers to be guarded against—namely, unless cleanliness is rigidly maintained, urban poultry-keeping becomes a nuisance and perhaps a source of disease, and birds kept under these conditions are unsuit- able for breeding purposes.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c. EGUS from selected Pen of Blue Orpingtons, excellent layers. 51- sitting.—Heaton, Gordon- vine, Parbold, near Wigan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>•An Enthusiast Indeed. In the course of his fine tribute to the memory of Scotland's immortal poet at the local celebration of " Bur-r-ns nicht," Captain Mathams took occasion to reply to, detractors who affirm that the beloved " Bobbie " was a drunkard. I once overhead a little argument on this very point between one of these critics and an indignant and scornful Scot. Said the former, "Why, man, I know of a midden on which your hero not once nor twice lay all night in a drunken stupor," and he was plainly non- plussed when the Scot, °with evident sincerity and earnestness besought him to disclose the precise situation of that sacred manure heap, that he might forthwith go on pilgrimage thereto!</p>
        <p>Sunday Trading. As to this, I am disposed to share the opinion of 'Well-wisher," whose letter appeared in last week's Lymm news, though perhaps my approval of that yi6w may be put on more general grounds. I have always found that a certain) class of dealers will open their shops on Sundays whatever objectors may say or do. I have repeatedly known the police stirred up to action against them, but these distasteful and unpopular prosecutions under an ancie it Statute have invariably defeated the chief aim of the well-intentioned instigators, and have finally htid to be abandoned in despair. The remedy is with the public, and is simple enough. Let those who perceive that Sunday trading is a grossly unfair form of competition against respectable shopkeepers wisely observ- ing • the day of rest give their custom ex- clusively to the latter and leave the offenders to their thoughtless and mistaken supporters—and to their unenviable reflections.</p>
        <p>Crime: Its Causes And Antidote. This was the subject for consideration at Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church last Sunday. The preacher referred particularly to juvenile offenders. I have myself touched upon that problem from time to time, and have noticed in this column what is being done by means of the Borstal system. I happen just to have glanced over Dr. James Devon's "The Criminal ,and the Community," and in that timely Ontribution to the discussion ot vexed question the experienced author remarks with cutting significtince that the expert may know a good deal about the offender, but it has been proved over and over again that he does not know how ,to reform him. Of course, one who writes thus must be ready with his alternative proposition. Dr. Devon declares that unless the criminal is brought into sym- pathetic contact with someone in the com- munity, who will enable him to resist tempta- tion and encourage him in well-doing, he never does reform. He holds that a prison ought merely to be a place of detention in which offenders are placed till some proper provision is made for their supervision and means of livelihood in the community; and goes on to urge that alike in the case of the young offender and the old, the only safety for the citizens and the only chance of reformatibn for the culprit lie in his being boarded out under proper care and guardianship in the community. "The proper guardian for one," he is careful to add, "would not be proper for another." No, indeed! Who would care to admit the gentle Mr. William Sikes into the family circle as an alternative to prison discipline? "Na, no, it winna dae"—for hardened criminals, that is; though as to first offenders of tender years it might well be worth while to experiment along some such lines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Prevention.</head>
        <p>Prevention.</p>
        <p>After all, this were far preferable to cure, and •in a Yorkshire town in which I lived a while an officer from a distant part of the country, who was appointed chief constable, openly boasted that ere long he would inake it possible for any member of the community to suspend his watch by the chain from the arm of a lamppost by night, in sure and certain hope of finding it there next morning. I had heard others talk like that, and with growing scepticism awaited developments. Suffice it to state that when I left those parts the form of street decoration—or lunacy—in question had not begun to manifest itself.</p>
        <p>A More Dangerous Class. The foregoing has largely to do with the poor and the ignorant, but it may be well to suggest for once in a While that the apparently most wicked man may not really 'be the most danger- ous. One who has lost his character is surely incapable of inflicting as much harm as due who yet passes as respectable. Nevertheless, we are more concerned with the relatively , small offenders than with the bold, educated scoundrels who turn their talents to systematic swindling on a substantial scale. In other words, we "heed the pin-prick more than the blow at the heart." The time has assuredly come to realise that no veneer of religion, philanthropy, or education can atone for tam- pering with the social order. Sinners must be properly graded—and properly grilled according to their deserts. As to these, whatever may be said about juvenile offenders, th'ere should be no mollycoddling: Would a ship's passengers be likely to lynch pilferers and excuse miscreants caught boring with augurs in the vessel's bottom? I recall the line of argument pursued by a writer in the "Atlantic Monthly" some years ago. He denounced as serious error the presumption that offenders ought to be graded according to badness of character or according to the harm they inflict upon particular in- dividuals, and the vain imagination that there are excellences which eonaitute a sufficient set-off to sin. The American writer, reviewing trans-Atlantic conditions, reasonably put it that the highwayman, with his alternative, "Your money or your life!" does less mischief than the entrenched monopolist, who robs the public in other ways. The government clerk who secretly markets advanced crop informa- tion would hardly steal overcoats, whereas the hall thief is equal to the whole gamut of larceny. The life insurance presidents who let one another have the use of policy-holders' funds at a third of the market rate may still be trusted not -to purloin spoons. The official who sells the opportunity to use the mails is an accomplice in wholesale robbery; but for all that he has his scruples against pocket-picking. Yet when, as occasionally happens, one in high places is brought to justice, his punishment is incomparable With that of the common or garden convict. Rough On The Orator. • I cannot get away from the theme; it is in- terminable. For further example, one has heard street corner tub-thumpers holding forth on the land question and maintaining that it belongs to the people because at some remote period it was stolen from them. I remember an excellent story of such a speaker, who was suddenly in- terrupted by a lady. "This gentleman," she announced, "complains that the rich own the land, because their ancestors stole it. Stole it from whom? From their ancestors. And from whom did they steal it? From the Ancient' Britons. And they stole it from the prehistoric inhabitants of these islands, who stole it from the monkeys, and now this gentleman wants his property back again !"</p>
        <p>Of The Bygones. • • It occurred to zne a while ago that readers who, like myself, have attained the grand climac- teric might be interested in a contemporary's recollections of certain forms of amusement in the bygones, long before it had entered into the heart of man to conceive of the cinema and other modern •attractions; but I have not yet been able to indulge in any such reminders, or to tell the. Younger generation of readers any- thing of public entertainers who flourished in the days to 'which their fathers sometimes refer and in the old times before them. Pressure of other matter has hitherto put all such things out of the question. V freelY to indulge in thelhi recollections permitted very lections to which age is prone. I must try, to avoid its proverbial garrulity, or I, who now gratefully acknowledge the generous hospitality of these columns, shall be in danger of the editorial judgment. How- ever. I will continue to bear in mind that bit of autobiography (so to express it) which I have been contemplating of late.</p>
        <p>A Wonder. Mistress: "1 see you. had a card from your young man at the Front,. Mary." Mary: " Yes'ni. And wasn't it a saucy one! wonder it passed the sentry."--" Punch."</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PURSES OF GOLD.</head>
        <p>PURSES OF GOLD.</p>
        <p>Fathers Wilson And Primavesi Honoured.</p>
        <p>WHY POPULAR PRIEST LEFT.</p>
        <p>Some of his parishioners having been curious as to why he left St. Mary's Roman Catholic Church a few months ago, after nearly twenty years' association with them, the Rev. Father Wilson gave his explanation on Tuesday even- ing, when he was presented. with a purse of gold in St. Mary's Hall. "No doubt you have wondered why I left St. Mary's," he said, "and you probably know •in some sort of way that my leaving was not forced upon me. Well, I might as well admit that I asked to be relieved of the burden of St. Mary's simply and solely because I felt it was getting beyond my strength. I loved St. Mary's and its people fur too much to remain here, though it would have been very pleasant for me to have ended my days here. But I felt that if I remained I could not do justice to the work of the parish. Nothing would have grieved me more if I had seen the work of the parish thrown back through my own incapacity due to failing health and strength. I .should not like you to imagine that I got tired of Warrington or its people. I was only tired of the work, because, as you know, one's strength and buoyancy will not last for ever, and mine were •beginning to fail. I saw that I was no longer • strong enough for the work which Father Hind has taken up, and I may say that I do not know anyone whom. I would sooner have had to succeed. me than he." (Ap- plause.) • As indicated, these remarks were a portion of Father Wilson's speech at a presentation ceremony: Father Primavesi, who had also recently been transferred from St. Mary's, was honoured with a similar gift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>NOTABLE OCCASION.</head>
        <p>A NOTABLE OCCASION.</p>
        <p>The occasion was, indeed, notable. The hall was crowded with well-wishers, amongst whom were the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) and about a dozen priests, who occupied the front row of seats in the area. The platform was packed with children singing gaily. It will be recollected that Father Wilson, after nineteen years' incumbency at St. Mary's, went to Broomedge, near Preston, and Father Primavesi, after thirteen yeArs' residence to the very day, proceeded to rage Hill, Liver- pool. At Tuesday evening's function, Father Wil- son's successor, Father Hind, was the first .to pay the reverend gentleman tribute, and later referred to the presence of the Mayor. "His presence here," he said, "is only one of the many acts of kindness he has done to us.- He seems to be ever ready to do acts of kindness or show sympathy to anyone. We Catholics have certainly had more than our share of his Worship's sympathy and assi%tance in many ways." (Applause.) Mr. „J. Eckersley made the presentations, making a happy speech in doing .so. "I have known bath of the reverend Fathers longer than any person in the room with the excep- tion of Father Hind," he observed.' "My first interview with Father Wilson was a very strik- ing one and he made a very great impression upon me. He was responsible for the discip- line of the college, and I thought at that time that the principal part of his duty was the giving of the stick. (Loud laughter.) Two or three of those on the platform will agree with me that he was a first-rate hand with the stick." (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>Not All Saints.</p>
        <p>In all the years Father Wilson had been at Warrington he had shown the greatest anxiety for their spiritual Welfare, and if they were not all saints it was not his fault, because he had given them every opportunity by means of retreats and special services; he had been specially careful that children should be in- structed at an early age. Then, again, Father Wilson had done a great deal of work for, the schools, and, among the improvements he had been responsible for in the church, were the rebuilding of the -organ, and the erection of the tower and bells. (Applause.) The Catho- lics of Warrington owed a deep debt of grati- tude to him indeed. Father Wilson had done a great deal of public work. :Unfortunately, owing to business and other reasons, Catholics did not take a particularly active part in. public work, but while Father Wilson was amongst them he saw that their interests were not neglected. Whatever he did, and wherever he worked, appreciation always: followed hits. (Applause.) With regard' to Father Prima- vesi, the speaker said they would never forget his tireless energy and devotion to them. (Ap- plause.) For them both they wished the joys of long life and peace, and, when the end came, the joys of Heaven. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FATHER WILSON EMBARRASSED.</head>
        <p>FATHER WILSON EMBARRASSED.</p>
        <p>In replying, Father Wilson said that al- though the situation was very embarrassing— he had never had a similar experience in his life before-,-he must admit it was very grati- fying. There was no doubt that he had taken a very great interest in the parish. It was the first he had ever had charge of. When he came he fully made up his mind not to have any- thing to do with bricks and mortar, or with public work. (Laughter.) his ideal was to devote himself entirely to the clerical and spiritual work of the parish, but, in one way or another, he fell from his ideal. 'After all, however, he did not regret it. He had been literally forced into bricks and mortar on several occasions, and he thought they were all the better for the building schemes which had materialised. (Applause.) Ile was also glad he had introduced the good nuns to assist. in the training of the young people in the parish, and he now made public acknowledg- ment of the excellent work they had done. in the past, were doing now, ,and, he hoped, would continue to do.</p>
        <p>With regard to his public work, Father Wil- son said he had no regrets. On all sides he had found that members of the Corporation were willing to recognise Catholics and give them their proper, position as full and entire citizens of Warrington. (Applause.) In leav- ing Warrington, one thing was quite certain: he should never be settled in any place which would give him as much interest and to which he should be so much attached; nor should he be anywhere where he could expect to find the same response, appreciation and affection he had experienced here. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FATHER PRIMAVESI-SERIOUS AND FACETIOUS.</head>
        <p>FATHER PRIMAVESI-SERIOUS AND FACETIOUS.</p>
        <p>Alternately serious and facetious, Father Primavesi made an interesting speech, which ceased abruptly at the oncoming of the tides of emotion. lie said that during the thirteen years he . laboured with Father Wilson as a curate he experienced what all other curates working with him felt—that when he gave them any work .to do he treated them as free lances, allowing them to launch forth and do their best. From him they received every en.eouragement. In going over the past, he (the speaker) recol- lected much work attempted, some failures, and, he hoped, also a few sheaves of golden- corn. (Applause.) What had been accomplisled was in no small measure due to Father Wilson. "The position of a curate beginning parish work re- minds me of a young doctor going into an in- firmary," he said. "lie goes • there with con- siderable knowledge, but no experience, and immediately begins to practise on the poor bodies of the people. It is just the same, except in another sense, with a young curate. When he comes to. a parish he has a certain amount of knowledge, but has no experience of the work, and, as you parishionerS allow us to practise upon you, we owe you a great deal of thanks. (Laughter.. and applause.) But at the same time, when I was practising upon you—(laugh-: ter)—l could not help but• grow iutimate with you, and be attached to you. This had been reciprocated, I think." (Applause.) Cockhedge Rallies to Colours.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father said he was' thrilled at the way Cockhedge had risen to the nation's need, and rallied to the Colours. Of its men its houses had almost become depopulated. (Applause.) Cockhedge had done much for St. Mary's, and St. Mary's had done much for Cockhedge. Cock- hedge had a rough nature, but underlying it was a big heart. M time went by, and when he was a hermit on a lonely and distant hill in</p>
        <p>Liverpool—(laughter)—he would continually think of dear old Cockhedge and its people. He thanked them from the bottom of his heart for the present. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ffff</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MAYOR ON PAST CONTROVERSIES.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR ON PAST CONTROVERSIES.</p>
        <p>Wishing to pay the town's tribute to Father Wilson, the Mayor referred particularly to the reverend gentleman's public work. Father Wilson, he said, had been on the Education Committee since 1902, and had been the best attender on that committee. He had attended no fewer than 850 committee meetings; (Ap- plause.) He (the Mayor) doubted whether the reverend Father know that himself. (Laugh- ter.) In all those years he had hardly missed a meeting. The result of Father Wilson's pre- sence had to a very 'large extent helped to ease the controversy between those of the Pro- testant and. Roman Catholic Faiths. In War- rington there had been no trouble at all, and, as Mayor, he stated fearlessly that it was chiefly due to the influence of the Roman Catholic representatives on. the Education Committee, Father Wilson had said that he did not come to Warrington with the intention of doing public •work, but he went in for it, and did it well. (Applause.) He the Mayor) wished the reurend Fathers would stir up their people to take a larger share in public work. Those who • had done -so had been amongst the very best workers, and he was sure it would not only benefit themselves and their community, but the whole of the 'town. Warrington had a lot to thank St. Mary's for beautiful bells, which sometimes wakened them up a little too soon—(laughter and ap- plause)—the fine tower, which could be seen from Warrington Bridge, and that commodious building in which they were then met. Other communities might well follow Father Wil- son's brick-laying schemes. (Laughter and applause.) He. (the -Mayor) was present out of pure affection for Fathers Wilson and Primavesi. The latter had done noble work for the Belgians, and was one of those who met them on coming into Warrington so many months ago. He knew their language, and had been of great assistance to them. It was to be hoped that the usefulness which the reverend gentlemen had been to the town and district would continue wherever they went in the future. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ENTERTAINMENT.</head>
        <p>THE ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>The foregoing speeches were sandwiched into an excellent programme of miscellaneous items —mostly musical. The school children contri- buted part-songs, under the leadership of Mr. A. Kilburn; Miss E. Green sang "Friend of Mine" and "My Dear Soul"; Miss Richard- son "Ave •Ataxia" and "Angus .Macdonald"; and Mr. R. llenegan "Good Company" and "They all love Jack." Mr. A. Kilburn played the violin and Father P. Willson contributed a pianoforte solo, entitled "Rustle ef Spring" (Sinding). In a -white tie and straw hat (look- ing the acme of a, seaside stroller) Mr. W. Cain presented humorous -Lancashire dialect songs, a feature of which was that Mr. James Cald- well, a blind gentleman, 'accompanied him on the piano. For the soloists generally, Miss A. Eckenley and Miss A. Richardson were at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>NATURAL HISTORY.</head>
        <p>NATURAL HISTORY.</p>
        <p>Mr. L. Greening's Notable Gift To Museum.</p>
        <p>A LIFE'S LABOUR.</p>
        <p>For again favouring the Museum with the. benefit of his labours in natural history, .Mr. Li 1111031113 Greening was heartily thankecl at the Town Council on Tuesday evening. This time the gift consists of: MS. notes and records by donor on the natural history of the district; miscropes, collections of micro-slides, natural history specimens, periodicals, pamphlets, cuttings _</p>
        <p>and collecting apparatus and materials.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett mentioned that on four previous occasions Mr. Greening had presented handsome gifts; in fact, they hdd received from him almost enough to stock a museum. The donor had devoted the greater part of his leisure time to natural history studies, and unlike *some collectors, had again and again given the town the benefit of his labours. (Hear, hear.) He had taken what might be termed an ancestral interest in the Museum. When the lower portion of Buttermarket-street was being demolished they discovered, with great interest, a room which was said to have been used Its a museum by Mr. Greening's grandfather. He (Alderman Bennett) believed it was the • first natural history museum—or one of them—in Warrington. (A pplause.)</p>
        <p>Councillor lien-shall said that really the gift was prodigious, an its peculiar value was that it would not fade aNd lose its value, but in fifty years from now would be priceless, because the data on which the results were grounded would have disappeared. Councillor Joseph observed that Mr. Greening had shown not only great generosity. but a large amount of sacrifice, in giving up a life's labour. It was a well-known fact amongst natural his- torians that there were certain specimens pecu- liar to a district—just as there were peculiar specimens of humanity in different districts— (laughter)—and Mr. Greening's gift illustrated that truth very forcibly. (Applause.) MRS. SHIPMAN'S PICTURES.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett also specially mentioned the loan of three ' pictures to the Museum by Mrs. Shipman. He added that he believed the Museum was beginning to justify itself, and, he hoped, to become popular. In his opinion, it was most foolish economy on the part of the Government to close museums, as they were a useful instrument in education.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS.</p>
        <p>Members Give Up Their Lives On Land And Sea.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the National Independ- ent Order of Oddfellows was held on Saturday at the Cattle Market Inn.</p>
        <p>To give the delegates an opportunity to show their last tribute of .respect to the late brother, P.P.G.M. E. E. .Pester, the meeting was post- poned for an hour. The Secretary read the accounts and balance sheet, which were accepted. The district had had thirteen deaths during the past year, three members being killed in action and one drowned on H.M.S. Royal Edward. A committee was formed to make arrange- ments for the Lancashire and Cheshire confer- ence in this town in April. The election of officers for 1916 was as follows: G.M., W. G. Burgess; D.G.M., N. Lawton; treasurer, W. Pennington i and secretary, J. J. Wilson. P.P.G.M. W. Pennington made a pre- sentation- on behalf of the district to the retir- ing G.M., G. Crozier, expressing the satisfaction he had given in the manner be had discharged his duties during the year. • Bro. Crozier, in returning thanks, spoke of the critical time friendly societies were going through during the war. There were about 80 members of the district at present serving their country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>OVERWORK AND WORRY DRIVE A HARD BARGAIN.</head>
        <p>OVERWORK AND WORRY DRIVE A HARD BARGAIN.</p>
        <p>It doesn't pay to defy nature's laws. We all need peace of mind, rest, outdoor exercise and eight hours' sleep to keep well. We must not overeat, nor drink much, unless it he milk or 'water. They are both good and should be freely used. •</p>
        <p>Anything bad leads to, trouble. Bad habits are no exception. Careless living and overeat- ing are among the worst—they help load the blood with Vric Acid poisons. The kidneys act as safety valves, filtering the blood and Passing off the poisons, but they ..are bound to weaken under any long continued strain, and once they falter, you feel dull, .tired and nervous. Your back aches, • you lose weight you have head- aches, d*zy- spells and urinary disorders. The first thing to do is to get back to simple, sensible'habits. Eat less, sleep more. and be careful what you irink. But you must also help the weakened kidneys. You can rely on Doan's Backache Kidney Pills for this help. They are a special medicine for the kidneys and bladder only. That is why. so many people recommend Dean's.</p>
        <p>All dealers, or 2/9 a box front Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells St., Oxford St., London, IV.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>e 4. – – r – –</head>
        <p>e 4. - - r - -</p>
        <p>letttrrs to or (Ehitor</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATE% V, PROFITS AND INCOME TAX.</p>
        <p>Jarman is usually rather careful ii. what he says or writes, but on this occasion he. has allowed his pen to run away with him to. the extent of inaccuracy. He says this matter of taxing Co-operative dividends "has gone so, far as a. decision of the House of Lords on the matter, which certainly should be final." Mr. Jarman know, or ought to know, that the Idecision he refers to was on an insurance ease. and -that Co-operative dividends were not men- tioned in that decision. He says each Co-operative member is liable, ' to income tax to the very same extent as every other person... That is so as regards his personal income, if he declares it, but in no way includes the Co-operative dividend. This, is simply beg- gin g the question.• Ile says - Our agitation is to tax Co-operative Societies "as if they were private organisations run for 'individual profit." What are Co- operative Societies run for if not for profit? Is that not one of the chief if not the chief argu- ment they use to seduce people into joining, and. as he himself puts it that "the Co-operative Societies are formed of individuals," and as they quarterly declare 'so much profit for .distribu- tion to the members, can any. sane man'say they are anything else but organisations run for profit to the individual members? He rings the changes on the profits being "of the nature of discount br saving on income." Taking it—.for.the. ,sake of argument—that the profits are discount or saving, to use his words. It is not discount or 'saving on income at all. but is gained. by interfering with and reducing the volume of taxpaying trade of the country. It displaces.. taxation and does 'net. replace it, thereby robbing (I do not use the word offen- sively) the revenue and increasing the burden on others. They did over 138 millions trade in 1914. That trade was only safely conducted by and through the protection of the -State and of our Navy and. Army. They declared over 15 millions of profit which, at the present rate of income tax should pay £2,660,717 per annum for this protection, but does not pay one penny of it.</p>
        <p>He says they are not behind others in doing their best to assist the country in ,the present crisis. What have they done compared with others? They even protest against excess profit tax. What next?</p>
        <p>• Re their circular, he says it was issued simply with the intention of assisting the Government. but does not 'explain how. The members are invited to increase their share' capital in the society. How, can this help the GSvernment ? Doubtless he wi,ll. say, "Oh, we have invested £2.000 in War Loan." But what is that? It is onitabout half-a-crown per member. The circular also refers to the security. Their balance sheet may show SA Ms. security for every of share capital, but the statement in the circular is not full enough to show. I believe if it were left to the ordinary mem- bers of Co-operative Societies the majority would vote for paying •income tax. I am afraid that it is the officials who are the stumbling block.</p>
        <p>Will Co-operators refuse the call to be loyal? I hope not.</p>
        <p>ROBERT WALKER, General Secretary, National Traders? Defence League. IS. Corn Exehange Buildings, Manebe..ter, 2nd February, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SCARLET FEVER.</head>
        <p>SCARLET FEVER.</p>
        <p>Eight Unsuspected Cases In</p>
        <p>Two Families.</p>
        <p>NIEDICAL 01-TICER'S IMPORT..</p>
        <p>warning to parents was uttered by Dr. Murray, the medical officer of the Warring- ton Rural District Council at the monthly meet- ing on Wednesday afternoon. In his monthly report he stated there had been eight cases of scarlet fever at Hollins Green, the patients being members of only two families, and added "as there was no medical man in attendance, I, along with the inspector, went and found the patients in different stages of scarlet fever. The parents were unaware of the nature of the disease, probably- because most of the cases had been of a mild character, both in respect to the illness and to the rash. We impressed upon them the seriousness of their position, and I understand they have since carried out our instructions."</p>
        <p>The meeting of the Council was presided over by Mr. W. Furness, and there were present: Messrs. 3. Ban, G. Mather, J. Backhouse, P. Longton and the Rev. A. M. Mitchell. The officials present were Messrs. J., C. Sutton (clerk), Dr. Murray (medical officer), B. S. Newcombe. (surveyor) and F. Kerfoot (in- spector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>RIXTON WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>RIXTON WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read • a letter from the Rixton Parish Council concerning the offer of the War- rington Corporation re the Rixton water supply. The Rixton Council were of opinion that the proposed Cost of the Corporation's scheme would entail a'.great hardship upon the ratepaiers, and suggested the whole matter should stand over for the period of the war, or until the rateable value Waeranted the outlay.</p>
