      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>VILLAGE WITH ONE LAMP.</head>
        <p>VILLAGE WITH ONE LAMP.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Sankey Parish Council wrote suggesting that the Warrington Corporation'.; schema of fixing automatic lighting clocks on to the gas lamps in the district should be left over on the grounds that the present lighting arrangements were restricted. • Later in the meeting the Surveyor (Mr. New- combe) reparted that WoOlston, Winwick and Penketb had written agreeing to accept the automatic lighting. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Mathji.: 'How many lamps have Woolston ?</p>
        <p>The Surveyor: One. (Laughter.) And they make it a eondition.roulton should be the same as themselves:</p>
        <p>Penketh agreed that the automatic nlock:4 shafild be fixed to all the lamps except the one at the tannery, which already had one. (Laugh- ter).•</p>
        <p>Mr. Matter: Send them. to the top of the eltv•;s. (More laughter.)</p>
        <p>COLLIERY EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Collins Green Colliery Co. have served the. Clerk with a notice to the effect that being the owners, lessees or occupiers of certain mines or minerals in the Council's district, namely, the Potatoe.Delph Seam, lying under the portion (I. Phipps-lane, between Back- lane and the junction of the Gorsey and, Clay. lanes in the township of Burtonwood and within .10 yards 'therefrom, and desirous of working the Same, it was their intention to do so 30 days after the serving of the notice.. The notice was dated Xanuary 25th, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RELIEF FUNDS.</head>
        <p>THE RELIEF FUNDS.</p>
        <p>-- • -- T 6 Chairman announced that £125 Os. 51. had been collected for the Belgian Relief Fund. Of this, various•sums had been paid away, and there was a balance in the bank. He suggested that £25 should be sent for the relief of the Belgians in Belgium. There was still a balance, in hand of V 239 16s. 9d., which had been col- lected for the National Relief Fund. He sug- gested tl3O Ms. 9d. should be sent to the Central Fund. His proposals were agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ACKNOWLEDGMENTS.</head>
        <p>ACKNOWLEDGMENTS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the following letter, in -reply. to the vote •of sympathy accorded the relatives of the late Captain Withington: "Please accept our 'sineere thanks for the sympathy shown to us by the members of the Rural District Council., also for your kind letter. It is a great comfort to us to feel how much our father was loved and respected.—Arthur Withington." The Clerk also read 'a letter from Sir Harcourt E. Clare, which stated :—" My Dear Sir,—Will you kindly convey to the Chairman and members of the Warrington Rural District Council my :sincere thanks for their individual congratulations conveyed in your letter of the 6th 'January, Apon the honour bektowed upon me by his Majesty, and may I also express MY thanks to you personally P—Yonrs Harcourt lE. Clare." •</p>
        <p>"Nothing hut margarine has entered my door slime the war began."—" Dr. C. W. Saleeby, " Daily Chronicle.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>,;;</p>
        <p>• For Oaks., Pastry, Puddings a elm BAKING POWDER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>burri eius.</head>
        <p>burri eius.</p>
        <p>AFTER THE WAR.</p>
        <p>T. Miller Wants " Natural Divisions " Of Territory.</p>
        <p>Declarations not far-remoVed from the prin- ciples of the Union of Democratic Control were -made by the Rev. Thomas Miller in the eourse of an address to Bold-street Wesley Guild on Monday. evening. First, however, the reverend gentleman paid a tribute to the lads who had gone to the Front, and reviewed the effects of the war •upon our Political, industrial and religious institutions. He said that pinny of those who formerly took an active part in their meetings, were now trans- lating their loyalty to Jesus ChriSt in other terms. We regretted their absence, but rejoiced in their consecration to their county's service and to the higher Causes of the kingdom of God. Referring to ,the difficulties in statesmanship that lay ahead of us, the speaker thought that politics would have to be net only a study, but a pursuit of Christian men. "A polling booth is as holy as a prayer meeting, and it is quite as sacred a place to me where we consider soeial conditions as is a committee where we con- sider questions 'relating to a spiritual revival." Some asked why we should look far ahead in- stead' of thinking only about pursuing the war to, an end. Certainly the end seemed more re- mote' than ever, but there were hopeful signs. There was no cause -for downheartedness or fear, but Tor thanks for the goodness of God. in the earlier books of the Bible ' he could find nothing more wonderful than the wonderful de- liverance by God which we had had in the last eighteen months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>When The War Is Over,</head>
        <p>When The War Is Over,</p>
        <p>We all believed that peace would come, .of course, and we hoped that, if not by love of Peace but hatred of war, men would love peace and pursue it: .The war must have a great influence upon aational life and national boundaries. From other ages we had received legacies which were not to our own advantage—national divisions made "according to political necessity and not according to the wishes of the peoples or by what was just. In future, nations must be .divided according to their instincts and racial features. When the war came to an end Alsace and Lorraine might vote as to Which country. they should be in, the dual empire of Austria- Hungary, and the different States might all fall asunder and become independent nationalities. The 'pledge of home rule to Poland should be -coriled out and, if it were not, it would be the (*My of _other nations to see that Russia ful- -filled her pledge. The division of Italy must not remain, as it had been Napoleonic—i.e., robbed nii•orne of its best coastline. If England did not get Heligoland—which seemed to have been given away in a spirit of generosity—he hoped it would be denationalised, and that the same would be done with the Kiel-Canal. What was wanted was that .every nation should be free from menace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Drink Throttled The State.</head>
        <p>Drink Throttled The State.</p>
        <p>Later, the reverend gentleman de(lared that the. State had throttled the churches and the -drink traffie had .throttled the State. Ile did not: believe in doing any injustice to anyone .eonnected with the 'Trade, but Christian men n d Women Must See to it that this menaee to mr national life .was overthrown.'</p>
        <p>Mr. Kenneth Bishop presided over a go,xl at- tendance, and Miss D. Cain sang "The Voice ot the Father. 7</p>
        <p>Mr. Miller, who was pastor of Bold-street -Wesleyan Church for five years, and is now stationed at Sheffield, also preached on. Sunday .morning and evening to good congregations. The anthem in. the morning was "My Soul. Truly -Waiteth," and in ,the evening " I Praise Thee, 0 Lord." from Mendelssohn's " St. Paul,", Mr. Pearce being the soloist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>CHURCH UNITY.</p>
        <p>Bev. J. E. Rattenbury Suggests British Empire As Model.</p>
        <p>A striking address on the need of unity in the Church was given by the Rev. J. E. Ratten- bury, the well-known London preacher, at the Padgate Wesleyan Church on Tuesday evening, when he lectured on "The Church and the Future." He contrasted the different kind of unity as typified 15y the German Empire and the British Empire, and declared that the .unity of the Church must be modelled on the lines of the British Empire. After referring to the changes due to the war. the lecturer said that whatever the Church of Christ meant, it must always remain in sonie sense the body of Jesus Christ.. A portion of the secular Press declared that the Church had failed. There was a sense in which he agreed with this, and a sense in which he did not. The Church had failed inasmuch as it did not pre- vent the war. In this respect the Church had failed .centurY after century, but it had never more truly represented the spirit of.,Jcsus Christ than it did at this moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>" Holy ." Catholic, Not " Roman " Cathciic.</head>
        <p>" Holy ." Catholic, Not " Roman " Cathciic.</p>
        <p>He believed the Church of the future was the Holy Catholic Church—by that he did not mean the Roman Catholic Church. One of the things which had hindered the Church in this age was its disunion. That 'there were all sorts of denominations in England was 'one .fact, but it was a small one. The most deplorable fact was that there' were so many national churches in existence which stood for nations, and the Christian -Churches of Europe, instead of speak- ing in one voice, spoke in various voices. One of the practical things the Church had to con- sider was the question of unity; for if they could get anything like unity, it would . count enormously against the differences and strife amongst the nations of Europe. A great num- ber of people .had hoped that international movements would prevent war; that if they could get international bonds among the people, war would be impossible. These bonds must, however, be on the lines of real Christian federa- tion. The Christian man was no true Christian unless he loved the kingdom of God better than he loved the kingdom in which be lived. Two Conceptions Of Unity.</p>
        <p>There were two conceptions of unity in the .world to-day—the German and the English con- ceptions. The German conception was prac- tically the same as that of the medieval Church, but the English was higher, and he be- lieved stronger. The German idea was built up on force—it-was a mechanical union; the British idea was a spiritual uni6n. German unity was an organisation, crushing the individuals who opposed it and converting 'lnman beings into machines. They had an idea of British unity in the fact that England, Canada, South Africa, Australia, India, and all the parts of the Empire were not bound together by mechanical bonds, but were hound together by spiritual bonds, which Germany could never break. (Applause.) In this war spiritual bonds had proved to be stronger than iron. The British conception of unity was a model for the Church Of Christ. .He did not believe in 11. mechanical unity—he did not believe it was either practical or wise. He was not suggesting unity on the lines of the Roman Catholic Church—so far as. he was concnmed be would like to see the German Empire and the Ronan Church fall together.- He -wanted to see a Lnity of the spirit of the Churches, in which each Church could follow its own tradition, Fut Would be hound together in common law and in common love. The sort of unity he wanted was a unity on the lines of the unity Of the Britist Empire—the Churches bound .together in :true. spiritual union, having respect for each other. At the conclusion of the lecture the speaker was accorded a vote of thanks, moved by Dr. Bennett and seconded by the Rev. A. J. Morton. Dr. Ferguson presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>OF ALL AGES.</head>
        <p>OF ALL AGES.</p>
        <p>Rev. 11. B. _Firth's Appeal For More</p>
        <p>Communicants.</p>
        <p>At what age should Christians be confirmed? The Rev. 11. B. Firth discusses the questiOn in the "St. Jame's' Parish Magazine" this month, and begins:— - " In the-first week of February, if all be well, we shall begin the very important work of giving in.struction to . candidates for confirma- tion. Great diversity of opinion exists as to the niost suitable age for the reception of this sacramental rite; our Church has not specified, as she has done, for instance, in regard to ordination, how old a person must be who is to be confirmed, and at different periods of history varying standards have been adopted. It is stated that the famous john Wesley, who was the son of a Lincolnshire clergyman, was a communicant when he was eight or nine years old, and as' confirmation-niust be .received before admission to communion he must have been confirmed when he was a mere child. There' is a great deal to be said in favour of an early age being taken as the standard; children are baptised and regenerated by the llolk,Ghost in their infancy, why should they not receive the strengthening grace of the Holy Ghost when they are still quite young? If any of our readers have boys or girls who Will be thirteen or over that age by Easter we urge them to send them to the classes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Adults, Too!</head>
        <p>Adults, Too!</p>
        <p>"But although we advocate an early age for confirmation it must not be supposed that it is the privilege of children only. So 'vitally im- portant is this means of grace that the rulers of the Church have appointed, for the duration of the 'war, a bishop who is with the troops on the Western Front and whose chief business is to confirm such of our soldiers who have not already been Confirmed and who now desire to 7eCeive this 'holy rite. There must be many actult Churchpeople in the parish who, for one reason or another, have not yet been confirmed. Let -.them summon up their, .courage and seize the opportunity while it is present."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CH TRCII COLLECTIONS DOWN.</head>
        <p>CH TRCII COLLECTIONS DOWN.</p>
        <p>The general offertory . at the Parish Church last Year was .C5O less than in 1914, and the Rector (the.-Rev. Canon Willis), at, a meeting of the Church Council held recently, said that an average of £lO a Sunday should easily be obtained from such a congregation as . that which Attended the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The. Vicar of St. James' .(Latchford) also. comments upon the collections at his church. In his parish magazine this month he says:— " One of the. things which the editor of. the magazine finds very distastefuLis to grumbWor find fault—at least in print—but might not the collections both at the Church and the Mission be very largely increased? It would be., gratify- ing if an 'improvement were speedily dis- cernible."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>.F. X. AJAN'S FAREXVELL AT</head>
        <p>.F. X. AJAN'S FAREXVELL AT</p>
        <p>AVYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Carter presided at the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday, and said he was pleased to have the opportunity of speaking to them be- fore his departure to Chatham on • Monday to join the Royal Field Artillery. Re had a hearty send-off from the piembers.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Evans 'gave the address upon "Has the Christian Church been a failure?"</p>
        <p>The Secretary welcomed Sergeant Wright, who was present from Maidstone. Mr. Wright has two brothers who have been wounded in Flanders. Both are recovering.</p>
        <p>A girl shouldn't marry a man till she knows hll about him—and then she wouldn't want to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>F • . ~,..„ ..: :,. " .. ... .:-...,‘„,,,,, ~..,,,, ...„ ...„,,„,,,,.. ..,.#.,. „:„..,... g ~;,,,, .. ':i;,! r', :),`,. ' 4., /. ,•• '''''': -., :-, •,,,,T- Ali . . • ,"</head>
        <p>F • . ~,..„ ..: :,. " .. ... .:-...,‘„,,,,, ~..,,,, ...„ ...„,,„,,,,.. ..,.#.,. „:„..,... g ~;,,,, .. ':i;,! r', :),`,. ' 4., /. ,•• '''''': -., :-, •,,,,T- Ali . . • ,"</p>
        <p>+++++++++++++++++++:.+++++++++++++++++++++++,,:•+++++++++++++++++++++ +++++++++++++++++.:•++++++.:•++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++ ++ ++ +• .:. ++ + +</p>
        <p>e, v k -0 '4., •`. • k 3 . t. 4 . • ,</p>
        <p>ber of the Legion, and thirteen women holding diplomas are now training men as cooks at the Army Schools of Cookery at Dartford. There are from thirty to forty men in a class, and the course last three weeks. When proficient, the men are sent to the Front. •</p>
        <p>Margarine. War economy has introduced margarine into many households that would formerly have been horrified at its presence. It is its unhappy name, suggestive of train oil, or cart grease, that prejudices people against it, and it is a pity a more euphonious appellation was not dis- covered for this substitute for butter, when it emerged from a crude unsavoury compound into a wholesome and nourishing article of diet. In many households where the best brands of margarine have taken the place of butter, the change has not been noticed by uninitiated mem- bers of the family. If people will take the trouble to beat together one part of butter and two parts of margarine, they, will go far to minimise the sentimental objection to the latter product, and obtain something cheaper. equally as good, and practically indistinguishable from the butter they have been used to.</p>
        <p>A White And Grey Year. Fashions in colours will this year be regulated by the dyers. instead of dictating her choice of. shades .as usual, fashion will be subservient t,.) economic conditions. The problem of dyi - stuffs is one that Britain. France and the United States will have to solve, if the war does not end speedily: In the absence of covyirs, might .be supposed that to make white gcc ds would be a Aimple • matter. Such, how,:ver: is not altogether the case, for very • coaiifierable difficulties have arisen with regard to bleach.- ing. Consequently, grey is likely to take ahe place Of colours and white. Not only will all coloured cotton goods be considerably dearer than last year, but no retailer will guarantee that they will wash, for colours are fading in strength. It is certain too that all white goods, including notepatier, will rise in price consider- ably as the year advances.</p>
        <p>A Material Set. Far has never in the history of clothes, been</p>
        <p>so universally worn as during this and recent winters. Many women regard good furs as investment for money, and are eager purchasers at the sales, where bargains are to be found. But great care and judgment is needed, as since the dyeing of substitutes has become a fine art, even experts can be deceived. Undoubtedly the best fur for wear, therefore ,the cheapest in the end, is the undyed skin, for the various pro- cesses to which the pelts are subjected by the dyer lessens their value, and, their resistance ,to wear. The smallest pieces of real fur should be treasured in these days, when ornaments for coats, buckles for shoes, and all kinds of trim- mings for hats can be made from them. But when a dress allowance does net admit of the purchase of even the cheaper furs, fortunately the most fascinating and becoming sets are to be had in imitation pelts, plush, velvet and silk. Odd lengths of fur come in most usefully for these sets, and if pieces are carefully cut they can be easily sown, and even patched if time and trouble can be expended on them. Edgings of fur add greatly to the appearance of a material or ribbon set.</p>
        <p>The Infinite Variety Of Sleeves.</p>
        <p>Sleeves at the present moment are receiving la very great deal of attention from tailors and dressmakers, for they are the most important part of a bodice, if not ,a coat. When selecting la shape—and never was 'there a wider choice— 'the kind of material to be used must be care- fully considered, because some of the numerous styles can be carried out only in soft, clinging fabrics, whilst others demand firmer and more Substantial Stuffs. The sleeveless bodice which has firmly established itself in popular favour admits of much variety with very little ex- pense, for a change of sleeves gives a new corsage. Not only may oddments of sdk-, lace. velvet, velveteen and fur be used for the c..n- fection of pretty sleeves, but •half-worn may be "done up" and worn. un.t.r a little coat, that is so easy to make at li)m!. Two ..r even three in different materials and 7tyl?i, re not .an extravagance; for they too can be male from oddments snapped up at the sales at blr- gain prices, or secured from the better r;arts of half-worn coats and other garments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Women In Banks.</p>
        <p>The girl or woman bank clerk has proved her capacity and has probably conic to stay. • More- over, women have mastered in -a month or two the work which the youth took a year or more to learn, although slowness is one of the draw- backs alleged against the employment of women in banks:. The work has attracted a good type of. girl, fairly well educated and intelligent, , who, but for the war, would be engaged for the most part, in the pursuit of pleasure and amusement. All are. said to be very keen on their work. In Finland, in America, and else- where, women have been for some time employed as cashiers, but it is doubtful if they will be given these posts in our banks, and there are several departments from which they will be excluded. The monotony of the, work of a bank clerk is said to act as a deterrent in the case of many girls capable of undertaking the ' duties and of carrying them out successfully. But the work is no more trying in that respect Ithan is household routine and drudgery, supposed until recent times to be woman's only legitimate sphere. Women Army Cooks. The Women's Legion has proved that trained women make more economical Army caterers and better cooks than untrained men. The wastefnlness in camp kitchens became so notorious that the authorities sanctioned the appointment of one hundred women cooks under Lady Londonderry's scheme. The success that attended the initial experiment led to many other appointments, notably at Epsom and East- borne, and at the hospital and convalescent camp at Alnwick and Blackpool. Not only have the women effected a saving of thousands of pounds, but they have provided better food, with (Treater variety in, the menus. Another brand': of work which promises to be equally useful has been recently undertaken- by mem-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>The Hair.</p>
        <p>What is good to wash the hair with when the scalp is affected with eczema? The treatment consists in daily washing of the head, or other parts affected, with warm water, 1 pt.; glycerine, loz. This should be thoroughly rubbed over the skin; the dilute eitrine ointment, sold by all chemists, may be used at night. Another remedy is water, 1 pt.; borax, loz.; to be used twice daily. You should also take a good tonic for the blood and to build up the system. Red clover tea drank freely is. a splendid blood purifier. - Laxative Food.</p>
        <p>From this point of view the best food is Scotch or Irish oatmeal, the coarser grain. Oatmeal may be the best or the worst food in the world, according to the way it is cooked. It ought to be well boiled, and for family use it is best cooked overnight and left on the stove all night. It should be firm, not sloppy, as is the common way. It comes in best in the late autumn, winter, and spring; during summer its use might be left off, and vegetables and fruit take its place. It is best as a breakfast food, and some prefer it alone—that is to say, without the addition of other foods, excepting, perhaps, a dry biscuit. The true oatcake, which is not the sugary stuff that is sold in shops, is one of the best forms of bread, and it is best made of the finer oatmeal, good samples of which can be had in Scotland. Failing good, sound, fresh oatmeal, one can fall back on rolled oats, which have the advantage of being pre-cooked. It is easy to spoil the effect of the best oatmeal food by covering it with cream and sugar, as some do.</p>
        <p>.:. ...:. Child I.3ren 8 L.;ol'ller. 4C. (,) 4* Conducted by UNCLE JACK. :4*</p>
        <p>re,Join This Corner! Enter hTnhe Competitions! CWriiltde Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington.</p>
        <p>MY CONGRATULATIONS.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>Yon will be as delighted as I have hen this week at the result of our effort to enlarge our circle of Children's Corner Cousins. I said I should-like to increase the r6ll to 700 by the summer, and, strange to say, exactly that number has been reached at .one bound—thanks to several very active little nephews and nieces.' . ,There appears to have been a .very friendly race between Niece Lilian Himsworth and Niece Eveline 'Cotton for the prize for the one sending in the Most. names of new* members. At one time Eveline had won, but Lilian sent in later lists, and totalled .57 new. members! Eveline 'sent in just 50 names. I think it only fair, in the circumstances, that each should have a prite, and I shall have pleasure in sendingjhera a first prize each! .I think I sho:uld have Uncle Ned's approval for this, because I. see he set me the example when he had a similar competition a few months ago, and two competitors did very well. The letters I have received speak for them- selves, and I reproduce them below. 1 should like you to send in particulars as to address, age; &amp;-`,G., where they have been forgotten.. You an easily see by the list. . I welcome all my new nephews and nieces, and hope they will join: their other cousins in I making the list of members -still bigger. Shall we try to get 1,000 members before the summery</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S COMPETITION. • Next week I intend to give a prize for the best essay sent in on The Book I Like Best, And Why. , Your loving UNCLE JACK,</p>
        <p>It should be simply clipped in milk, and, best of all for this use, the buttermilk of the north country, Scotland and Ireland. Bread is much overrated as a food. Baker's bread is unwholesome, and provocative of dys- pepsia. _ln these days some improvement has _ _ _</p>
        <p>been made by the introduction oi malted forms of bread, but we shall never get a wholesome bread so long as yeast is used in making it. All white, fine-floured breads should be avoided; rye- bread is better than white, as it contains more cellulose than common bread.</p>
      </div>
