      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TEA MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>TEA MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>FOR PAROCHIAL FUNDS.</p>
        <p>Successful Tea And Entertainment At</p>
        <p>St. Ann's.</p>
        <p>, The annual tea and concert, held in order to swell the funds of the various parochial organisations connected with .St. Ann's Church,' took place on Monday evening in the School- room, Dannett-street. A fair number of parishioners and friends sat down to partake of a good repast, prepared by several lady members of the church. Tea over, the company adjourned to another room, where a first-class programme arranged by Mr.W..H. Rigby, was entered upon. It opened by the rendering of a chorus entitled "There's a land," by the day school choir, all the mem- bers of which- had been trained by Mr. Rigby. The efforts of the children gained unstinted applause, as also in three other choruses, "A little bit of Ifeai-on," "Shine, shine moon" ani "Gay Gordons." Madame Lily Fairhurst de- lighted all with her songs. "When you corn? home" and "An Englishman's home," the, eilil- dren joining in the chorus of the latter song in a whole-hearted manner. Miss Elsie Hewitt</p>
        <p>was heard to advantage in the rendering of two songs "Call of May-time" and "Valley of laughter." With • Mr. W. H. Rigby she also partook in a duet entitled "Excelsior." Mr. Rigby's rendering of "A fat little fellow v, th his many eyes" was greatly enjoyed, while Mr. W. Harrison fairly scored with his humoro selections. John Smith, a scholar, gave a humorous recitation entitled "Water scenes," nd Annie Lawless and Alec Evans, also scholars, respectively gave " S.unshine of your smile " and "Under the bamboo tree."</p>
        <p>The accompanists during the evening were Mr. W. 11. Rigby and Mr. B. Randall.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were accorded on the proposi- ion of Rev. J. Davies (vicar).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SCANTILY ATTENDED.</head>
        <p>SCANTILY ATTENDED.</p>
        <p>Rev. If. B. Firth Deplores Indifference</p>
        <p>To thirches.</p>
        <p>The St. James' Church annual congregational tea was held in the School on Monday evening. Nearly 200 persons sat down to an excellent tea. A concert was afterwards given by Madame Sara Silcock (soprano), Madame B. Eckersley (contralto), Mr. J. Sephton (tenor), Mr. T. Crowe (alto), Mr. W. H. Dutton (baritone), Mr. J. Hopkins (humorist), and Mr. A. Trow (pianist and entertainer). The Vicar (the Rev. H. B. Firth) said it was sad that the call to a more religious frame of mind had not been responded to by the nation as a whole. The churches, instead of being 'scantily attended, ought to be overcrowded with devout worshippers. The Vicar then stated how thankful he was personally for his recovery from his recent illness, and spoke of the great kindness and sympathy he had received from a multitude of friends. Referring to the work of the past year, he spoke of the completion of St. Hilda's Mis- Sion Church, upon which there rested not a half- penny of debt. Electric light had recently been installed in St. James' Church with excellent results, and the parishioners now had a church most admirably lighted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>BOLD-STREET WESLE VANS. Sunday School Roll Of Honour Unveiled.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLE VANS. Sunday School Roll Of Honour Unveiled.</p>
        <p>The scholars of the Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School held their annual tea party and entertainment in the Friars' Green Schoolroom on Thursday evening. After a sustantial repast, at which about 150 scholars sat down, an enter-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>CHLORODYNE</head>
        <p>CHLORODYNE</p>
        <p>THE RELIABLE FAMILY MEDICINE.</p>
      </div>
