        <p>THE BEST REMEDY KNOWN</p>
        <p>FOR COUGHS, GOLDS COLIC, AGUE, and kindred ailments.</p>
        <p>NO BETTER PRESENT to make a friend on netive service at home or abroad than a bottle of this famous Medicine. Of all Chemists: 1/3, 3/-, 5/-. ALWAYS ASK FOR A 'don. COLLIS BROWNE."</p>
        <p>I • , „ * , •'' ' • „ 'GIVES STRENGTH TO. WI N. , - • - '• • ' ~• . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>tainment was given, in which many youthful artistes rendered pleasing songs. The prizes for regular attendancp were presented by Mrs. Percy, meson, who bestowed a word of praise to each recipient. A feature of the evening was the unveiling of a Roll of Honour by Mr. Silcock on behalf of those who had joined the Colours. It occupied a conspicuous position abgre the platform, on •side of which was a Unlon Jack. The Roll of Honour was designed by Mr. Ernest Light, and -k a work of art. 'lmmediately after the unveiling the scholars marched past, each 'saluting when in front of the framed tribute to the accompanying strains of the Russian and French anthems, and "rule Britannia" sung lustily by the ahildken. The Sunday school report was in the form of a sketch, and was splendidly portrayed by about 30 of the scholars. After the junior portion of the programme had been gone through, an entertainment which was much appreciated was submitted to the elder scholars and friends of the Church. The Chairman was the Rev. P. meson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BIG CASUALTY LIST.</head>
        <p>BIG CASUALTY LIST.</p>
        <p>70,000 Missing From Churches And Chapels.</p>
        <p>An interesting topical touch is found in the Rev. Percy meson's "News Letter," issued from the Bold-street Wesleyan Church In appealing for larger congregations he Trst sets forth these figures People in Warrington and immediate district Sunday night congregations in all the Churches—a generous estimate 10,000</p>
        <p>Missing</p>
        <p>70,006</p>
        <p>Grant exemption certificates to The sick. The aged. Those who care for the sick and aged: Children, Those who are bound to work on Sunday. Still there are tens of thousands missing! And the Sunday morning casualty list is more alarming still. Where ate the missing?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>STUCK TO MS GUN IN SPITE</head>
        <p>STUCK TO MS GUN IN SPITE</p>
        <p>OF POISON GAS.</p>
        <p>. . In the agony .and confusion of that crisis, Lynn realised that behind that cloud of gas the enemy were advancing, and that the trench was as good as lost, lie made up his mind that the trench should not be lost. There was no time to fix his respirator, though his eyes and lungs were full of the poisonous fumes, and his efforts to breathe brought the blood to his mouth: One by one his comrades succumbed and dropped, and soon he was alone. The advancing Germans were near now, confident that their new weapon had delivered their enemies into their hands. They expected a trench empty except for corpses. They were mistaken. The very might and majesty of Britain stood waiting for them in the person of a simple private soldier. With a bound Lynn was on the parapet and had trained his machine-gun straight ahead through the gas. The Germans could not see him, and they fell in heaps until the remnant lost heart and re- tired defeated. No German soldier set foot in that trench, whiith the valour of one man had saved.. . .—Extract from "How Private Lynn won the V.C.," in "Deeds That Thrill" (Hutchinson and Co.).</p>
        <p>A Little Knock.</p>
        <p>"Some people can make even the most com- monplace subject interesting," said the loqua- cious youth.</p>
        <p>"Yes," answered Miss Cayenne. "1)o tell me, something of yourself."</p>
        <p>696 Bessie Elwell. "I am expecting some more new members next week.</p>
        <p>"Your loving. niee(?. "JANE DOWLING."</p>
        <p>MARY ROCHE, 6, Bostock-street. . 697 Ethel Oultram. 698 Annie Roche, 6, Bostock--street. 700 NEPHEWS AND NIECES!</p>
        <p>Those who have taken part in the competition brought my family of nephews and nieces up to 698. Two -little ladies who have Sent in their own names, have the credit of making the list in my big book just 700 strong. The letters they sent were nicely written, and said— "36, Parker-street, Warrington. "Dear Uncle Jack,—One of my little friends, Lily Webb, of Catherine-street, persuaded me to become One of your nieces. I think it will be very nice, and I will write as often as I can. I will be very pleased to know my number. "lour loving niece. " MAISIE HANNAN.' "18, White-street, Warrington. "Dear Uncle Jack.—l am writing to tell you that I would like to become one of your nieces. A friend. of mine, Lily Webb, asked me to, join, so if you please, Uncle Jack, I should like ,to know .my number: , • "Your loving riper,. " BLODWIN JONES." -</p>
        <p>I ana'..inuch obliged to ,Niece Lily Webb, and 'have, special pleasure .in enrolling her friends .and giving them their numbers:- 699 Biodwin Joces, 18, White-street. MO Maisie Hannan, 36, Parker-street.</p>
        <p>AVM</p>
        <p>?aft.</p>
        <p>Safeguard your Health with Dfir J. COLLI3 BROWNE'S</p>
      </div>
