      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>MRS. HAMBLETT and her SON ARTIII,TR. of 17, Egerton-street. d.s RA .Are t•-• P th- many friends who have sent floral tributes and expressions of sympathy during their recent sad bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>" ZEPPS."</head>
        <p>THE " ZEPPS."</p>
        <p>. This week the Germans have honoured Eng- land with the biggewt Zeppelin raid on earth. Two, versions hold the field as to the reason for this Barnum-like show. On the one hand the Germans say they are carrying the war into the principal enemy's camp, and they , claim to have done such unkindly acts as to have bombarded Liverpool docks and to have laid our industries—Our principal reserve of strength—in ruins. Of course, that, theory .is for home consumption and the 'bewilderment ,of neutral countries. When we hear of what the Germans are doing elsewhere we can judge of the truth of their statements by the truth of their statements regarding events of which we have first-hand knowledge.. When a great -murdering machine, is let loose to roll upon . and Overwhelm Paris in' six weeks, and Paris remains unscathed! it - the-N-etidt Of eighteen' months, something .ha,s to be done and said to keep inquiring minds at home and abroad -occupied. And -so they are being occupied by these pyrotechnic displays which have painful results, but in the end will harm Germany more than us. The other theory—our theory —is that these outrages against all laws, human and .divine, are the outward signs :of the inward state-of despair4hich has taken poSseasionf of , the Teuton mind Pinned On hind and..SWept off the faec ef the Seas, the Gwraan..seeks-freedom—where ? -. In. Serbia .and Turkey and in the air. He will be pinned in Serbia and Turkey in: due course, and as we have beaten him on land and sea, eVen to the 'extent of exterminating his submarines 'in . home waters, So we shall 'beat him in the air. There are those who advocate. repriailit-in' 1 .. ! kind i..that we should,' like. the . Germans, take 1 tti bombing innocent and helpless people from the clonds.. Lord Bosibery is 'among those _... . • . who - would' like' to "mete measure for ' measure." That is the only argument Prus- sians understand, and we are sufficiently un- Christiafilike in the presence of such atrocious opponents to agree heartily with his Lordship on one -condition. That is, would it pay? Emierts ,tell.us:that the energy' and expense of :a _Zeppelin raid are -out-of ~all -proportion to the military ends achieved, and that we can to better advantage engage our brains and our explosives at the Front killing German men. It counts in war more than killing bra bjes., If that is so, "we. must .not 'miss our bone by # grasping at a shadow, especially as we are not striving -after showmen's effect liiie the Ger- mans.. As we have said, we shall beat the Germans agove the earth, and doubtless we shall be able to check them there.as.effectually as we have checked them under the waters. In_any,case, the Zeppelin will not terrify us. Up to . now,: it has . been. Lord 'Kitchener's best recruiting agent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WHY THESE " SCENES:?</head>
        <p>WHY THESE " SCENES:?</p>
        <p>Dean Swift said that for want of a greater obstruction a man would stumble over a straw. For want of something to fall out about on Tuesday night the Warrington Town Coun- cil, on the initiative of Councillors Gough and It; jiadalpd-over "Wm;arent noth-.</p>
        <p>7firsodianrat</p>
        <p>Again and again I have told Alderman Bennett that he is the most illegible writer in town.</p>
        <p>To a Roman Catholic functipn on Tuesday he sent a lettcr. Father Hind read the type- written message fluently, but on arriving at the signature, stopped suddenly.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, who was sitting near by, came to the rescue by deciphering it for him. It was fortunate he had seen the Alderman's caligraphic contraption before.</p>
        <p>Business at the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday did not require the Court to be opened. Thee was nothing doing.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately, however, the Juvenile Court had to be opened, thereby spoiling the record, and incidentally depriving the Chairman of the Bench of a pair of white gloves.</p>
        <p>Congratulations to the Town Clerk and his department for the excellent compilation of facts they have made in the Year Book which has just been issued.</p>
        <p>It contains most useful information regard- ing the -.Corporation and public life in, the borough generally. I may add that the book itself has been printed at " The Examiner " Office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
