        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 5, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 5, 1916.</p>
        <p>ing in one of those " scenes " for which the Council have become famous, and troth .which they apparently cannot be weaned even by the claims of a country engaged in" tt life-and- death struggle. It was difficult to see what the bother was about, but the Councillors named seemed to be aggrieved on two grounds —that they had not been given a list of con- tractors when a certain tender was before the Gas Committee, and that they had: been denied the right of free speech. The two last men in the world to complain of lack of freedom of 'speech are surely Councillors Gough and Broadhurst. Councillor Gough simply refuses to be silenced at any time by anyone; and we all know Councillor Broad- hurst. It was stated that the Chairman of the Committee, Alderman John Evans, said that unless the Chair was respected he would vacate it. He was quite right in doing so— and it is altogether a different matter from refusing a colleague a hearing and curtailing his liberty of speech. The two Councillors appear to have asked that,all members of the committee should, have a list of the firms tenderink; supplied to them. That was a proper request. A, decision as .to the tenders was postponed, and the responsible official was instructed to supply all members who desired one with a copy of 'the list of contractors and their prices. This was done. Then, we say, why the bother? Is it 'because it is impos- sible to conduct the affairs of Warrington without a wrangle, or, as Alderman Tinnion suggested. 'without throwing -about insinua- tions and innuendoes recklessly and indis- criminately? We might, at all events, have a truce for the period of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>"The Rambler" in Thursday's " Daily Mirror " wrote: "How A. J. B. Keeps Fit.— ] hear that Mr. Balfour is keeping fit by occa- sional games of lawn tennis on Lady Cros- field's hard courts. A.J.B.' is very fond of the game, of which, considering his age, he is a good exponent. It seems a long time now s;nce he was playing on the Riviera with poor Anthony</p>
        <p>Mr. George Edgar haS just published an- other novel, " Kent, the Fighting Man," this time. A review of the boo.. will appear in 'The Examiner." In the meantime I give this notice that a rich treat by a local writer may now be purchased at the booksellers.</p>
        <p>Councillor Wilson is anxious for a painting renaissance in Warrington.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council be suggested that a white band should be rainted round the middle of each lamp post so that we nocturnal travellers will not collide with them when the lights are out.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell is always having to assert his principles. At the Brabazon sale of work on Thursday a charming lady tried hard to persuade tam to buy a twopenny ticket for a cake about to be drawn for, and would not be refused until the reverend gen- tleman told her he did not believe in raffles.</p>
        <p>Then she saw it Was of no use, and moved off to someone who had no objection—a soldier.</p>
        <p>I see that Piper Laidlaw, whom we feted a few weeks ago, is again in the picture. He was presented with the V.C. by the King at Buckingham Palace on Thursday, and had his photograph in most of the newspapers yester- day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>VIGILANT.</head>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
        <p>MASON IC INSTALLATION.</p>
        <p>New Master Of The "Lodge Of. Lights."</p>
        <p>There was an unusually crowded attondance at the Masonic Hall, Warrington, on Monday night, when Bro. Thomas Irlam Fletcher, of Hill Cliffs, was installed Worshipful Master for the ensuing year of the "Lodge of Lights," No. 148, a lodge which recently celebrated its 'lsoth anniversary. Worshipful Bro. C. J. Smith, 1.P.M., was the installing master. •</p>
        <p>Fletcher invested his officers as follows :-- • .</p>
        <p>Bros: R. T. Harding, S.W.; Peter Peacock, J.W.; John Muskett, S.D.; Joseph Banks, J.D. ; Frederick Pearson, P.M., secretary ; James Hall- wood, P.M., treasurer; W. B. Cook, P.M., organ- ist; Harry Haviour, LG.; G. S. Rice, tyler; James T. Locker, 5.8,; Thos. Locker, 3.8.; James Armstrong, A.S.; John Swinton, S.; John Sampson, A.S. _ _ _</p>
        <p>.A.mong those present were W.Bro. the Rev. T. T. 'Lee-Jones, P.M., ,P.Pr.G.C. (chaplain); W.Bro. H. V. Pigot, P.M, P.Pr. W.Bros. R. H. Pearson, P.M., P.Pr. G. 0.; i'rederick Howard;;,., P.M., P.Pr.A.G D.C., DC.. c.v.; Robert Shacklady, P.M., 249, P.N.G:D.C., A.D.C.; Hugh F. Greenough; P.M., P.Pr.G.S.B, ,W.Bro. Jas. Fletcher, P.Pr.G.D.C., of Black- pool, brother of the ,W.M.-elect, was also present. All the Worshipful Masters 15i-010,otherlodices in town, with the exception of W.Bre. the Rev, W. Bracecamp ("Charity"), who is on active service in France, attended. The 'lodge was represented by W.Bro. Tickle, .Mr. Itracecamp's deputy,: the other lodges being, represented .by W.Bros. H: Maddock ("Gilbert Greenall "), T. S. Steel (" St. "), J. Moore 'Murray (" Friendship ") and H. Woods (" Rectitude "). The banquet was dispensed Itith ;owing to the war„•_but the loyal and Id[nic toasts were duly honoured. A select musical programme was supported by Bros. J. H. Draper, J. L. Daven- port, and .R. T. Harding, Bro. W,. B. Cook act- ing as accompanist. • W.Bro. W. B. Lambert gave a recitation from "Julius Coesar."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</head>
        <p>DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>Has The Local Order Been Approved?</p>
        <p>MEETING OF CONTROL BOARD</p>
        <p>YESTERDAY.</p>
        <p>The Liquor Control Board met in London yesterday afternoon, when the proposed restric- tions for Lancashire, Cheshire and Flint were discussed in view of the facts previously placed before the members by deputations from local organisations. No statement was made to the Press, but we have reason to believe, says the "Manchester Evening News," that the Order was approved, and that it will come into force in ten days' time.</p>
        <p>Councillor -Wilkinson was indignant that the time of • the Council should .be wasted in suck a. way as it had been wasted that 'night. It who arranged to adjourn the committee and supply a list to all who wanted one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>"KEPT IN THE DARK."</head>
        <p>"KEPT IN THE DARK."</p>
        <p>PIPIOF RAIL</p>
        <p>14\ -,fr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WORKMEN'S REVEILLE.</head>
        <p>WORKMEN'S REVEILLE.</p>
        <p>Councillors Gough And Broad. Sensitive Councillors Object To hurst Make A Complaint. I Works' "Buzzers."</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN'S THREAT TO LEAVE CHAIR. -</p>
        <p>When Councillors Gough and Broadhurst com- plained of being kept in the dark at the Town Council on ,Tuesday, they were not discoursing on Zeppelins or matters warlike. They were merely arguing about the follow- ing minute of the Gas Committee :—" Tenders for retorts and setting, fireclay , goods, feeding frames; and truck tippers were opened, initialled, and referred to the general manager to tabulate."</p>
        <p>The minute looked unimaginative enough, but these gentlemen 'started a discussion which de- veloped..into a.. wrangle, .and, as .the Mayor (Alderman Peter. Peacock) remarked at another meeting the same evening, the Chamber became "a veritable. bear . garden." • As soon as Councillor Gough , rose to move that the. minute be -referred -back, the Mayor intervened with the 'suggestion that .he would be well,advised to •defer :the matter until the_ next meeting of the committee, but the Coun- cillor heeded him not, as he desired to press the matter whilst his memory was fresh. He contended that at the committee meeting some- thing occurred which 'was an interference with the principles of free speech. At the meeting there was only one tabulated list of tenders, and that was practically monopolised by the Chairman or Deputy-Chairman, until he called attention to it. "If there is one man in this Council whom I have had to contend with more than another it is Alderman John 'Evans, the chairman of that committee," he declared em- phatically. The Alderman looked round at his accuser, blushed, and shook with glee. . "I have the hardest work to gain particulars concerning the Gas and Water Committees," Pursued the Councillor. "He vetoed us in com- mittee. Our place is here, if we cannot get a hearing in committee, in order that it may be brought to the notice of the public." "Trying to Conceal Things."</p>
        <p>Councillor Cunnington broke in warmly: You had a fair hearing in committee. Never mind bringing in Alderman 'John Evans., Councillor Gough: If there is one danger we have to fight against in the interests of the :Corporation it is ,thISL-trying to keep us in the dark and to conceal things when it is our endeavour to bring them out. I don't believe in closing my eyes; and opening my mouth, and receiving just what they like to put into it. What we want, and what we intend to agitate for, is freedom of action.'</p>
        <p>COuncillor Broadhurst seconded, and after various altercations with the Mayor, said that whether Councillor Gough and himself were defeated or not did not matter one iota. They would come forward hundreds of times to fight for the principle. Was it right and just that when 16 tenders were received they should not have a tabulated list before them in order that they might vote conscientiously? councillor Wilkinson: You had one.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst: One list only appeared at the Gas Committee, and that is in the Gas Engineer's possession, not in ours. Every mem- ber of the committee should have the whole of the names of the people who are tendering. On Monday night Councillor Gough wished to make a statement.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: Discuss that at the next meeting. Councillor Broadhurst: Thank you, Mr. Mayor; you are in the chair, and I wish to pay you every. respect. I will discuss this one month to-night, that is all. If you place . a restriction on me, I can assure. you that now we have commenced we will go through with it. Thatis no threat., Sixteen .naines.and only .one given! That is why I ant going to .support Councillor, Gough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Attack Upon a Worthy Alderman."</head>
        <p>Attack Upon a Worthy Alderman."</p>
        <p>, Alderman Tinnion regretted that the matter had beers raised in such a way as to be an 'attack upon the worthy Alderman who presided at the meeting of the committee on Monday. The matter was properly and thoroughly brought forward, and the Chairman gave ample opportunity for discussion. Councillor Broadhurst (to the Mayor) : It is distinctly unfair that you should allow Alder- man Tinnion to deal with yesterday's meeting after debarring me from doing so. Alderman Tinnion went on, however, and said the tabulated sheet was not kept' in the posses- sion of any particular individual, but was passed round. It was, he submitted, quite ample for the purposes of any explanation that any reason- able man might require. Alderman John Evans gave ample .opportunity for discussion, but Councillors. Gough and Broadhurst wanted to reply to every speech that was made.-- Councillor Broadhurst: I must protest against this. .I am surprised, Mr. Mayor, that. you .will not apply the closure to him as You applied it to me. -</p>
        <p>The Mayor: Councillor Gough moved this amendment. I won't say he did it deliberately to deceive me, but he never mentioned a word about last night's meeting. I thought he was referring to the meeting in. the minute. Xou brought it out first. I should have ruled him out of order at once if I had known.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst: Why don't you allow me to.put our case? The Mayor: You have Put it very well, I think.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst: I have not, because 'I would have told you this: that Alderman John .Evans distinctly 'said that if Councillor Gough spoke he would vacate the chair. Councillor Gough had to finish speaking after that threat..</p>
        <p>Alderman John -I did not over to vacate the chair on account of Councillor Gough's speech. It was the display of temper by Councillor Broadhurst - Councillor Broadhurst: The display of temper yesterday was equal to what has taken place to-night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>" Insinuations and Innuendoes."</head>
        <p>" Insinuations and Innuendoes."</p>
        <p>Later. Alderman Tinnion said that what he objected to was the insinuation which always came from certain quarters. They never dared to tell the bare and honest truth. It would be somewhat strange for tlaem to dose: ' They liked to :back, up by insinuations and innuendoes. He, gad only spolien to 'try to remove the im. pression they have tried to get about that there is something underhand and unfair being car- ried.,on.. •</p>
        <p>Councillor Lindley said the list was there for everybody to see. As to that night's opposition, he had never heard of a more nonsensical thing taking place in a business meeting. Only four voted for the amendment, which was defeated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>YEAR'S POLICE WORK.</head>
        <p>YEAR'S POLICE WORK.</p>
        <p>Fewer Adult Offenders; More Juvenile Offenders.</p>
        <p>"The Chief Constable (Mr. :Martin 'Nicholls), in his ninth annual report to the Watch Com- mittee, says that list year 144 -criraeti were re- ported the borough, or 83 fewer than in the previous year. For these offences 125 persons were proceeded against, ,a decrease of 40 on the figures for 1914. Sixty-six juvenile offenders (10 'years: of age' and under), charged with theft, &amp;c.. were dealt with during the year, an increase of 20 as com- pared with 1914. The number of persons proceeded against for non-indictable offences, exclusive of absentees, was 1,157, of which number 800 were aPPre-i heeded and 357 summoned, a decrease of 170 in the number of arrests and 154 in the number of summonses compared with the previous year. Fewer Charges of Drunkehtioss.</p>
        <p>Five hundred and eighty-six persons were prosecuted for drunkenness, 29 tess than in 1914.</p>
        <p>The authorised strength of the police force is 90. ThiTrty-one members are at present serving in His Majesty's Forces. The annual inspection of police took place on February 24th, 1915, and the Certificate of Effi- ciency has since been received. The conduct of the members of the force has bets.a. satisfactory duriag the year. •</p>
        <p>:Alderman Tinnion declared that there was a good deal to be said in favour of the amend- ment. It was "nice to get up in the morning, but it was nicer to lie in bed." (Laughter.) They had in Warrington more means of calling the people to work than any other town in the kingdom. At Bank Quay there was a, squeaky little tin pot of a thing—(laughter)--- which went off at three minutes to twelve, three minutes to five, three minutes to six, and, what was essential, at five o'clock. It was a resounding noise which certainly kept people awake. Manufacturers might have reasons for the use of these steam whistles, hut there were too many of them, and it was a fact that several works blew the whistle within a few seconds of each other. In the morning he heard whistles from Widnes, then from Sankey Bridges, from Monks, Hall and Co., from Crosfield's and several other works, and he thought the Council should exercise a little more control than they did. He believed the regulation was—at any rate it was understood as far as this particular hooter was concerned —that the whistle should be for eight seconds only. Alderman Smethurst had said the whistle goes on for five minutes, but if he heard it for that length of time it would be nerve-melting. Eight seconds was ample. The greatest-'nuisance was near• his, (the speaker's) house, which could be removed to a great ex- tent, namely, the noise of the trams' going 'round the corner. The trams went squeak and made such a noise that you never knew whe- ther they were on the track or going through the house. (Laughter.) The resolution ought to be referred back so that the whole question of the steam whistles could be arranged and net have one blowing for eight seconds and another from half a minute to a minute.</p>
        <p>"IN THE !NI ttitbiz ut- IHE PUBLIC." Councillor Dr. Joseph (Deputy. Mayor said the matter was brought before the Health Committee, but he did not know whether it really was a matter of health. He•quite agreed that an effort should be made to reduce the whistling to a minimum, as it must be very confusing to the workmen to hear so many different sounds from the hooters at different times. He could get to sleep through any number of "buzzers," but in the interests of the public generally it was right that the ex- isting arrangements should be 'revised.</p>
        <p>Alderman -James Evans said there was a resolution of the Council which limited the time the hooters • should be blown, and from time to time complaints had been made at this being exceeded. But if there was to be a striot limit of eight or ten seconds men would have to be engaged to do the timing where they set a watch. The Council could not inter- fere where the whistle was only blown for a few seconds; but they could when it went on for five minutes.</p>
        <p>Only eix voted that the minute be referred back, and the amendment was declared lost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>"MOST ANNOYING."</head>
        <p>"MOST ANNOYING."</p>
        <p>Set to music, the opprobrium levelled against works' hooters by certain members at the Town Council meeting, on Tuesday, .would make an excellent- "'Hymn of Hate." :The' hooter. had its- champions, but it .was generally admitted that we can have too much of a good thing. Councillor Lindley raised the discussion by calling attention to the Health Committee's minute. "that sanction be given to the War- rington Wire Rope Works, Ltd., to blow a steam whistle for the purpose of summoning or dismissing employes, provided that such a whistle • shall not be blown for a longer period than ten seconds at one time." In moving that the minute be referred back, rhe described the whistle as being a cross between a .fog horn and a .ship's siren. It was a great nuisance and - was blown „seven or eight limes .a day— perhaps more. Counoillor Lindley lives within speaking dis- tance of the. works. So does Alderman Sme=: ,thurst,. who 'seconded. He mentioned that :he had communicated with the Town Clerk, who had done his best to minimise the nuisance. As the Council were aware, there was a good deal of sickness in the town, and to all the sufferers, as well as to his wife, who was seri- ously ill, the noises created by the hooters were most annoying. Almost every works had its hooters, and he thought these should be re- stricted. In other towns certain works had been selected, and these only were allowed to use whistles. Eight seconds was the time al-, lowed for ,the summoning of workmen, but in some cases the whistle sounded for nearly five minutes.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman Peacock): It SOUNDS like five minutes! Alderman Smethurst: It is five minutes sometimes. I think we ought to take the matter into serious consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>COUNCILLOR GOUGH CRITICAL.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR GOUGH CRITICAL.</p>
        <p>In supporting the minutes, Councillor Gough was very critical regarding the previous speakers. "It shows you how sensitive they are about these things when they are next door," he said. "but neither of them considers that firms put these whistles up at great ex- pense and only for the purpose of obliging the workmen so that they can get to work. Yet Councillor Lindley and Alderman Smethurst say they should not do it betause it is so near their houses. It is like saying 'You shan't play in our backyard. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BELLS EFFETE.</head>
        <p>BELLS EFFETE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst supported the com- mittee, as he believed in 'doing justice to all firms. The whistle blew for a purpose,,namelY, for the benefit of employers and employed. It was nice for certain people to be able to lie'in bed, and when they heard the whistle, to turn over again and go to sleep again—(laughter)-- but what about the men who had to get up at an early hour in the morning and be at their work in five minutes? (Hear, hear.) It was absolutely essential, especially to a firm like the Wire Rope Works, Ltd.. that they should have the use of a "buzzer," because a small bell could not be heard in a wire ropery or a forge. Bells were of no use nowadays. Why should they -allow Messrs. Crosfield's, Monks, Hall and Co., and other firms privileges which they would not grant to the Ropery? He did not believe in preventing the Ropery from having a "buzzer," "just for the sake of a little inconvenience to those fellows who can have another turn over in bed." (Laughter and hear. hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>"SQUEAKY LITTLE TIN POT."</head>
        <p>"SQUEAKY LITTLE TIN POT."</p>
        <p>SECRETARY FOR THIRTY-FIVE</p>
        <p>YEARS:</p>
        <p>Rev. John Yonile And The Lancashire</p>
        <p>Congrmational Union.</p>
        <p>The a cti ve. official connection of *he Rev. , John Yonge with the Liverpool District ,of 'the Lanta- . shire Compwational Muonteame. to an end,on Tuesday, when he resigned from ,the post of secretary, which be had held:for 33 years. Mr. Yoage, who was -formerly pastor of the Wycliffe Church, Warrington, 'one of the , veterans of Lancashire COngregationalism, being 'in his 78th year: For nearly half a century he has been associated with Congregationalism at ' Heywood and Warrington. In 1890 the churches of the county showed, their appreciation of his many services to the denoinination by ,electing hini chairmen Of the Union. He has also been president of the Manchester Congregational Board, and in 1895 the Congregational ministers of Liverpool elected him the chairman of the Liverpool Board. At the annual meeting of the ministers and delegates of the churches in the*LiVerPOol" dis- trict at Birkdale on Tuesday, several speeches were made in praise of Mr. Yonge's services, and it was decided to recommend the Executive Committee of the Lancashire Union to appoint him an honorary secretary for the county. The executive met on Thursday and heartily agreed to this suggestion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Rev. Herbert Dunnico.</head>
        <p>Rev. Herbert Dunnico.</p>
        <p>On the Rev. Herbert Dunnico leaving his church in Liverpool to become secretary to the Peace Society, he was presented with an illuminated address, a cheque and writing case. Mrs. Dunnico was the recipient of a brooch and 117therlifta for -herself and son.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of the Warrington and District Milk Dealers' Association was held in the Corn Exchange, Horsemarket-street, on WedneSday, eKening, .Mr. Thomas Woodward presiding. Mr. J. /lowness, of Clifton-street, Latchford, was appointed. hon. secretary, and Mr. Geoffrey- Brocklehurst, hon. treasurer. A cordial vote of thanks was ,passed. to Mr. Thos. Ford for his services, as lion. secretary.. Mr. Ford has held that position Since the forma- tion of the association in 1899.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Jar</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
