      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>Cafe Chantant.</p>
        <p>A cafe chantant will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday, the 17th inst., in aid of the Raddon.Court Red: Cross. Hospital. ,</p>
        <p>Pound Day. Raddon Court Hospital Pound Day will be held next Thursday. The special needs are vegetables, groceries, tea and cakes. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Fewer Births.</head>
        <p>Fewer Births.</p>
        <p>Nearly 50 per cent. fewer births occurred in Watrington last week than during the corre- sponding week last year. the figures being 26 and 43 respectively. The deaths were almost the same-21 and 22 respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>New Church Annual Lectures.</head>
        <p>New Church Annual Lectures.</p>
        <p>In the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, on Tuesday, the New 'Church annual lectures will corn. mence, when the Rev. J. Ashby, of Sont will speak on "Man In Two</p>
        <p>Fairfield Collecting Society. The annual meeting of the Fairfield Collect- ing Society will be held in the Patten Hall, Winmarleigh-street, on Saturday, February Nth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Caught By " Cow-Catcher " of Trim</head>
        <p>Caught By " Cow-Catcher " of Trim</p>
        <p>Thomas Ciinnington (56), a tanner, of 283, Winwick-road, was found lying on the tram- lines in Winwick-road on Wednesday evening. Whilst in that position he had been caught by the "cow-catcher" of one of the Longford cars, He was conveyed in a serious condition to the Warrington Infirmary by an ambulance from the Lord Derby Military Hospital, and died on Thursday night.</p>
        <p>Rural Council Confers With Winwiek Asylum Committee.</p>
        <p>A conference between the Warrington Rural District Council and the Winwick Asylum Com- mittee re sewage disposal was held on Thursday. The meeting was private and without prejudice.</p>
        <p>British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath, Latchford and Warring- ton Branches of the above Association held a united intercessory meeting on Wednesday afternoon. A good number attended from the different branches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Brick-street Mission.</head>
        <p>Brick-street Mission.</p>
        <p>The Young Men's Bible Class gave a very enjoyable tea and concert on Thursday, when the annual distribution of prizes took place, Mr. W. Barton, their late teacher, handing the books and other very useful article sto the young men, accompanied by a very profitable address. Games were enjoyed after tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Warrington Market Prices.</head>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday-78 Warrington Market the price of wheat ruled at Ss. 6d. per 701 b., and 4s. 6d. to 4s. gd. per 451 b. was raised for oats. Straw brought 675. 6d. and oat bales 70s to 728. 6d. per ton. Flour realised 50s. to 555. per 2801 b., 28. 21-d. to 28. 41-d. beinglhe price of thirds. The price of bran and other offals .was is. 11d. to 2s. Id..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Festival at Bewsev-road</head>
        <p>Festival at Bewsev-road</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>!The Guild Festivk-a of the Bewsey-road Wes- leyan Church will be held to-morrow. The morning and evening sermons will be preached by the Rev. E. B. Keeling, and in the afternoon a musical service will be held, at which Alder- man Bennett will be the speaker and the Mayor will be in the chair. The Rev. E. B. Keeling will lecture on Monday night on "How Prussia Was Manufactured."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Milk Dealers' Association.</head>
        <p>Milk Dealers' Association.</p>
        <p>Local_ Organist Joins the Colours.</p>
        <p>Mr. WilfridThardiman, son of Mr. T. Hardi- man, Manchester-road, has relinquished his position as organist at the Sacred Heart Church, Bank Quay, which he has held since he .was sixteen years of age, for the more strenuous duties of military life. He has joined the 2ndrtth South Lancs. Regiment, now in train- ing in the South. At the conclusion of a recent evening service the members of the choir and several of the leading parishioners wished him every good luck and a safe return.</p>
        <p>Wood's College Social. The day and evening students of Wood's College spent a most enjoyable evening at the Cairo-street Schools last Friday. A varied musical and vocal programme was contribute 4 by the Misses Bate, B. Wright, Hipkisa; Oakley and Rabliitt and Mr. H. Shirtcliff (piano). Miss Saunders acted as pianist for the dancing', and the refreshments committee Were Misses Johnstone, Johnson, Heade, Kenyon, Proud and Turton. Mrs. F. W. Wood received a choice bouquet, and by the students tokens of esteem and respect were made to Mr. F. Wilson Wood (principal and founder) and to Miss C. Butler (head assistant).</p>
        <p>Junior Conservatives' Annual Meeting • At the annual meeting of the members of the iTunior Conservative Club on Thursday evening, Mr. Roger C. Parr presiding, Mr. W. L. Williams (hon. secretary) mentioned that there were now 72 members serving in the Forces. He deeply regretted that several casualties had occurred. These were: Messrs. A. Parker, W. Bradshaw, Captain E. Fairclough, F. Gardner, H. Glover, W. Howarth, S. Monks, A. Moor- house and A. Mulhall. Moving the *re-election of Mr. Parr' as president, Alderman Bennett spoke of the necessity of thinking about prob- lems that would beset the country after the war. Some of the subjects he mentioned were imperial unity, preferential treatment amongst the countries of the Empire and the Allies and taxation, both municipal and national. _</p>
        <p>The election of officials resulted as follows Hon. secretary, Mr. L. Williams; 'hon. treas., Mr. J. Birchall ; representatives on the Con- servative Council, Messrs. W. L. Williams and C. Bowers; hon. auditor, Mr. L. A. Voisey ; com- mittee, Messrs. C Bowers, R. Bloomfield, H Lockett, J. Reyes, B. Ingham and H. Wells. Church Clocks. A correspondent writes:—</p>
        <p>"I was -very much interested in reading the article in last Saturday's issue respecting Church Clocks, and, as for the last 35 yeari) 1 have had experiences with all the clocks referred to, I would like to make one or two observa- tions.</p>
        <p>" Previous to relinquishing my active con- nection with the business I had the oversight of three of the clocks mentioned, the town cloek, St. James' clock, and the Cemetery clock, wiiist,previous to the year 1895, on behalf of the firm with whom I was then emptoyed, W. Woods and Son, for a long period of years 1 regularly wound the clocks at the Parish Church and St. Paul's, Which in those days rendered faithful services in their respective localities. In, addition to these clocks, in those days I wound .week by week what is probably the most interesting turret clock in Warrington, at the Blue Coat School, in Winwick-street. I was always particularly proud of the perform- ances I got from that clock, which, by the way, has only one hand, and its , faithfulness as regards timekeeping can only be determined by hearing it strike the hour. This clock was made not many miles away from Warrington, and to the best of my knowledge it bears the date of 1736. I have known that clock to run three months and not vary more than one minute. -</p>
        <p>"Reverting to those referred to in your article, your suggestion that if any one clock in Warrington is right it must be the town clock is quite justifiable, as its record, which is written from week to week in the tower each time it is wound, shows its variations to be very small indeed. This clock, along with the clock in St. James' Church, were made by Messrs. Joyce, of Whitchurch, who were, during last week, apart from the public gaze, quietly putting the clock at St. James' in order, after an accident it has recently sustained, and before your next issue. it is highly probable it will have regained its former habits, which are usually of a very creditable character. The mechanism of both the town clock and St. James' clock is ,of a very interesting nature'</p>
        <p>w</p>
        <p>A trio of convalescent soldiers were on the war-path as auctioneers at the Brabazon Society's sale of work at the residence of Mrs. Broadbent, The HoHies, Latchford, on Thurs- day afternoon. They were dreadfully in earnest, businesslike, bright spirited (as becomes.soldiers either in or out of the trenches—preferably out) and mucous voieed (as becomes auctioneers). One fellow stood at the table and wielded a rolling-pin joy- fully and nonchalantly like a swagger cane, bringing it down on the table with a .75 gun percussion which made the ladies quake. Per- haps it was in order to have done with the noise that the bidding was hastened. Another soldier held aloft the various lots—mainly embroidery, rugs and cakes—after the manner of a professional assistant. He was anxious that clients should understand what they were bidding for and frequently passed down the chairs to impress the ladies with the quality of the articles. Another soldier sat booking the takings at a desk, meekly, but efficiently, like Bob Cratchit. Occasionally he warmed his hands by placing them on his head and not by andlolight. He was sandy. • ne of the rolling pin had a brislowa,y with him. He was out for fun as well as trade. "As you know," he said, "these articles havebeen made mostly by the inmates of the Workhouse. We want to sell them in order to buy them more materials, supply them with books, &amp;c., and give them a ' treat ' now and again, and buy a gramophone. Now, ladies, it is a good cause. Let's do our best for them. We might be in the Workhouse ourselves some day."_</p>
        <p>And so the bids ran up. Once there was a lapse. He of the rolling-pin turned upon him with the upheld hand: "It's your head that's spoiling it," he said, "why don't you put your hat on? You've frightened them!" He was as bald as a plum and blushed like an American Baldwin apple.</p>
      </div>
