      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Friends. and War.</head>
        <p>Friends. and War.</p>
        <p>To-morrow evening, at the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket-street, Mrs. A. C. Wilson, of Adanchester, will give an address on.'4`Friends and War."</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. An exhibition of the photograo..; el the blu,ve. Society was opened at their rooms on Tuesday, and proved to be one of the most interesting functions of the year. Amongst the exhibitors were Mrs. Lowe, Miss Jackson, and Messrs. J. Harding, J. W. Rowlands, H. Boscoe, K. F. Bishop, C. Hale, A. Davis, W. R. Jackson, E. Seymour, A. Barnett, Hughes, F: Holloway, A. Frost and S. Massey.</p>
        <p>Gas Offices May Be Removed. The Gas Committee having decided that the Emergency Sub-committee be instructed to in- quire into the propriety of removing the staff from the Mersey-street offices to Bank House, Alderman Bennett, at the Town Council meet- ing on Tuesday, said that if the idea were ,adopted it would be carrying out what many members of the Council had advocated for • years, and he believed it would be welcomed by the public. The Council agreed to the minutes. • Fox As Pet. - Mrs. Eleanor A. Bridgwater, of Sylfaen, Stockton Heath, writes as _follows : "I see in a newspaper a photograph of a baby fox found in a trench in France by the French soldiers, who were affectionately 'mothering' it. As all dumb animals are responsive to intelligent understanding of them, I have no doubt those kindly friends will train it to be- come a valuable domestic pet, with the same good luck that befel one rescued by our British . Tommies' during the Battle of Waterloo, which became the regimental pet. On the con- clusion of the war it was brought by Colonel Hindle, to Thelwall, where, in the early 'forties, one of its descendants acted as watch- dog in the yard kennel there. When the house . was taken over by the Stantons some 19 years ago, a Mr. Charles Fairhurst, farmer, of Mar- tinscroft, confirmed this story. As a boy he lived at Thelwall, was employed at the Old Powder Mill, played • the bassoon at the little , chapel where his sister sang in the choir on the Sunday."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Pansy League and Mr. Stewart Royston.</head>
        <p>Pansy League and Mr. Stewart Royston.</p>
        <p>If the object of the Pansy League is to instil in the minds of young people a desire "to be thoughtful of others," then its members have indeed shown it for our brave fighters in a very practical, way by sending the handsome sum of £ll to Mr. Stewart Royston, Sunnyside, Paddington, Warrington, for his Wounded Soldiers' Walking-stick Fund. Every dona- tion is paid in as received and no military hospital will be refused a grant of walking- sticks for the wounded if funds in hand are sufficient to cover the cost. Upwards of two hundred thousand have already been distri- buted, and a great number will be required if the terrible war continues during the summer months.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Simpson, R.A.M.C.. Ad- ministrator of the Lord Derby War Hospital, Warrington, writes: "Please let me thank you for your successful effort to obtain 750 walk- ing-sticks for the sick and wounded at this hospital. Your kindness. is very much appre- ciated. I have arranged, as you desire, that the stick shall become the property of the soldier, and that he may take it away with him when he leaves the hospital."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SOLDIER AUCTIONEERS</head>
        <p>SOLDIER AUCTIONEERS</p>
        <p>Persuade Ladies To Make Good Bids For Crochet And Cakes.</p>
        <p>WHO DID THE WORK?</p>
        <p>!- It .was -certainly an interesting event. Mrs. 'Mortimer had opened the 'sale' and Miss Broad- bent, the energetic secretary and, treasurer; had explained that the work was made by of the Workhouse Hospital 'and 'lnfirmary. The Brabaxon Society certainty brought pleasure` into their lives.:</p>
        <p>There was a pin-cushion competition taken part in by helpers. Miss Amy Grounds gained the first prize, and among other prize winners were Corporal Tipper and Private Jowsei, of itaddon Court Hospital.</p>
        <p>The raffle for the cake ended rather happily. Mrs. Broadbent drew out of a bag the ticket purchased by Mrs.' Davies ; the donor, and she, with excellent -promptitude, handed it to a wounded soldier. Accepting it on behalf of Raddon Court, he bowed as exquisitely as an eighteenth century courtier. It was quite a picturesque movement. It beat forming fours. Subscriptions to the Society were received from Miss Ethel Appleton, Miss Truda Cros- field, Mrs. Charles Lyon, Mrs. Long, Mrs. Mill- ing, Mrs. Gordon Roberts, Mrs. Edward Fox, Mrs. Lionel Pairclough, Mrs. Carleton, Mrs. H. Cock, Miss Wheeldon and Mrs. Holt. The helpers were Mrs. and the Misses Broadbent, Miss Ethel Appleton. Mrs. Ford, Mrs. Park- house. Mrs: Booth, Mias Ruane, 'Mrs: Mathias and Mrs. J. P. Booth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>. WANT MORE MONEY.</head>
        <p>. WANT MORE MONEY.</p>
        <p>Labourers Decline To Accept Sir G. Askwith's Award.</p>
        <p>A further meeting of the unskilled and semi- skilled labourers in the engineering trades in Warrington and district was held in the Socialist Haiti Bridge Foot, on Tuesday, to con- sider the award of Sir G. Askwith in regard to the wages of men in the Manchester area, which includes South-West Lancashire. The new scale has already come into operation. The 3s. war bonus was converted into an acrrance of 45., thus bringing the wages of labourers in Warrington up to 265. per week;</p>
        <p>-The meeting decided not to accept the award, but to press for the original claim of 6s. a week over the bonus.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CARTERS GET BONUS.</p>
        <p>Representatives of masters and men having interviewed Sir David llorrell and Sir George Gibbs at the Board of Trade, the carters' dis- pute in Warrington has been settled, an extra war bonus of 2s. per week having been agreed to. A carter looking after one horse will now receive 28s. a week, and one in charge of twv) horses 31s. The employers express the hope that men will attend regularly to their work. as neglect means loss to both employers and employes, besides great inconvenience to tho works 'they serve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>!.... – 7 mil</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
