      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>£2,350.</head>
        <p>£2,350.</p>
        <p>Minimising The Danger If They Agricultural Society's Jumble Come. Sale's Excellent Result.</p>
        <p>The financial result of the Agricultural Society's Jumble Sale, held on November 18th at Wilderspool, has exceeded all expectations, and the SUM to be shared between the Agricul- tural Relief of the Allies Fund and the British Farmers' Red Cross-Funds-amounts to £2,350. A meeting of the committee was held at the Corn Exchange, Warrington. on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. William Bainbridge was in the chair and among those present were: Mr. John Backhouse, Mr. Edward Marsh (vice- chairmen of the committee), Mr. J. Mainwaring (hon. auctioneer), Mr. Harry Gandy (hon. secretary) and, Alderman Arthur Bennett (hon. treasurer), as well as a large number of the committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett's financial statement, which had been audited by Mr. Sidney Tunstall, showed that the receipts amounted to .£2,434 4s. 9d. and, after the expenses had been deducted, the balance in the bank available for distribution amounted to 0422,349 18s. 11d. This sum has been made into a round figure of 0f.:2,350.</p>
        <p>Submitting this statement to the meeting, Alderman Bennett said he had never submitted a statement with greater satisfaction in his life; it was a credit to everybody concerned, and exceeded their most sanguine expectations. The Chairman: It is most satisfactory. Secretary's Report.</p>
        <p>In his report, Mr. Gandy paid a tribute to the Catering Committee. He said: "I can only speak in the highest praise of the ener- getic ad enthusiastic way in which Mr. Willett and his colleagues, both ladies and , gentlemen, worked, and we all. know of the great benefits which we received by .their -efforts?' -; The re- port continued: "'The admirable arrange- ments of the ground had been superintended by Mr. Hugh Darbyshire, who, as captain, was seconded by a very strong body of: stewards. The three auctioneers worked hard throughout the day and well merited the praise which their abilities evoked on all hands. . . The committee received very substantial help in various ways from Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co.. Peter Walker and Son, .IL Rogers, Ralph Johnson; the Warrington Co-operative Society, the Band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, and, last but not least, the War- rington and District Licensed Victuallers' Association, who provided and worked the bar free of all costs."</p>
        <p>It was formally decided to share the money equally between the two funds mentioned. "thanks to the Workers.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mainwaring moved that the best thanks of the meeting should be given to the President of the Committee (Sir Gilbert Greenall), Mr. William Bainbridge (chairman), Messrs. Backhouse and Marsh (vice-chairmen), Mr. H. Gandy ,(lion. secretary), and Alderman Bennett (hon. treasurer). Mr. William Weir seconded. The motion was carried with enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>In his reply, Mr. Gandy referred to the assistance that had been given him by Messrs. Moorfield; Dykes and Darhyshire. Alderman Bennett spoke of the Services of Messrs. T. Dunvilre, Walter Dennett and George. Rice.</p>
        <p>= The Chairman moved a vote of thanks to the Honorary Auctioneers iMessrs. J. Mainwaring, L. Bennett and Stafford), and also to their stag.</p>
        <p>Mr. Marsh seconded and the motion was car- ried unanimously. Mr. Mainwaring and Mr. Bennett replied. Mr. Stafford, it was stated by the Chairman, "has gone to do a little bit more" for his country since the sale. Guetsing Competitions.</p>
        <p>During the meeting the Chairman announced the results of the guessing competitions which were held on the day of the sale. The first competition, in which the competitors endea- voured to guess the total amount that would be realised on the sales, was as follows :-1, H. B. Nicholson, Broad Heath, Padgate (who guessed ,t 2,433 4s. 7d.); 2, Mr. T. Williams, Ship Inn, Lower Walton (.02,435 10s. 6d.); 3, Miss B. Darbyshire, 13ellefields, Appleton (1:2,435 15s. 61.).</p>
        <p>The other competition was to guess the weight of a live sheep *hich had been given by Mr. G. Dufton. The correct weight of the sheep was 1341 h., and this figure was guessed by the following, who will share the prize :- 111-r. Robert Walker (Midland Hotel); Dr, W. J. Peacocke, Mr. T. H. Pretty (Warrington), and Mr. Beswick (Dingle Parm, Appleton).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WORKS' HOOTER TO WARN US.</head>
        <p>WORKS' HOOTER TO WARN US.</p>
        <p>- The first man in Warrington. to know of the excursion to England of :the •Zeppelinsextet• on Monday evening was the Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls). He immediately in- formed the Electricity and Gas Works, who had all lights in the street turned out, and the halo, or glow, which people in the districts 'observe over the town at night-time, disap- peared.</p>
        <p>'Those in the quiet of their homes lind in the gaiety of the amusement houses were fav- oured with light as ustuil and were unaware of the gloom outside. But when they emerged they soon remarked the change and. experi- .ericed considerable difficulty in proceeding Along the. streets. 'lt was ones of the darkest nights we have had for some timein -. being allowed to maintain their lights the amusement halls here were more fortunate than in Manchester, for instance, where the electric current was completely cut off. The most piquant incident locally was at St. Tames' tea party, where dancing was timed' to con- clude at 11.30, but .cea.sed an hour earlier at the suggestion of the authorities. who desired the lights to be extinguished. THE TOWN COUNCIL DISCUSS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>LIGHTING.</head>
        <p>LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>The Denihnd For Improvement.</p>
        <p>It was a singular coincidence that after months of meagre Vlumination of the streets the Electricity and Tramways Committee re- ported to the Town Council on Tuesday as follows</p>
        <p>Complaints having been ,received of the restrictions on. the lighting or the main streets, and the Town Clerk having advised that there was no legal Order in force restricting .the lighting, it was . resolved that the engineer be instructed to confer with the Chief • Constable, with a view to the improved lighting of the main thoroughfares.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett expressed the hope that the minute did not mean that there was to be any relaxation of the precautions they had taken, and the grim necessity of which had been emphasised within the last few hours. On Saturday he saw a light in the sky, which he at first =took for the 'moon. It gradually dawned on him that it could not be the 'moon and it actually turned out to be Trinity clock: (Laughter.) The thought passed through his mind: Is all the danger over? Have .we been living in a nightmare? Have all the precau- tions of the last few Months been .futile and unnecessary? If not, why do we suddenly light the clock?" Alderman Tinnion explained that he raised the question at the Watch Committee because on New Year's Eve the town was in utter darkness, and to many people going home from places of worship it was a great source of danger. But to increase the lighting above the restricted limit now in force until 11.30 or 12 -o'clock would; be a great mistake. While on Special occasions the lighting might be con- tinued a little later, he thought the reduced lighting was, generally speaking,. sufficient for the main streets of the town. • Councillor Joseph said the matter of street lighting was hardly one for the Elec- tricity and Tramways Committee, but for the Watch Committee, the Mayor, or the Chief Constable to determine. It was easy to be wise after the event.. Until the previous night very few people thought a Zeppelin visit was within the range of practical politics. He had not the slighteSt doubt that precautions would be taken in_ future.</p>
        <p>Thus, we may safely leave the lighting ar- rangements in the hands of the Chief Con- stahie.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>BE PREPARED!</head>
        <p>BE PREPARED!</p>
        <p>..,ifinal Arranged For Ciiidoner Of</p>
        <p>Residents.</p>
        <p>It has been arranged that in ease of 'the appearance of hostile aircraft in this district the hooter at Crosfield's Works. Bank Quay, shall sound two-minute blasts, with an inter- val of half a minute between each blast. The identity and reach of this hooter are unmis- takable. and, on hearing the signal, the townspeople are advised to remain in their homes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Turn Off the Gas.</head>
        <p>Turn Off the Gas.</p>
        <p>Householders, in case of an attack, are strongly advised to turn off the gas at the meter and resort to candles- to prevent fires from bombs spreading. In houses, shops and offices there should always be an Ample supply of buckets filled with water, and for larger Premises small 'hand-pumps. Pitchers and other minor household vessels should be kept filled ready for use, and where there are baths, washtubs, &amp;c., these should be kept filled, so That a supply of water may be available inde- Pendently of the water mains. Where there are garden utensils, such as syringes, garden Pumps, Sie, they should be readily accessible. The principal dangers in raids are :—(1) The explosion, (2) risk of .poisonous gases being re- leased. and (3) the danger of fire. When pos- sible, a well-protected cellar is the Safest place against the first.named, and as an alternative tho occupants should select such well-protected shelter as may be aviiilable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Other Precautions.</head>
        <p>Other Precautions.</p>
        <p>_Although most ,of the .borafri.'carried by the 'Zeppelins pre of an incendiary, character. it IS as well to be prepared against the dangers of poisonous gases. Theise are acid in character, 4ind can, be neutralised by common washing soda: A solution might be Prepared by dis- solving Ilb. in a gallon of water. A widely- meshed bath-towel dipped in this, squeezed out and applied to the mouth will afford protection in an atmosphere contaminated by chlorine, nitrogen oxides, or sulphurous acid. - There is .nothing so effectual as a wet blanket thrown over the scene bf conflagration, followed by the application of buckets of water on the -blanket. As much water as possible should be poured on as promptly as possible. The fire brigade should be notified as early its possible. The main thing is to keep cool, and to get to work at once on the conflagration.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTONIANS IN THE MIDLANDS. A Warrington lady and gentleman who, prior to their marriage lived at Warrington, and sub- *icemen* went to reside in the Midlands, were out visiting on Monday night. and during their absence a bomb dropped upon their home and 'yr reeked it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</head>
        <p>PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</p>
        <p>Major (temporary Lieutenant-Colonel) ,IL K. Woods, South Lancashire Uegiment (cOmmand- inß Service Battalion), wha was formerly stationed at Orford Barracks, has been gazetted Brevet-Colonel for services in the field. The Prince of Wales' Volunteers (South Lancashire. Regiment).--i-Lieutenants to be tem- porary Captains: Edward S. Catlow and Geoffrey C. Townroe ; Second:Lieutenants to be Temporary Lieutenants: George, C. Owen and PPreY Carter.</p>
        <p>Major Mott Honoured. In a list of names of officers and men whom Genera/ 'Sir lan 'Hamilton has mentioned for services in connection with the operations described in his dispatch of December 11th, 1915, their appears the name of Captain (Temporary Major) G. R. Mott, of the Reserve. of 'Officers, who has been serving in Gallipoli with the Bouth Lancashire Regiment (Service Battalion), and who was, some years ago, st*tioned at Warrington as Adjutant to the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment (Territorial Force).</p>
        <p>Mr. J. M. Cobb a Passenger on the</p>
        <p>Appam. Mr. J. Mansfield Cobb, well-known in War- rington as resident engineer for nearly three ,Soars on the Warrington Bridge work.), was a Pas!tenger on the Appam.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FOR BELGIAN WOUNDED. Continental Artistes At The Royal.,Court:Theatre.</head>
        <p>FOR BELGIAN WOUNDED. Continental Artistes At The Royal.,Court:Theatre.</p>
        <p>• In co-operation with Sir,Joseph Beecham and Mr. H. L. Warburton, a concert was given in the Royal Court Theatre on Sunday evening, under the auspices of the British Red Cross Society, in aid of funds for relieving Belgian wounded soldiers. The project was ,well 'sup- ported by a sympathising public, and it is pleas- ing to record that some £4O was realised for the deserving object named. - The artistes concerned were a group of vocal- ists and instrumentalists of high repute in musical centres in Holland and Belgium, and it will be remembered that one of them, M. Jules Colbert; appeared in Warrington on a similar occasion a little over a year ago. Although much of the vocal music was, un- fortunately, not given in the English language, and, therefore, not altogether intelligible to many of those present, there was a high degree of enthusiasm shown throughout the evening. in, regard to the singing of the concert. party. The two ladies, .)line. Van Hee and Mine.• Riuini, have 'voices .of a sweet but somewhat 'hard quality; butheing, both of them, tempera- mentally gifted, they used their vocal powers in so wholeilearted and soulful a manner that criticism WM largely disarmed by the compelling interest of interpretations, in which great ani- mation and brilliance of vocalisation were always conspicuous features. The distinguish- ing mark of extreme vivacity was, furthermore, shown also by the two male vocalists, M. Van .Roey and M. Colbert; indeed, it is a moot point ;.whet her the last-named .- singer . is not so far emotional in the use of his baritone voice as partially to obscure its tone and timbre. Some song-excerpts from the, operas of Gounod and MaSSeriet were, -ainthigst others, excellently ren- dered, by each and all of the members: of the concert-party, and each Was in turn recalled with vociferous applause. Of the few English songs to be found in the programme it is to be regretted that so large a proportion were by one composer, even could these songs be claimed to be of all-absorbing interest, which is by no. means the case. Such vocal morceaux therefore as Lolir's "Little grey' home in the West," Haydn Wood's "0 flower divine," and Mar- shall's "I hear you • calling me," served. agree- ably .to leaven with a: thOroughly English flavour the mass of Vocal items-Which. did not otherwise appeal strongly to 'the audience, incidentally securing for•the'singers concerned unmistakable evidence of ,high atipreelitibu of 'the manner of their interpretation. The violin solos by M. Camby evinced a highly finished technique coupled with great intensity of feeling. M. Camby's .tone ,was not great in volume, but it was singularly,,Ture in quality, his double-stopping. and . harmonics being espe- cially true and clear. M. Paul Rocks played the piano accompaniments with conspicuous ability, but with a slight. tendency to over- assertiveness.. Under the coriduetors;hip of Mr. James: .noy, . the ,Royal Court Theatre's aug- mented orchestra performed 'one of Auber's over- tures and a selection from Gounod's "Mirella."</p>
        <p>In course of the evening the Mayor, Alderman Peacock. took occasion to thank the audience for their appreciation of the efforts of the artistes, who had come specially from London for the occasion. Warrington was one of the firstilgorthern towns to extend hospitality (some 14 months ago) to Belgian refugees, and it afforded him great gratification to know that the people of this town had done everything in their power to assist the fugitives from the gal- lant Belgian nation. That evening the audi- ence were showing their sympathy with the wounded soldiers of Belgium. Special thanks were due to Sir Joseph Beecham for so kindly lending the theatre, and to Mr. H. L. Warbur- ton (manager) and staff for their willing and courteous co-operation in carrying out the con- cert arrangements. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ZEPPS.</head>
        <p>THE ZEPPS.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 5, 1916.</p>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN J. JOHNSON.</p>
        <p>Popular Soldier Dies From Wounds In</p>
        <p>The Head.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. William Johnson, who reside at 49, Bewsey-road, 'on Saturday received news I that their son, Rifleman John Johnson, of the Rifle Brigade, had died from wounds. The message conveying. the ill-tidings was from Father Francis Newton, 17th Casualty Clearing Station, who, writing under date January 25th, said :-- "It is with the deepest sorrow that I have to inform you that your son, J. Johnson, 7th Rifle Brigade, died on the 23rd from wounds in the head. 'He was quite unconscious when he arrived at the hospital. I buried him in our little cemetery amongst his comrades, and a cross marks his last resting place. May God bless you and keep you, and give you His grace to bear your trouble." Before enlisting, Rifleman Johnson, who</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>HARD TIMES IN SER.% It.</p>
        <p>Fighting Rain, Snow, Frost—And The Bulgarians.</p>
        <p>Private J. Shaw, of the Inniskilling Fusiliers, writing to his wife in Warrington, from th.. Convalescent Camp near Alexandria, Egypt. says:— .</p>
        <p>"Since writing you last from Salc.niel, we have experienced some rough tames I must say before proceeding, we xere the first English division to land at Salonica. After lying here a-short. time we were ordered up country, and entrained (in waggons) , for Servia, eventually arriving at .Glevgilli (Servia).. Hem. after a short rest by the Koadside, we were told we had a three days' march before Us, and then proceeded, passing through many deserted villages. Night came and we were ordered to take out our oil sheet and blanket and get down to it. At midnight the rain came pouring in torrents, and we were completely 'washed out.' There was nothing for it but to get up and walk about till daybreak when, after a Mirried meal, we were on the trudge again. This marching by day and bivouacing ;by night was carried on for three days, until we arrived at our tern-. porary destination, a village named Dedili. After being here five days we had orders to move again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>4,000 Feet High.</head>
        <p>4,000 Feet High.</p>
        <p>" After another day's march we relieved the ' French on Rocky Peak. This peak, I might say, is almost 4,000 feet high, and the narrow road up it at its widest part is only 4ft. No trans- port or mules could get up, which meant we had to carry all our supplies up.. At daybreak the following morning our outposts brought in five -Bulgarian prisoners, who had given them- selves up, a corporal being amongst them who must have been a watch repairer by trade. Luckily he had some tools in a tin box, so our Captain and several others had their watches repaired free of charge. After the second day up here snow came and our worst troubles be- gan. It snowed, blew and froze for six days and nights. sleep was. impossible. Firing en both sides practically ceased. We mind see the liublins on our left and Om Bulgars on our front running about to keep themselves warm. Our great. coats were frozen stiff on us. I never felt so cold in all .my 'life. Men were taken away frost-bitten= in hands awl feet. After the ninth day we wei-e. relieved by the Irish Fusiliers. Frost-bitten.</p>
        <p>"This day I Nuke to grief, contracting frost- bite in my feet before being relieved. It was a common sight to see our chaps digging with picks in the ice and snow for their equip- ment, &amp;c. Eventually a batch of frost-bitten cases were whipped off to the field ambulance, and from there in motor vans to Dorien. Here we entrained and arrived at Salonica, and were dispatched to the Canadian hospital, where we were treated very well. From here we were shipped and sent to Alexandria. On arrival we were again put-in motors and dispatched to 15th General Hospital, and from hero to my present addiress. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Like Summer In Egypt.</head>
        <p>Like Summer In Egypt.</p>
        <p>"I have now been here almost four weeks, and am pleased'to say my feet are improving.- The weather.here is Al, being almost like an English summer, and this is now • winter. My . chum, who also had frost-bitten feet, left the regiment two day's 'before- me, and I- thought he 'mist have been sent home when, .judge of my sur- prise yesterday morning I got a letter from him qaying he is in hospital in Cairo, so I hope, in the near future, to meet him once again down here. where he must eventually come to join the details.</p>
        <p>"I am sorry to say I have not seen "The Examiner" now for this last nine weeks. I have a friend who sends it regular. but since shifting about so much I am afraid they have gone astray, as I had received no correspondence from home or friends for eight weeks. until yesterday morning, when I received a batch. "J. SHAW. "Signaller."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LETTERS OF A ROYAL ENGINEER.</head>
        <p>LETTERS OF A ROYAL ENGINEER.</p>
        <p>Picturesque Description 01 Life At</p>
        <p>The Front.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TYRES. A Warrington member of the Royal Engineers in France, writes home as follows:</p>
        <p>A THOUGHTFUL HEN.</p>
        <p>"It is like a mid-summer day to-day (Sun- day), and we are fixed up again in a dirty old farm. The church bells are ringing, and it looks very. much like: old England, until we heaf the old dames with their ' Merci Beaucoup,' Gui &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"We are having a rest quite a long way from the firing-line, where we can only hear the dis- tant roar of artillery and see nothing but the usual sights of a little country. village. I know you will be pleased to know this; for you can reckon on your 'dear little boy' being quite safe and out of danger.</p>
        <p>"Of course, -when we are resting we generally work harder than if we were up in the front line, and we get fed up with the rests just as quickly as with occupying the line. I have got a nice cushy job working with the Q.M.S.—that is, when I am not drawing; it's better than dig- ging trenches, you know.</p>
        <p>- "-I am billeted in an old stable, where I keep my little store of surveying instruments, draw- ing tackle and spare clothing, &amp;T., for the troops. I always get'a fresh egg for breakfast, for a saucy, little hen has the audacity to come and lay its eggs in a corner of my billet, and so to compensate for the impudence X take the</p>
        <p>a We are all in the best of health, quite well, and nothing to worry about."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>FAMOUS MOTOR TYRES.</head>
        <p>FAMOUS MOTOR TYRES.</p>
        <p>• "The weather is still behaving itself, and I have the privilege of touring the countryside in that familiar G.S. waggon. • True it has a few springs, but with a four horse team trotting along the roads for 15 or 20 miles each .way, day after day, I am not ashamed to say that I sometimes get rather weary of it. "The country is rather barren and there is nothing much to interest one. So tt, pass the time away I have acquired the .habit of trying to guess the make of the heavy motor lorries as they, come tumbling past me—and I am get- ting quite a dab hand at the game. You can just imagine me sitting on the G.S. waggon guessing names of motors as they hurry past. It was while employed on this game that I noticed the number of tyres with the famous red line. I mean, of course, the St. Helens .Cable Co.'s production, the S.R. tyre. I see scores of them in the course of a day—and if the motor lorries are at rest, take the trouble to see the condition they are in. You will be pleased to: know that the dear old Warrington production compares very favourably with any. other make, and motor drivers in general consider they have clicked ' if their lorry is fitted up with the famous S.R. tyre. "I mention this because I am exceedingly proud of the old town, and, of course, proud of its products, and so when I see the familiar red line I think of dear old Warrington and the well-known works at Mersey side. We are in good health and quite happy, nothing to worry us except plenty of work, and, of course, Lanca- shire boys can always work."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>TER II FIC BOMBARDMENT.</head>
        <p>TER II FIC BOMBARDMENT.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Craik Says He Is In The Thick Of It.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Fred Craik, son of Councillor Craik, Manchester-road, writing home this week, says: "A lull in the fighting means a letter home, but I am afraid I shall be interrupted before I get far with this. It may interest you to know that I am just breathing the fresh air after hav- ing had a gas attack by the Germans. They have been trying very litird to break us up since we came into action, and we have had prac- tically no rest. Last night at 11 o'clock we were firing away, and at five this morning we were at it again. .We have had no rest since, and are again, awaiting the order 'Open fire.' There has been a very heavy artillery bombardment to- day, and. it has been a job:dodging the shells. They have sent us all sorts—bigh. explosives,</p>
        <p>shrapnel*, 'Jack Johnsons,'-&amp;c.—and it has been an experience, I can tell you, "Our battery has been very fortunate tip to now, thank God, for we have no casualties. Other batteries have suffered rather badly. Letter Hurriedly Finished.</p>
        <p>"We are fairly in the thick of it. We have come across our local 'Terriers,' South Lanca- shires and other regiments closely associated . _ _ _</p>
        <p>with our district. i do believe tile Germans are trying to gas us again, for my eyes are smarting terribly. They are up to all sorts of tricks. I shall soon get the signal for gas hel- mets, so' shall be all right. Beds are unknown things here, and we sleep, about the guns just as we are, ready to fire at a moment's notice. This will have to be finished hurriedly, for we are being shelled like fury. ft seems as though our positions are known."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>PRIVATE ERNEST LITTLEWOOD.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST LITTLEWOOD.</p>
        <p>however, now recovered, and is home on a short furlough. Private Littlewood, who is 25 years of age and single, was formerly employed in the netting department of Messrs. Rylands Bros.' works. He was wounded in the head during the thrill- ing charge on the 16th June, and, writing home after the event, told how he had accounted for five Germans, and how his rifle, bayonet and all equipment were blown to bits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL WM. STRINGER</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL WM. STRINGER</p>
        <p>Territorial Thinks Germans have Shot Their Bolt.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal William Stringer, whose home address is 6, . Manley-street, Warrington, on Tuesday, arrived for a brief furlough from the firing-line. Twenty years of age, he is attached to the bomb throwing section of the local Territorials.</p>
        <p>As a tribute to the popular Lance-Corporal's homecoming, the street in which he resides is gaily decorated with festoons. Lance-Corporal Stringer, looking the piature of health, was drafted to the Front on the 10th of February of last year, and has been thrOugh many stirring engagements, , fortunately without sustaining any injuries. He was in the severe fighting for the occupation of Hill 60, besides being in the memorable British charge on September 25th. June 10tb also saw him in the thick of the fray, an occasion which, to quote the Lance-Corporal's own words, was literally hell itself. Interviewed by our representative, he said that so close was his regiment to the, German lines when he departed from the Front, that it was an easy matter to throw messages on paper from their own lines into the German trenches. "Often," says he, "have I picked up scraps of paper thrown by the foe imploring us to ex- change food for their cigarettes, which, needless to say, have not borne fruit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Germans In a Bad Way.</head>
        <p>Germans In a Bad Way.</p>
        <p>"To my mind they are in a bad way, for all the. Germans I have seen seemed half- starved and heartily sick of the war. The messages seat over are all written in English, which.shows that many Germans who, prior to the war, resided in England, must be in their ranks. Mother and many kind friends used to forward parcels of eatables, but they are un- necessary, for oat there we have everything we want. There is no lack of food, which means a grTt deal to us. The fighting just before 1 came away was rather severe, but I think they have shot their bolt, and the efforts they are making now are frenzied, with the knowledge that it is their last chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>His Biggest Frfglit.</head>
        <p>His Biggest Frfglit.</p>
        <p>"I used to say that there was never a German bullet made for me, and it seems I am right, at least up to now. It is rather humorous," he continued, `l. but the biggest fright of the last twelve months I have experienced has been dur- ing these last few days, for it took three ships to bring 'yours- truly' home. The first .boat had to put back owing to the .terrible weather, and the other broke down. The 'crossing was simply awful, and so bad that I even wished myself back in the firing line. However, it was with great joy when I saw old England in the</p>
        <p>distance. I can tell you I looked a fine .old sight, what with the caked mud of the trenches and the natural result of a storm-tossed boat. All the lads.l left are in fine condition, and eager to finish the task set before them. Yes! I think it won't be long before we are all home for good." , Lance-Corporal Stringer, before joining the Forces, was employed by Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.. Ltd., and was connected with st. Barnabas' Church, where he is highly esteemed and admired. He celebrated his,twentieth birth- day in the 'trenches, and returns to the battle- field on Wednesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Asked To Rescind Their Unpatriotic Resolution.</p>
        <p>The following letter has been sent to Mr. Chas. Dukes, secretary of the Trades and Labour Council, by Mr. A. Morris, Warrington Branch Secretary of the Amalgamated Union of Co- operative Employee :-- [corY.)</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir,—l, have been instructed to for- ward to you the following resolution, passed by the members of the above branch, asking also that you will place same before the next Trades Council meeting:— "I remain, "Yours fraternally, "A. moon's. Hon. Sec." Resolution.</p>
        <p>"That this meeting of the local branch of the A.U.C.B. deplores the attitude taken over the Military Service Bill by, the Local Trades Council, but we respectfully ask them to rescind their resolution, and, if possible, get a canvass of the various Trades Unions in the town. We contend they have, in passing such'a re,solution, overstepped the bounds of the principle of Trade Unionism. Failing the resolution being rescinded, we shall have to consider about sever- ing our connection with them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NOT UNDER THE CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>NOT UNDER THE CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>Met to consider whether they should affiliate with the Trades and Labour Council, .the War- rington Branch of the Operative Brieklayttrs' Society, on 'Monday, unaniipously decided not-to do so "under the -present conditions."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RIFLEMAN JOHN JOHNSON.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN JOHN JOHNSON.</p>
        <p>was 27 years of age, was employed as an agent for the Prudential Assurance Company, Ltd. He was a well-known figure in Warrington, and his geniality and good nature gained for him the respect and esteem of a large circle of friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Johnson And Durham—Boxer.</head>
        <p>Johnson And Durham—Boxer.</p>
        <p>As a boy he attended the Sacred Heart and St. Alban's Schools, an was a regular wor- shipper at St. Alban's Church. He was also -a member of the Junior Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>Mr. Johnson was slightly wounded in the fingers on December Bth, but after a sojourn of a fortnight, he returned to his duties. He wrote his last letter—a very cheerful one—to his married sister in Elaine-street, in which he said he had again--this was the second time—been recommended for a stripe, but he had refused, "for I prefer to stay with the 'boys.' We are just going down to the firing line, and are look- ing forward to a little bit of excitement." During the whole time he .had been in the Army, Rifleman Johnson—he himself a boxer— was a favourite comrade of Joe Durham, the well-known local boxer. Indeed, they had shared many a parcel together. He also went in for a little swimming. A Churo's Glowing Tribute.</p>
        <p>Corporal J. - Cowan (a Birmingham man), writing to Mrs. McKay, i 5, Elaine-street, sister of the late Rifleman Johnson, says that death resulted from concussion of the brain arising from a wound received while in the trenches. He did all he could for the injured man. "I must say," he adds, "that the death of poor Jack has thoroughly unnerved me, as he was my chum ever since he has been out here, and he was more than a brother to me. In fact there was nobody in the regiment more loved and esteemed by his comrades than your brother, and all the boys in my company wish to ex- press their deep sorrow at your loss, and hope that you will try to bear It. He has died the death of a hero in -defence of England, hearth and home. . . . I tender my heartfelt sympathy to you and his family, and hope that before long we shall be able to avenge the death''of one of the best lads I-have ever met." Rifleman Johnson's rnother has received many letters of sympathy, including one from Mr. Brocklehurst and fellow employes of the Pru- dential Society locally, all speaking in the highest terms of the deceased as a friend and a colleague.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MR. NIILN'S SOLDIEII-SONS.</head>
        <p>MR. NIILN'S SOLDIEII-SONS.</p>
        <p>Two Of Them Wounded—Another On Sick Leave.</p>
        <p>Two sons of Mr. G. P. Miln, of Warrington and Chester, who are serving in the Public Schools Battalion .of the Royal FUsiliers' France, have recently been wounded by a rifle grenade. The wounds of the younger one, Mr. W. Wallace Mile, are described as serious, and it will probably be two: or three weeks before lie is •able to be sent home. The wounds of the elder brother, Mr. J. Douglas Miln, -are happily less severe; Both speak favourably of, the•treat- ment they are receiving at one of the Casualty Clearing. Stations, and .one of them adds in his letter: "Oh! the luxury of lying down I'llll __</p>
        <p>length in bed after fifteen- days in the trenches, sleeping anyhow in a very much over-crowded dug-out, is very Much appreciated.", Another of .11r. Milies sons, Lieut. Gordon Miln, of the Bth Cheshires, is at present home on sick leave suffering from the effects of jaun- dice and frost-bite, contracted in Gallipoli.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>NEARLY WASHED AWAY AT</head>
        <p>NEARLY WASHED AWAY AT</p>
        <p>SALONIKA.</p>
        <p>Writing to his father, Mr. A. E. Hare, of NO, Lovely-lane,' Bombardier Hare, who is on active service, says :—" We encamped about four miles from Salonika and were nearly washed away, for it rained for about a week. We are quite unaccustomed to this, and did not relish it. On November 7th we entrained for Serbia, and after a four hours' train journey detrained at . We encamped for one day about five ' miles from this village, and next time a littl, behind the fighting line at .that time occupied by the French. From here one could hear the gunfire and see quite plainly the shells bursting. In due course we took a portion of the front from the French. After a time we got com- fOrtably settled in houses, just in time to escape a stretch of very cold weather. Fighting took place irregularly until December 7th, when we were compelled to retreat to Salonika. We ac- complished this by road, during which we had a rough time."</p>
        <p>Bombardier Hare. who is 20 years of age, was formerly employed in the Erasmic Department of Messrs. Crosfield's Works, and was the first to enlist in the office. He is now acting as brigade, clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>CROSS OF GLORY.</head>
        <p>THE CROSS OF GLORY.</p>
        <p>patient Of The!wall Heys Hospital Has</p>
        <p>Coveted Honour.</p>
        <p>Inquiries solicited by our representative to the Matron. of the above institution proved that Sapper Ford, of the Royal Engineers, was en- gaged in assisting the staff with the decorations at the Parr Hall for their whist drive and dance to be held there the self-same evening. On reaching one's final destination one had not much difficulty in being confronted with this gallant hero. Sparse of frame and ruddy of complexion, one would think from his accent that he was a Scotsman, yet the information was vouchsafed us that he was born in Cairo, Egypt. Pinned on his light grey uniform was a medal strikingly artistic and of value. Made of gold, it is in the shape of a Maltese Cross, with two sabres. The ribbon is red and green striped silk, with a tiny star of 'gold attached. Helped to Blow up a German Factory.</p>
        <p>"The medal which you see pinned on my breast was presented to me by President Poincare and General Joffre, both of whom complimented me on an action of great heroism, as they termed it, but to my mind there are thousands on the battlefields who perform actions equalli as great as mine, but who are not -so fortunate. Of my own personal action I do not wish to go into detail except to remark that it was earned in helping to blow up a Ger- man factory. There were close on 250 men in my battalion, but only five came out safe, and I was one of the five. I have a great hatred for the despicable German race, for, believe me. with my own eyes have I seen our men tortured to death, and we have been unable to render assistance. Men such as I, who have been en- gaged on explosion work, they would literally tear to pieces, for when once we engage on tt task of this description we don't merely ' re- move ' one or two of them." Hereabouts our representative remarked how well he looked. "Yes," replied Sapper Ford, "looks can belie. See, you, the back of my tongue. lt is half withered' 'away by the poisonous fumes of German gas, that's what we have had to contend with."</p>
        <p>So one left him, only a few hours later to again renew acquaintances at the whist • drive and dance at which he had worked so ener- getically a few hours earlier in the day. From his laughter and gaiety one saw that at least he had for the moment forgotten the horrors of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>BUFFALOES.</head>
        <p>BUFFALOES.</p>
        <p>Splendid Success Of 'Whist Drive And Ball In Aid 01 Charity.</p>
        <p>The efforts of the Friendly Societies on be- half of charity have in the past been crowned with success, but none more so than those of the energetic workers who organised the whist drive and ball which took place in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening, in aid of the Widows' and Orphans' Fund and Warrington Infirmary. There was a gathering numbering upwards of 350, all of whom spent a highly delightful time. The affair was original, inas- much as those who did not prefer to spend the whole evening at dancing were asked to parti- cipate in 24 hands of whist, the tables being set in one of the corridors. Dancing held pre- mier place in choice, but fully 100 sat down at the tables, where keen competition for many handsome prizes held sway. The successful participants were as follows:— Ladies.-1, Mrs. E. Warburton; 2, Mrs. F. Starkey; 3, Mrs. C. Cotterell; 4, Miss M. Povey; 5, Mrs. Caldwell. _</p>
        <p>Hidden Prize-winner.—Mrs. Craythorn. Genttemen.-1, Mr. • Maybury; .2, Regi- mental-Sergeant-Major Caple; 3, Private S. Fern; 4, Mr. W. Chadwick; 5, Mr. J. Reid. Hidden Prize-winner.—Mr. H. Moss.</p>
        <p>The able M.C.'s for this part of the pro- gramme were Rtvimental-Sergeant-Major Caple and Messrs. J. Wolfenden and F. Starkey.</p>
        <p>The band of the R.A.0.8. was in attendance for dancing and played special selections, under the talented conductorship of Mr. It. Smith. A large number of the whist players, after the conclusion of play, made their way into the spacious 'hall. The M.C.'s for dancing were Messrs. J. Wilkinson', EL Pemberton, A. Hill ,and, A, Gni*. During the latter part of the „evening the pries were distributed to the suc- 'ceisful- whist- plaArs by Mrs. P. Starkey. Mr. J. Holden proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Starkey, who had so graciously presented the prizes, and also to the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) for his presence there that even- ing, which was seconded by Mr. A. Dutton. In a brief speech in reply, the Mayor said he was delighted at any time to _help in.xnak- ing a success of any function on behalf of charity. He was very pleased indeed with the work of the R.A.0.8. in Warrington, who, he said, were always to the fore in worthy ob- jects for their more - unfortunee brethren. (Applause.) It reflected great ,cr•edit on those who had given their efforts so spontaneously. The following the list of workers who helped to organise this most successful whist drive and ball :—Chairman .of committee, Mr. S. Eqggle; secretary, Mr. A. Roberts; joint secretary, Mr. J. Kennewell; treasurer, Mr. A. Dutton; committee, Messrs. 3. Wilkinson, J. Harrison, 'T. Davies, R. Pemberton. J. Holden, J. Wolfenden, IL Willis, P. Knight, G. Ham- blett and Regitnental-Sergeant-Major Caple. At the close the National Anthem was sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>IN BULGARIA.</head>
        <p>IN BULGARIA.</p>
        <p>Three Local Corporals Captured, But</p>
        <p>Safe And Well:</p>
        <p>Readers of "The Examiner" will be pleased to know that authentic news has been received in Warrington testifying that Corporal John Hallwood, the son of Mr. Alfred Hallwood, of Orford; Corporal Gi/lies, who along with Cor- poral Hallwood, was employed at the Warring- ton Savings Bank before the war; and Corporal Grange Yarwood, formerly of Cobbes' Farm, Appleton, are all well and uninjured. About three weeks ago we announced that the two first- named Corporals had fallen into the hands of the Bulgarians, and some anxiety was felt as to their, fate. But we are now informed that the three men are interned at Tatar-BarArdjik, Bulgaria. -</p>
        <p>Iti4rivate life these three yonng men were great friends and well-known in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>COUNCILLOR WILKINSON'S SILVER WEDDING.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR WILKINSON'S SILVER WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Liberal Club Presentation. '</p>
        <p>Cetnicillor John and Mrs. Wilkinson, of Lodge- lamo, have just celebrated their silver wedding, and int order fittingly to recognise the event their friends at the Liberal 'OA gathered in force on Saturday evening in the "Guinea Room" at the Liberal Club to) present the worthy couple with a token of the* respect in the shape of a silver rose bowl. Councillor Wilkinson, besides being chairman; of the</p>
        <p>Finance Committee of the Corporation, is the _ chairman of the Liberal Club and a Nfire-prtsi- dent of the Liberal Association, a comaination lof offices which reveals two important phases of his many public activities ,and worth. ; Complimentary and congratulatory speeches were made by some of the Councillor's oldest friends, light refreshments were served, and the ladies ,and gentlemen who had not taken a prominent part in the presentation, afterwards embraced the opportunity of privately congratu- lating Mr. and Mrs. Wilkinson upon the happy experience through which they were passing. The presentation was made by Alderman 'TM- nion, and amongst those prenent were Mrs. Tinnion, Councillor Lindley, Councillor and Mrs. Locker, Mr. and Mrs. T. Pemberton, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Wilkinson, Mrs. Cowden, Mr. and Mrs. P. H. Brown, Mr. and Mrs. A. Israel, Mr. G. S. Pendlebury, Mr. and Mrs. Critchley, Mr. Fred Walker. and Mr. W. Bibby. An Unequalled Comradeship.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lindley, in an introductory speech, explained that they had come together for the very pleasant purpose of paying a tribute to the worth of two friends who had happily reached n very interesting stage in life's iour- ney. (Applause.) Alderman Tinnion, in making the presenta- tion, said they were met to take part in what was a very happy function, i.e., the celebration of the siker wedding of Councillor and Mrs. John Wilkinson. Such events were landmarks in life, and were worth thinking about, worth looking after, and worth perpetuating. He thought Councillor Wilkinson—if he might say so—was exceedingly fortunate in the partner he had had during the last 25 years. Providence had not been kinder to anyone. He had health, comfort—he would not say wealth, but he cer- tainly had enough—a good wife, a family of which he need be proud, and almost everything that the heart of man could desire. They knew all this, and appreciated very much their ability to congratulate their two friends on this happy position. They prided themselves in that room that they had' a comradeship which could not be equalled, not only in the town, but in the world. It was partly because of this feeling that they were there to celebrate that anniver- sary. On behalf of the lady friends and the members, be had much pleasure in presenting a rose bowl to Councillor and Mrs. Wilkinson, and in doing, so he hoped that whatever their joys and whatever their wishes, they would always look back upon that night with pleasure. He sincerely hoped that their wishes would be realised, and that their future would indeed be bright. (Applause.) In a few well-chosen phrases, Mr. W. Bibby endorsed everything which had been said bv Alderman Tinnion. He sincerely hoped Coun- cillor and Mrs. Wilkinson would be as hap,..)y in the future as they had been in the past. Councillor Locker said his family lied teen associated with Councillor Wilkinson's family for many years. He had the sincerest desire that Mr. and Mrs. Wilkinson would enjoy tFe remaining days of their life in continued happi- ness and comfort. "A Brotherhood."</p>
        <p>Replying to the eulogies of his friends, and thanking them for their gift, Councillor Wil- kinson said that for many years he had been engaged in taking part in similar functions, but he had never thought of the feelings of the re- cipient of the present. He could assure them he would sooner help to take part in a presen- tatiOn than receive a gift. It was, however, ex- ceedingly pleasing to him and to Mrs. Wilkinson to hear the remarks of their friend, Alderman Tinnien; he was always their sheet anchor in times. of trouble.—(Alderman Tinnion: You're not in trouble now.) (Laughter.)—There was a comradeship amongst the members, but he would go further and say there was a brother- hood amongst them. He believed if any mem- ber of .that room were in .the slightest trouble, the other members would rally rounrhim. He thanked them for their kindness towards them, and assured them he would hand the rose bowl down as an heirloom in his family. (Applause.)- The inscription on the rose bowl which was supplied by Messrs. Eustance and Co., was : " Presented.to Councillor and Mrs. John Wil- kinson an the occasion of their silver wedding by his friends at the Liberal Club. 26th Decem- ber. 1915." ,</p>
        <p>The wedding took place at St. Paul's Church. Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>DRILLING THE ATTESTED.</head>
        <p>DRILLING THE ATTESTED.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Training Corps To Teach Men In AA;lalting.</p>
        <p>As in other towns, the officers and N.C.O.'s connected with the Volunteer Regiments are anxious to drill all men who have been attested, so that when called up they will be fit for service more readily. Experience has shown that many advantages accrue from this, one of which is that their muscles become more supple and the uncomfortable stiffness of the early stages on the barrack's square need not be feared—a- not un- important -detail to those who go through it. The willingness of the local volunteers to train attested men was notified to the borough by the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) at the Town Council Meeting on Tuesday, when ho read the following letter from the secretaries of the South-West Lancashire Volunteer Regi- ment :</p>
        <p>"At a meeting of the committee of the War- rington Company of the South-West Lancashire Volunteer Regiment, it was unanimously decidea to appeal again to you for the benefit of your influence, and that of the Town Council, with the men of Warrington and district on behalf of this movement. -</p>
        <p>"The War Office has approved of the offer of the Central Association of Volunteer Training Corps, on behalf of affiliated corps, to give pre- liminary training to men who have attested in Section B Army Reserve, and this approval has also been endorsed by Lord Derby. A number of these men are training with the Warrington Company and the Sankey. and Penketh Detach- ments, but it is felt that this number should be very considerably increased if the importance of acquiring the rudiments of drill, musketry, field training, Lc., were brought more prominently before the men affected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Appeal To " Starred " Men.</head>
        <p>Appeal To " Starred " Men.</p>
        <p>"Our committee especially wish' to impress upon those attested men who are hopeful of deferment or exemption, the urgent, duty which devolves upon them of becoming fit for home service, and this they have now an opportunity of doing in their spare time by joining the Training Corps in Warrington and district. Take the case of 'starred men who have at- tested. For what have they attested? Surely they shOuld all do what they can, and we suggest that every man who is not too old or whose time is not fully occupied with munition work, should make himself fit for service with the colours or at home.</p>
        <p>"We linow theew hpre your entire sym- pathy with us in the Volunteer movement, and hope that your Council will help us to bring home to the men of this district their bounden duty to their King and country." The Mayor added that he hoped attested men would respond to the invitation. It was also desirable that the few men who had not attested in Warrington should do so immediately.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>2,720 CONTROLLED WORKS.</head>
        <p>2,720 CONTROLLED WORKS.</p>
        <p>The Minister of MunitiOns announces that he has made an order under section 4 of the Muni- tions of War Act, 1915, declaring 298 additional establishments as controlled establishments under the Act as from January 31.</p>
        <p>• A total of 2,720 establishments have now been declared as controlled under the Act from the date of the first order, July 12, 1915. to January 31, 1916, inclusive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
