        <p>Private Henry Brookes Promoted.—Mrs James Taylor, of West Hyde, Newfields, has this week received news irQm her son, Private Henry Brookes, of the 115th Cheshire Regiment, that he has been promoted to the rank of Corporal. The new corporal is a member of the patriotic family whose history we gave in our last issue. Clean Canal Boats.—Mr. D. Mort's annual report on the results of his inspection of the canal boats states that during the year he in- spected 42. These contained registered cabin Space for 142 adults, and were occupied by 47 men, 18 women and 25 children. The boats generally were clean and in good repair. In only one case was a cabin dirty.</p>
        <p>British Red Cross Hospital.—On Thursday afternoon a draft of about 20 patients were -received into the British Red Cross Hospital f rem the Lord Derby Hospital, Winwick. The hospital had been closed for the last three weeks for cleaning purposes, and to give the staff a well-earned rest.</p>
        <p>Oughtrington Social Club.—A veil'y successful whist drive was held last night at the Ought- rington School, the function being organised by the members of the Working lien's Club, Sandy- lane, Lymm. There was a large assembly, and capital prizes were awarded. The whole of the proceeds were in aid of the local Red Cross Hospital.</p>
        <p>I',</p>
        <p>-1:4 4</p>
        <p>ii-5..',5,--'.- i i :--;;:. .t.:-,11,:,..,- ...i .•. 4: t'1,..:;::.. r*,..:..i,.,,.,..,.. p„,:,..,,,..".11..... rcii':-.77., .i -sr,i</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>At The Council Meeting.—The members pre- sent at the Council meeting on Monday evening were Messrs. R. H. Gotobed (in the chair), C. E. Cowper W. G. Groves, F. Lockyer, G. H. Parker, F. Ingham and A. Mclntyre. The officials present were Messrs. W. MuHard (clerk), Dr. L. Thorp (deputy medical officer), C. Barnes (water engineer), T. West (gas manager) and D. Mort (sanitary inspector).</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal D. Kirkpatrick Now Con- valescent.—Lance-Corporal D. Kirkpatrick, whom we some time ago reported to be suffer- ing from jaundice, has this week written to his mother, Mrs. W. Kirkpatrick, of the Bridge- water Arms, stating that he is now convalescent. He is at present an inmate of the 17th General Hospital, Alexandria, Egypt. He was attached to the Royal Engineers. 2nd East Lancashire Regiment. His brother, Private Thomas Kirk- patrick, is at present in Fiance, and in the best of health.</p>
        <p>Private Lester Sturton Recovering.—Private Lester Sturton, of the 2nd Loyal North Lanca- shire Regiment. *ho is at present an inmate of the Military Hospital, Wye, Kent, has this week communicated with tone of his Lymm friends, conveying tho news that he is recover- ing from the effects of septic poisoning. He is expecting to journey home for a short furlough within the next month, as the doctor says that by then he will have fully recovered. Private Sturton pontracted septic poisoning thrcugh drinking out of a dirty vessel.</p>
        <p>Narrow Escape from Drowning.—A Lymm youth had k very narrow escape from drowning on Saturday morning, whilst fishing in the Bridgewater Canal, near Lymna Bridge. His name is Alfred ' Forsyth, and resides lat the Milestone Hotel. The youth is subject o lits, an I it seems that 'whilst particiomng in lie sport he was attacked by one; with the result that he fell into the canal. The shock of tpe immer- sion seems to have brought him round, and, being a -good swimmer, he managed to keep afloat. He was eventually rescued by Mr. Jas. Cowsill. Fortunately, Forsyth is none the worse for his experience.' Y.M.C.A. Huts.—An auction sale on behalf of the local funds for, the Y.M.C.A. movement is to be held in the Drill Hall on Thursday; when a miscellaneous list of goods, consisting of animals, produce, furniture, plants, poultry .land clothing will be disposed of. The auctioneers are Mr. Mainwaring; of Warrington, and Mr. Banks, of Manchester. The opening ceremony is to,be performed by Mrs. T. J. Ridgway, after which an entertainment will be given; the ad- mission to the sale and entertainment being 6d. each. Already up to date the receipts and dona- tions total over £lOO, subscriptions ranging frcim a' few coppers to £2ll Any further donations or articles may be sent to the hon. treasurer, Mr.. T. F. W. Dixon.</p>
        <p>'Social And Presentation.—On Thursday even- ing the .Congregational Church Choir held a social, when an enjoyable evening was spent. Tea was provided in the Schoolroom, and about members of the choir and friends attended. *After the repast an entertainment was given, the chair being occupied by the Pastor, the Rev.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>'I</p>
        <p>Shields Gill. - Several appropriate songs were flung by the Misses Jolley, Appleton. Mrs. Lewis and Messrs. Hewitt and Walker. Dr. Seurrah</p>
