      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>SUNDAY TRADING.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY TRADING.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>am surprised at your correspondent "Well-wisher." His argument is empty and childish. He will not do much for his King and country feeding them on pop and toffee. This war began through selfishness and greed. Sunday trading is carried on through the same. The corner of the village is a disgracb to it on Sunday evenings, as many know who have to•go to the post. Some rude remark shouted •While they are passing, and the three principal things which all the shops sell are tobacco, sweets and minerals. All could be purchased on Saturdays without, any hurt to 'any. of them. You must not expect much help for King and country from people who do not try to make the village purer by example at home. It is not helping if a man wcirks his factory or him- self seven days a week, and I would ask the writer to try and leave the village better than he found it, not worse, by Sunday trading. '</p>
        <p>ABETTER WISHER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>BELGIANS..</head>
        <p>THE BELGIANS..</p>
        <p>Serious Allegation Made At Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>A serious complaint was made . against the Belgians domiciled at Oughtringt4.n, at the Lymm Council meeting on Monday. Councillor Cowper said that considering the amount of illness they had had in the district lately, the way in which Belgians at Ought- rington used their rooms and the manner in which they threw things out of the window was disgraceful. He was told the stench was some- thing cruel, and he thought the 'Council ought to write and tell them to keep things in proper order. He did not think they ought to come into the country and carry on in the way they had done.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: (Coun. R. Gotobed) : I think we may take it the matter is being dealt with, and that measures will be taken for the provision of proper receptacles. Councillor Cowper added he 11i been ast-g•;! to bring the matter before the Coancil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>"NOT MUDDLED."</head>
        <p>"NOT MUDDLED."</p>
        <p>Council Chairman's Reply To Assertions Re Finance.</p>
        <p>The Council's financial affairs are causing Councillor *Cowper some concern. At Monday's Council meeting he declared there ought to be a committee appointed to go into the matter, adding that if the Council were insolvent, the members ought -to know. ,abont,it.. -,There seemed to be a loss nu.the gas and water and everything else, and he certainly.-wou4l pot sign any more cheques. . . Coun. R.- H. Gotobed (the chairman): I don't see what' you .are driving. at. You are on the Finance Committee, and you are at. liberty to go into these matters and to ask questions. Councillor Cowper: [will do so. The Chairman: That is the proper course to adopt Any member .is allowed to ask any question he likes or to go and see the books to get any information he likes.</p>
        <p>Councillor Ingham: I think,the books are in perfect order, and if there is anything Mr. Cowper doesn't understand, the Clerk will ex- plain it to him. ' • . The Chairman: In addition to that; we have just had the auditor's report. I will , admit money is tight, hut* .I must take exception to the statement that the Council's finances are in a state of muddle.</p>
        <p>The matter was then allowed to drop. • It will be recollected that at the last meeting of the council Councillor Cowper- said that the Council seemed to be in a muddle with regard to financial matters, and he suggested that an Advisory Committee should be formed: to make inquiries and to formulate suggestions.—The Chairman replied: I bare suggested that we should have a sub-committee to consider the question of finance. ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>SERGE \NT T. W. FIELD.</head>
        <p>SERGE \NT T. W. FIELD.</p>
        <p>The Long-Waited-For c'honour Received At Last.</p>
        <p>In the-official honours published on Thursday is included the name of Sergeant T. W. Field, of the 1/7th Battalion Lancashire Fusiliers, he having been awarded the Distinguished Conduct Medal. Up to the present it has not been stated as to how the popular Lyinm soldier earned it, but we presume it is concerned with his meritorious service in Turkey, which has been mentioned from time to time in "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>We reprint his own words, as quoted in a letter to his wife, written whilst on his way home. "You will know by now that I have had four shrapnel wounds off a Turkish shell; and one was in my ankle, one in my hip, one -in my thumb and one in my ribs— all on the left side. All except the one in my .hip-bone are now healed up, and I write you this note sat up in my cot on the high seas close to Malta. I. was in a, charge in Turkey with my boys, and the Turks met us and we had a ter- rible fight. "But we got their trenches off them; and then they cut us off, and I was three days and three nights, fighting all the time until we were relieved. Lam Mentioned in dispatches." .Sergeant Field is well-knolin to " Examiner " readers as a competent correspondent, his many interesting letters and articles having, enlivened our columns on numerous occasions.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Field, who is a married man with three children and lives at Newfield, Lymm, is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>SERGEANT T. W. FIELD. D.C.M</head>
        <p>SERGEANT T. W. FIELD. D.C.M</p>
        <p>33 years of age, and carried on business in Man- chester. Being a time-expired Territorial, he volunteered for service with his old Battalion, the Lancashire Fusiliers (Territorials) and was drafted out to Egypt, eventually reaching the Dardanelles in May Last. He left there August 20th. He is well-known in sporting circles as a cricketer, and was captain of his regimental team while in Egypt. The popular Sergeant is at present an in. mate of the Heaton Park Convalescent Hos- pital, near Manchester, where he has been for some little time, suffering from nervous break- down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>D.S.O.</head>
        <p>D.S.O.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Alexander Wins Coveted Honour.</p>
        <p>In the official list of honours published this week, is included the name of Lieut.-Colonel Charles Taylor Alexander, a popular Lymm officer, who is attached to the 1/7th Lancashire Fusiliers.</p>
        <p>Up to the present no news has been forth- coming as to how the coveted honour has been won. The Lieutenant-Colonel resides at " Rossendale," Statham, and is highly esteemed and admired in the village, taking more than ordinary interest in the affairs of Lymm and the surrounding districts. Ire is the treasurer of the local Golf Club, and is a familiar figure on the links. ,Prior to the war Lieut.-Colonel Alexander was attached to the local "Terriers; eventually joining the Lancashire Fusiliers, and was drafted to Egypt in September of 1914. Un- fortunately, he contracted enteric fever a short</p>
        <p>time ago, from which he has not fully re- covered. At present he is an inmate of No. 1 Military Hospital, St. Gable's College, Camber- well. His wife left the village on Thursday on visit to her husband.</p>
        <p>:The above award, together with the D.C.M. for Sergeant T. W. Field, who was in the same regiment, naturally created unbounded enthusiasm in the village when the news be- came known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>GAS WORKS' COAL.</head>
        <p>GAS WORKS' COAL.</p>
        <p>Manager Has Difficulty In Keeping Up</p>
        <p>Supplies.</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the COuncil's Gas Com- mittee, it was reported that owing to the mer- chants being unable to supply coal according to contract, it was decided to approve of the action of the .manager in .purchasing coal elsewhere. The manager was also authorised to make pur- chases on similar lines.</p>
        <p>At Monday evening's meeting of the Council, Councillor CoAper asked if the words "more or less" were inserted in the contracts for the sApply of coal.</p>
        <p>'kilo" Chairman (Coun. Gotobed): Previous to this year we have always had, to state "more or less," but that is not so this year. Councillor Cowper: I am, delighted to hear it, for though I am chairman of the Gas Com- mittee, I don't know anything about it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Y.M.C.A. WORK.</head>
        <p>Y.M.C.A. WORK.</p>
        <p>Glowing Eulogy By Lymm Man</p>
        <p>Who Knows-.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—l hear that a committee has been formed in Lymm for the purpose of raising funds to provide a hut for the Y.M.C.A. May I, sir, through the medium of your valu- able paper, be allowed to support the appeals that the committee will no doubt be making? During the autumn of 1914 I volunteered to give my services to the Association for a few weeks, and it will indicate my idea of the im- portance of the work when I inform you that I am still engaged in the tasks of the movement. As am unsectarian and -non-political organisa- tion, it is best fitted to cater for the religious and social needs of all men, and it is in the Y.M.C.A. hut, hall, or marquee that one finds such a delightful blending of these .require- ments. Just as the Sunday services and nightly hymn, prayer, .and in many instances short address, are regular features in the Associa- tion's buildings, so are the concerts, games and competitions. __</p>
        <p>My memory is filled with many scenes of both orders. Amongst the reminiscences are-those of a certain hut crowded to the overflowing with men bowed in prayer, who, later in the service, were heartily singing such hymns as "Abide with me" and "Stand up, stand up, for Jesus." I have seen the same hut packed with brave fellows, who, within an hour afterwards have been on their way to the firing line, enthusias- tically demanding encores from a concert party. Why, even •as I write, the troops are enjoying a visit from one of our concert parties, and so far all the soloists have had to sing three or more songs in succession before being allowed to resume their seats.</p>
        <p>Another feature of Our work is the provision of writing materials and facilities for using same. Last summer in a camp of six hundred and fifty men, nearly 4,000 letters and parcels were posted at my pillar box, a daily average of 380 !—a fact which helps one to realise why the weekly stationery bill of the Association is prac- tically 6C1,000.. Stamps and postal orders were sold in abund- ance. and.many a home has been enriched as a, result of remittances sent because the Y.M.C.A. postal department was on the spot when the men .received their pay. :Many centres look after the washing of `!Tommy's" linen, and he</p>
        <p>has found the Y.M.C.A. a blessing in that re- spect. Libraries and a supply of papers and magazines find ample scope for those men who have a literary mind; whilst debates, often most instructive, and yet amusing, provide a similar field for the orators of the camps. Tug-of-war contests, " cock " fighting„ pillow fighting, &amp;c., raise the men to the highest pitch of entluisiasm and make .them forgiA the toils and worries of the day.</p>
        <p>Colonel Groves and Major G. C. Hamilton, M.P. Committee (to supply vacancies) : Messrs. Joseph Price, W. Thomson, R. Oliver, N. Worthington, J. E. Slee and T. W. Dutton; hon. secretary, Mr. Callender; and hon. trea- surer, Mr. Fred E: Evans. Mr. Coxon and Mr. Oliver were detailed to -represent the association on the Council of the Central Association at -Altrincham, with the President and Hon. Secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>" Home" at the Front.</head>
        <p>" Home" at the Front.</p>
        <p>The counter at which is provided an abund- ant and cheap supply of refreshments and almost every other requisite " Tommy " needs, is a- most popular feature, and to a large extent helps to reduce the deficit of the Association's funds. French classes, cinema shows and sing-songs by no 'means complete the list of the benefits the Y.M.C.A. gives the troops. Does " Tommy " appreciate the Y.M.C.A.? Ask him! The letters I have received and the Many expressions of gratitude I have had to listen to mean only one answer—Yes! "I ,can never express to you my. appreciation of your efforts," "Without the .Y.M.C.A. we should have been lost," and "1 .don't know what we should have done if it had not been for the Y.M.C.A.," are but specimens which I could readily multiply. We had the misfortune to have the large marquee, at which I was leader, destroyed by the great storm of September 29th last. Had the men appreciated our efforts? You should have seen how willingly they .worked in rescu- ing the valuable-stores, in Clearing, away the, debris, and in erecting the new marquee we secured the following day. The gloomy faces and remarks of those " l'onamies" on the. day of the disaster were only aecentuated .by their surprise, and elation .on the -arrival of the new tent. Immediately after the calamity one sol- dier wired to his parents, "Our home is gone from us—blown away 1". One prominent Scotsman has said that the Y.M.C.A. has saved a whole Division of the British troops. Perhaps his eulOgism is too generous, but it cannot be • denied that the Association has been a mdrked benefit to our bra-e fighters in lessening their temptations and in helping to sustain their stamina and optimism. If Lymm people rise to the _occasion and. pro- vide a Y.M.C.A. hut not only will all the sol- diers who visit it be indebted to them, but it will be a further proof of the splendid patriot- ism of the inhabitants.—Yours faithfully. A BERTRAM EDWARDS. January 27th, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>INFECTIOUS DISEASES.</head>
        <p>INFECTIOUS DISEASES.</p>
        <p>Scarlet Fever And. Diphtheria Still</p>
        <p>Prevalent In The District.</p>
        <p>Scarlet fever and diphtheria, are still rampant</p>
        <p>in Lymm. During January twenty eases of the former and ten of the latter have been reported to Dr. Thorp (temporary medical officer of health).</p>
        <p>Dr. Thorp submitted his report at the meet- ing of the District Council on Monday, and in it he stated :</p>
        <p>"During the month 10 cases of diphtheria, 20 cases of scarlet fever, 2 cases- of chicken pox, and 1 case of measles have been reported. On January 10th a case of scarlet fever was re- ported at Beechwood in an English child. A case was also discovered in a Belgian. These cases were removed to the Isolation Hospital. Two further cases were discovered at the Bel- gian Home on January 12th, and the committee .in charge set up an isolation block, and the two former cases from Beechwood were taken back. At present at the Isolation Hospital ,we have 7 eases of diphtheria and 10 cases of scarlet fever, which are being looked after by four nurses. The cases are confined mainly to the Heatley and Oughtrington end of the district. With the exception of one child who was very ill, one case of pneumonia, and one case requiring tracheo- tomy, the cases. have not been of a serious nature, and all are doing satisfactorily. The hospital, however, will not be closed for several weeks, even if no fresh cases are admitted. S.uch a number of cases cause a great amount of. effluent from the hospital, which has necessi- tate&amp; temporary measures with regard to filtra- tion of the same. This matter, no doubt, will receive your consideration when the promised inquiry and alteration of the hospital is taken in hand. Assistance has been obtained to over- come the difficulty caused by the illness of the caretaker, and .the hospital is being conducted to my satisfaction under the circumstances.</p>
        <p>"During the month the Sanitary . Inspector and I made an inspection of the bone works at Statham. We were told that the business was not being carried on at that place after this quarter—a matter of satisfaCtion. • We also in- spected two houses, a report upon which has been submitted by Mr. Mort. One slaughter- house was also inspected and found satisfac- tory."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>25 PER CENT. SERVING'.</head>
        <p>25 PER CENT. SERVING'.</p>
        <p>Conservatives Help To 'Recruit Others</p>
        <p>And Enlist- Themselves.</p>
        <p>In his financial statement at the annual meeting of the Lymm Constitutional and Con- servative Association and Club on Monday, Mr. Fred E. Evans said that considering the serious and critical times through which we are passing, it was almost surprising how well the club finances had been maintained. Fully 2,5 per cent. of the members were serving in some branch of His Majesty's Forces. Their subscriptions for the time being had been waived, which had meant a considerable reduc- tion in revenue. Several members had resigned for reasons of economy. The revenue of the house accounts had fallen heavily, partly owing to the reduced membership and partly because of the licensing, restrictions. Politics were for the time being dead, but the club premises had throughout the year been the centre of activity for those engaged on War work, the assembly and committee rooms hav- ing been placed free of charge at the disposal of the Red Cross Hospital Committee, also the Working Men's Committee in connection with the hospital, the Soldiers' and Sailors"- Fami- lies Association. The recruiting authorities had also found the club eminently suitable and convenient for the attestation and medical examination of recruits. - In the autumn the Parliamentary Recruiting Committee ap- proached the agents of both political parties with a view to organising and carrying out the canvass of the eligible men under Lord Derby's scheme. A strong committee was formed under the presidency of Mr. Alfred Watkin, with Mr. Callender and Mr. Reginald Barker as joint hon. secretaries. Mr. Callen- der's health failed him at this juncture, but the secretarial work was carried out by Mr. Barker, the Liberal secretary, who had the willing assistance of the members of this asso- ciation whenever he cared to call for it. - Mr. Rylands On The Twenty-twb.</p>
        <p>Yri his presidential address, Mr. W. Peter Itylands -remarked that there was ,nothing he desired to say on ,the subject of politics, but they could all turn their thoughts to the war and to the grounds of hope for the future. In Gallipoli failure might have resulted from hesitation, from not doing the right thing at the right .Moment, and if one was permitted -to criticise the Government, they .had Shown a lack of 'grit and determination—always waiting .to see what the other side were going to do, for something to turn up before forming a plan. In war, success came to -those who made tip their minds first,-and they had had a pain- ful feeling during the past twelve months that ,there had been a great deal lacking in the British conduct of the campaign; and he attributed it to the responsibility being placed in the hands of such a large body as 22 men, of whom hardly any had the slightest qualifi- cation for the position. The people of the country would have felt happier if they had felt that the men at home responsible for the conduct of the campaign had been trained men in the art of war. As a:set-off against the re- verses of the past year we had several things to comfort us. The first was our Navy. Noth- ing had happened to shake in the least degree our confidence in-it, and its ability to protect our shores. Throughout the War the Navy had never failed us or muddled. As long as our Navy served us as she had done we could not be defeated.' (Applause.) On the proposition of Mr. Itylands the con- gratulations of the association and club were sent to Colonel Groves on the distinction which had been conferred upon him in having been appointed C.M.G. The Hon. J. E. Cross seconded, remarking that he hoped the Colonel would live through the war, and afterwards have a long and happy. life.</p>
        <p>The following appointments were made : Viee7presidents: The Hon. J. E. Cross. Mr. W. Rattersby. I%jr. Coningsby Disraeli, Mr. Bury, Coxog, Mr. G. P. Del-h-tirst. Viscount Bury,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Burn Your Refuse I—The Sanitary Committee cif the Parish Council are experiencing the greatest difficulty in disposing of household refuse, and have issued a leaflet urging residents to burn as much refuse as possible. Taxi-driver Fined.-2-At the Warrington Police Court on Th.ursday, William John Richardson', a taxi-driver, of 4, Kimberley-drive, Stockton Heath, was alleged to have driven a motor car along Bridge-street, Warrington, at 20 miles an hour on January 16th. Though two police officers swore his car was travelling at. the rate of 20 miles- an hour, Richardson said it had taken him 15 Minutes to travel. from Hill Clifie Chapel to Warrington, so his speed could not have been more than 12 miles an hour.- Fine : 10s.</p>
        <p>Doll Nig ht.—One of the special meetings of St. 'Thomas' Band of Hope is Doll Night.. This was held. on Thursday evening, when a large number of girls with dolls were accommodated with seats_ on the platform, so that all could be seen nursing their favourites. There was a good attendance of parents and children. An excel- lent address was given by the Rev. the Hon. C. F. Cross, rural dean. The following took an active part in a most enjoyable meeting: Mrs. Knight, Miss Potts, Miss Hudson, Misses E. and S. Wheeler, Messrs. C. Hallows, Stannard, Hough; and Manuel. Each child received an orange on leaving with her doll. Sunday .School Teachers' Effort—The Church Room was crowded on Monday evening, when a concert was held on behalf of elle Prince of Wales'. Fund. The ,programme was arranged by the Sunday school teachers connected with St. Thomas' Church, and proved to be very pleasing. It was made :up of action songs by the Sunday school scholars, gongs• by Miss Blackburn, Miss Berry, Miss Capel, and •humorous songs by Mr. Arthur Moores and, Mr. Sutton. The Misses Lee, Mr. Lee, Mr. Sutton and Mr. Pearson took part in a sketch written by Mr. F. Pearson. The Vicar (Rev. James Collins) who presided, moved .a vote of thanks to' the artistes. , This was seconded by Mr. Wm. Mclntyre, and carried with applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>• TO BE HELD AFTER ALL. Garden Society's Show On August</head>
        <p>• TO BE HELD AFTER ALL. Garden Society's Show On August</p>
        <p>Monday.</p>
        <p>At one time it seemed very probable that the annual flower show of the Stockton Heath Garden Society would not be held this year. but chiefly owing to the determination Mr E. G. White (the hon. secretary), August Baitk Holiday has been fixed.</p>
        <p>_The -General Committee of the Society had considered the matter and, when the tnnual meeting of the members was held in the Church Rooms last week, recommended that owing to the necessity of curtailing . expmi- ture, the usual show should Let be held in but that the outside competitions should be held —though on a modified scale. The Generhl Committee had referred the matter to the Schedule Committee, who had drawn up the schedule of sixteen classes of 'competition. These classes included displays of sweet peas, carnations, violas and mixed flowers, suitable for cutting.</p>
        <p>At Friday evening's meeting the adoption of this recommendation twas moved by Mr. W. H. Woodcock (chairman of the committee) and seconded by Mr. J. 11. Clarke.</p>
        <p>The Secretary (Mr. White) said he was sorry he differed from the oommittee, but as he already' had preferred his resignation he claimed the right to speak as any other member of .the Society. lie moved as an amendment that the committee's recommendation re , the summer show be not accepted, and that it be an , in- struction to the committee to arrange a show on Bank Holiday, commensurate with the situation created by the war, due regard beim°. paid to the economic position in framing the sciledule. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>This course was seconded by Mr. J. Owen, and carried by a large majority.</p>
        <p>£54 In Hand.</p>
        <p>During the meeting—which was presided over by Sir Gilbert Greene.ll—Mr. White submitted his annual report, saying it must necessarily be briefer than many of its predecessors, fqr the entire activity of the Society last year was con- fined to the summer 'show in August. After an interesting account of the event, the report con- cluded :</p>
        <p>"The treasurer's accounts have again been audited by Mr. L. A. Voisey, and the committee asked 'to accord to him their thanks for his services, so readily and ably rendered. There is, it may be noted, 25-1 in hand. Whatever work lies in front of the Society ,during the coming year, it is hoped that the enthusiasm of the members will be equal to the calls upon them. There is still much work to be done, and only, by learning lessons by past experience and keep- ing the object of the Society ever in view will permanent success and satisfaction be attained." The report was unanimously adopted. - Submitting the balance Sheet, Mr. J. Holt (the hon. treasurer) said "I should like to say it goes against the grain to report a loss on the year's working of the Society. At the same time, when we take into consideration what the Society has had to face duriiiw the past year—firstly, the great national struggle our country is passing through—we cannot but expect it to have its \bearing on the Stockton Heath Garden Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Reduced Subscriptions.</head>
        <p>Reduced Subscriptions.</p>
        <p>"Our members, all desirous of doing their little share during the past year, found they ..must reduce their subscriptions owing to so ..many' demands from various institutions'-,-so much So that the Society suffers to the extent of .£lO.-19s. 6d., coupled with this is donations 35., making a total of £l6 „ 13s. 6d. Then again, the August show; to make this a sue t7e always look forward to having a fine .;ay. You will agree with me that we were not so ravcur- ably dealt with this last year—one reafty wrn- dered if we should be able to have an cpening owing to the rain. I can assure you it made us feel in the dumps on looking over the takings up to three o'clock, not having reached double figures. However, under the conditions I think we came .out „ wonderfully well, although the takings dropped £l7 odd less than 1914. Had we had a fine day I have no hesitation in saying the show -would have been a record for attend- ance. But, looking from the various stand- points, the members have every reason to be thankful for the present position we hold, with balance to carry forward of £54. 9s. 7d., knowing that every care has been taken to work the Society as economical as possible." , . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Officials</head>
        <p>The Officials</p>
        <p>The election of officers resujted as follows President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, .Bart, C.V.0., Walton ; Vice-presidents, Rev. James Col- lins, Messrs. IL R. .Armitage, W. Bainbridge, T. Baxter, J. H. Clarke, Leonard G. Davies, Rev. 11. V. Pigot, Messrs. Vere B. Davies, .B. J. Hall, W. 11. J. Jenkins, ..Roger C. Parr, H. J. West- brook and A. J. Willett; committee, 1111. W. 11. Woodcock (chairman), Mr.. J. IT. Fletcher (vice- .chairman), Messrs. J. Bennett, D. S. Birrell, J. Blinston, J. Brethertou, F. Broady, 'J. Colley, J. J. F. Ferguson, W. Hankey, G. Houghton, W. Howard, J. McDermid, R. Mochrie, .J. Owen, F. A. Rathbone, W. Skelton, F. Timperley, W. A. .Timperley, E. Vernon, W. Best, Cliffe, J. Hallows, W. McKenzie, T. Williams .and W. Yarwood ; hon. auditor, Mr. Louis A. Voisey ; hon. treasurer, Mr. J. Holt; hon. secretary, Mr. E. G. White. /.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>StOckton Heath Detachnient.</p>
        <p>Orders for week cominencing,. 7th February : Monda. Drill at, Headquarters. Fall-in 7.45 sharp: Thursday. Drill at Headquarters. Fall-in, 7.45 sharp. Saturday: UniformlPArade. Fall-in at Victoria Square 3 o'clock sharp. N.B.—Members are requested to note that there is. a Uniform Parade to-day (Saurday, sth- February). Fall-in Victoria Square at 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>By order, F, WARBURTON, Officer Commanding</p>
      </div>
