      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>The Late Captain Owen.—At the Liverpool Consistory Court this week, Chancellor Dowdall granted a faculty for the erection of a brass tablet in St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, to the memory of Captain George Webster Owen, who fell in battle on the Gallipoli Peninsula:</p>
        <p>On Furlough.—The two brew laddies, Fred Hall and Richard Gregson, looking the picture of health and fitness, have just returned to their training quarters after a short furlough. More than one grouped man has cast an envious eye upon the wearers of the .smart Highland uniform.</p>
        <p>Lance-C o rpora I C arrington.—Lance-Corpora 1 Arthur Carrington, son of Mr. Tom Carrington, The Park, Penketh, has recently returned to the Front after seven days' leave, the first furlough since his arrival, in France with the Army Service Corps thirteen months ago. His experiences during that time have been of the usual exciting order, with hairbreadth escapes inseparable from the conditions of this war On one occasion his mate next to him was killed, and upon another a shell buried itself into the -ground without exploding, but so close that none would have survived if it had gone off. Attached to the Indian Cavalry, his duties carried him into Oome queer corners, that needed all a man's patience and resource. Like all the men who have seen and know the true condition of things out there, it was extremely difficult to get him to talk about himself, but the air Of determination to see the thing through was apparent above everything else.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>THEL WALL.</head>
        <p>THEL WALL.</p>
        <p>MEMORIALS TO BE PLACED IN</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the vestry of Thelwall Church has decided to accept gratefully the offer Of the Rev. Wilfrid J. Stanton of a new commhnion table and new sedilia in place of those at present in use, and of the widening (by about one foot) of the dangerously narrow altar step, the cost of which improvements the Rev. Wilfrid J. Stanton proposes to defray as a memorial to his mother, the late Mrs. Stanton, of Greenfield.</p>
        <p>The vestry .also • cordially consented to the placing of a. marble memorial tablet on the north wall of the aisle by Mr. Harry Holden, in memory of his son, Second-Lieutenant Legh Holden, who fell in action last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Not True.</head>
        <p>Not True.</p>
        <p>A tramp called at the house of a local celebrity. "I've walked many miles to see you, sir," said the tramp, " because people told me you were very kind- to poor, unfortunate fellows like me." " Indeed!" said the other. "And are you going back the same way?" "Yes. sir," was the answer. "Well. just contradict that rumour as you go, will you? Good morning!"</p>
