      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>V.T.C.</head>
        <p>THE V.T.C.</p>
        <p>Hold Church Parade And Smoking</p>
        <p>Concert.</p>
        <p>The members of the Grappenhall detachment of the V.T.C. held their' first church parade on Sunday last, when they attended the morning service. They assembled at Milling's Corner, and marched to Lumbrook, where they met the Stockton Heath detachment, who had been in- vited to attend the service. From. there the combined detachments marched to the church, where the service was conducted by the Rector (the Rev. H. V. Pitot), who- is a member of the Odrps. Mr. Pigot was assisted by the Rev. E. Gunner, and an appropriate and patriotic ser- mon was preached by the Rev. G. C. Briggs (Vicar of Christ Church, Latchford). During the service an intercessory hymn was sung, Miss Norton, a niece of Mr. F. A. Frost, taking the solo. The officers on parade w:ere Platoon-Com- mander F. A. Frost, Drill-Instructor F. Taylor, Section-Commander and Quartermaster-Sergt. F. Read, Section-Commander T. Short, and Sec- tion-Corporals P. Tipping and Trantem. , Platoon-Commander F. Warburton was in charge of the Stockton Heath detachment. • Smoking Concert.</p>
        <p>A smoking concert was held in the Large Room, Grappenliall, the headquarters of the local detachment of the V.T.C. on Tuesday even- ing. Songs were sung by Messrs. W. IL Ticket', R. W. Wilde, G. Ainsworth, Shewbridge, Sea- bury, and Venables. Mr. C. Lambert gave two excellent recitations and also two songs from Albert Chevalier's repertoire. Mr. C. A. Lan- don also recited, and humorous items were pro- vided by Mr. J. Hands. A flute solo was played by Mr. W. TI. Podmore, who also joined Mr. Isherwood (clarionette) in a duet. Another good musical item was the pianoforte solo by Mr. Bradshaw. Messrs. G. Anderton and .T. Carter were efficient accompanists. A gramophone and records were lent by Mr. J. Hogg.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion Mr. F. A. Frost appealed to the visitors, if they were over military age, to become members of the V.T.C., or, if they had attested, to attach themselves to the corps in order to make themselves efficient before jokn- ipg Colours. The concert was such a success that in all probability others will be arranged, and it is suggested that a public concert should be held in the Large Room with the object of increas- ing the funds of the detachment..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment. Orders for week commencing Bth February :</p>
        <p>Monday, 7th February. " Musketry. Meet at the Range at 7.30 p.m. sharp.</p>
        <p>Tuesday, Bth February. Company and Squad Drill. Fall-in at Headquarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp.</p>
        <p>Wednesday, 9th February. Musketry. Match with Stockton Heath Detachment at the Municipal Range, The Baths, Legh-street, commencing at 7. p.m. sharp.</p>
        <p>Friday, 11th February. Musketry. Meet at the Range at 7.30 p sharp.</p>
        <p>Saturday, 12th February. Parade (Uniform) and Squad Drill. Fall- in at Headquarters at 3 p.m. sharp. N.B.—For list of men detailed for Musketry on Monday and Friday, and for the match with the Stockton Heath Detachment on Wed- nesday, see notice ,at Headquarters.</p>
        <p>Orderlies. See. Corn. F. Short. Private W. Tipping.</p>
        <p>By Order,</p>
        <p>F. A. rposT, 0.C., (Platoon Commander).</p>
        <p>Headquarters', sth February, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>STRANGERS SPOIL TOWN'S</p>
        <p>RECORD.</p>
        <p>When a farm labourer of another clime was charged at the Police Court on Monday with being drunk, the Chief Constable had again cause to complain about strangers disgracing the borough. "He is one of an enormous number of strangers we are getting here year by year. It is a disgrace. Whereas only 224 .of our own people got drunk last year 362 strangers over- stepped the bounds of sobriety. Our .record is spoiled by don't-care strangers." Fine: 95.; in default, seven days' imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>IN MS SECOND CENTURY.</p>
        <p>Notorious Character Says He Speaks</p>
        <p>The Truth.</p>
        <p>Almost as frequently as the Dock Sergeant, John Heaton stands before the Warrington magistrates. He was there on *onday for the 101st time.</p>
        <p>The charge was the usual one of being drunk and disorderly, and it was stated that all the way from Winwick-street, where he was arrested, to the Police station, he poured out a stream of indecent language. " I never did," he denied indignantly, as be- Comes an innocent.</p>
        <p>en can never .use any other sort of language when drunk," retorted the Chief Con- table.</p>
        <p>The Prisoner ceased his denial for a topical explanation. "Why I got a drop too much, Your Worships," he said persuasively, "was because my brother had got severely wounded in Gallipoli. lam very sorry." But the Mayor (Alderman P, Peacock) who was presiding, had a good memory. "You told us that last time," he commented. "But it's the truth this time," said Heaton with conviction.</p>
        <p>The Chief Consta,ble: He won't keep sober. As soon as he is out of gaol he is in again. Sentence: One month's imprisonment and two sureties of £5 each; in default, a further two months' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>The prisoner walked dejectedly down the steps his boots are helping to wear out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>BACK HOME.</head>
        <p>BACK HOME.</p>
        <p>Stolen Horse Finds Its Way To The</p>
        <p>Stable.</p>
        <p>Given a chance, a stolen animal will usually find its way home. _ _ .</p>
        <p>An instance of this was. mentioned at the Police Court on Tuesday, when Charles Hem.), Barker, .who was arrested in Hull in December, was charged with stealing in ,December, 1912, a trap and harness, the property of Mr. Connor, of b'ankey-street.</p>
        <p>The-prisoner having pleaded guilty, the Chief Constable explained that in December, 1912, the prisoner and another man asked Mr. Connor for the loan of a horse and trap for the purposo of bringing some potatoes in from- the country. The sum of 12s. 6d. was paid for the hire, and the vehicle was to have been returned the same day. This was not done, however, and informa- tion was given to the police. The property was traced in rather a peculiar manner. A local horse dealer happened' to be at a sale in a Liverpool horse repository in January, 1913. Tie had an idea that he knew a certain horse which was being put. up for sale, so he bid for it, had it knocked down to him, and then brought it to Warrington by road. When in Sankey-street he gave it a loose rein, allowing it to go where it chose, and it found its way not only to Mr. Connor's stable. but to its oilan stall. The police had discovered that the trap and harness had been sold in Liverpool. The trap was traced, but it was never recovered, owing to the fact that the money spent on its repair by the purchaser was considered to be too much to pay for. Thus the whole of the property was lost to the owner, excepting the bit.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable added that the prisoner had been arrested in Hull on a charge of steal- ing a cheque, but was found not guilty at Hull Sessions last week. He had been convicted several times for theft, and since he stole the horse and 'trap he had served two terms of im- prisonment, one being six months in duration and the other of rine months.</p>
        <p>The prisoner: Henceforward I will lead an 'honest and straightforward life. Sentence: Six months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>JEALOUSAIINDED.</head>
        <p>JEALOUSAIINDED.</p>
        <p>Wife Repudiates Husband's</p>
        <p>Accusations.</p>
        <p>Said by Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, to be jealous-minded and bad-tempered, George Spurling, of Chapel-lane, 'Burtonwood, was charged at the Warrington County Sessions on Wednesday with deserting his wife, whereby she now asked for a,separation order.</p>
        <p>, Mr. Browne, who appeared for the com- plainant, said the parties were married in 1907 at Bvrtonwood Parish Church. and there were three children. The defendant was of a very jealous disposition, exceedingly bad-tempered,</p>
        <p>t====3</p>
