        <p>YIELD TO MOTHER SEIGEL'S _</p>
        <p>SYRUP</p>
        <p>Study economy by makinl your own Cigarettes</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere in -,S d. Books..</p>
        <p>FOR</p>
        <p>Best House Coal NUTS &amp; SLACK, WASHED NUTS, BEANS, &amp; PEAS,</p>
        <p>CROMPTON &amp; SIiAfICROSS, LTD,</p>
        <p>COLLIERY PROPRIETORS. Dailam Lane, Warrington, AND AT CAR TERMINUS, LATCHFORD. All inquiries to F. W. PRAEGER, District Agerd</p>
        <p>quarrelsome, and used disgusting language be- fore his young children. On being married he went to live at his wife's mother's house, which was her own. He lived there rent free, only having to pay the poor rate. He was now under-manager at Ashton's Green Colliery, near St. Helens. and earned £3 10s. a week. He was always charging his wife with being too intimate with her uncle and another man, but there was not the slightest foundation for the allegations. When he left the home he kissed the 4ildren, remarking to the youngest, aged three years, 'This will be the last tine. I shall ever kiss you. I will leave you to be kept by your uncle on chalk and water." The uncle happened to be in the milk trade, added Mr. Browne. - Heaven And Hell.</p>
        <p>In the witness box, Mrs. Spurling said her husband was always making false accusations against her. 'Replying to Mr. H. J. Longton (for the de- fendant) the complainant admitted that her husband had written and asked her to live nearer to his work and away from her mother. In the course of a letter he wrote to her it was stated "All married couples have words more or less. .. . Some weeks with you have been like heaven and some like hell."</p>
        <p>Complainant admitted having a banking account of £2O which had been saved out of the defendant's wages. He always gave her his wages, but, she added, he forget what she had to pay out of them. When they got married he had no money—not even enough td buy a new pair of boots. She had to draw £l2 of her own money to get married, and had to pay 2.9 16s. fOr some books he had bought. Mr. Longton maintained that. the defendant had not deserted his wife. He had certainly left the house, but he wanted her to go with him. He was tired of the nagging of his mother-in-law. He would return, however, and live with her if his mother-in-law would live next door with her brother.</p>
        <p>The defendant reiterated his desire to live with his wife, and said she was all right, and quite a good woman. It was her relatives who were the cause of the trouble. If sbe would live with him away from her mother he was sure they would be happy. The magistrates dismissed the summons.</p>
