      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>LEFT HIS WIFE.</head>
        <p>LEFT HIS WIFE.</p>
        <p>Raker Sent To Prison For Three Months.</p>
        <p>"It's no use appealing to a man like you," was the comment of Mr. J. Rymer Young at Thursday's Police Court, when Charles Fredk. Haseldine, a baker, of Rugby, was proceeded against for leaving his wife and children chargeable to the Warrington Union. Mr. A. Bottomley (the clerk to the Warring- ton Union) said Haseldine left Warrington in October, and it was supposed he went away with a married woman. The wife, who was very respectable, became chargeable to the Union, and altogether had been paid ES Bs. in relief. Sentence: Three months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>JUVENILE THIEVES.</head>
        <p>JUVENILE THIEVES.</p>
        <p>Ten Birched And One Sent To - - Reformatory.</p>
        <p>Stealing by juveniles is still causing parents and the authorities much anxiety. No fewer than eleven boys were before the magistrates on Wednesday on charges of steal- ing.</p>
        <p>One boy, aged 13 years, was ordered to be sent to a reformatory school for five years on charges of stealing a sky-cycle, the property of Tryphenia Knott, of 236, Manchester-road, and stealing two pies from the shop of Mrs. Knox, of Padgate-lane. The Chief Constable said it had been found the boy had pilfered from every shop in the neighbourhood. He used to go into shops and ask for things he knew they did not stock, and then take the opportunity of stealing every- thing he could lay his hands on. Ho (the Chief Constable) had consulted the Director of Educa- tion, and they had decided that the best way to deal with the boy was to send him to reformatory. The mother knew the boy was taking these things, but did not try to stop him. The course suggested by the Chief Constable was adopted. Boys' Novel Methods.</p>
        <p>For ingenuity, ten boys who were brought be- fore the Court will be hard to beat. They were charged with stealing packets of sweetmeats, the property of the British Sweetmeat Co. It was stated in the evidence that these boys extracted packets of chocolate and sweetmeat from machines at the Bank Quay and Central Stations by using tin discs. These discs were manufactured by the boys. They were taken off the tops of ginger beer bottles and then placed on the tramlines so that a tramcar might flatten them out Fifty-three of these discs had been used in the ,machines at the Central Station and 48 at the Bank Quay Station.</p>
        <p>The boys admitted the offence, and the three eldest were ordered to receive six strokes of the birch, the remainder of the boys to receive four strokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>"NO FREE MEN."</head>
        <p>"NO FREE MEN."</p>
        <p>Employer Tells Workman To Be</p>
        <p>Patriotic.</p>
        <p>An assertion that while the war is on there are .no free men in England, was made by a Warrington employer to one .of his workmen who defied him, and at the Munitions Tribunal on Monday, obtained the certificate of discharge for which he had asked.</p>
        <p>. The man in question is James Green, of 16, Mercer-street, Newton-le-Willows, who com- plained that his discharge certificate was being unreasonably withheld. He further complained he was expected to do two men's work, and in consequence could not make sufficient pro gress, and was subject to insults, Sce. Green explained that he was a riveter, and his wages were 395. a week and a war bonus. He applied for permission to leave three months ago, but was refused.</p>
        <p>In reply to the managing director of the firm, Green admitted he had been censured for lounging about with his hands in his pockets. Did you demand your discharge? he was further asked. Green: I did not! Did you ask for your discharge ?—Yes. The Managing Director: That's the same thing.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. T. J. Ridgway) : Not quite. __</p>
        <p>The Managing Director: Didn't you say you were a free man?</p>
        <p>• Green: I told you I had not signed ,my liber- ties away. The Managing Director: Didn't I tell you there was no such thing as a free man in the country whilst the war is on? And didn't I ask you to be patriotic? Green Yes.</p>
        <p>Further evidence was given by the works' manager and foreman, and it was alleged thiit Green deliberately malingered in order- that he might be discharged. It was also alleged he had' caused the whole shop to be unsettled. The Tribunal granted Green a. discharge cer- tificate. • - Mr. Ridgway's assessors were Messrs. John R. Watts, of Northwich (employers' panel) and C. R. Bates, of Crewe (workmen's panel).</p>
      </div>
