      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>The League Table Unchanged Despite Hard-Fought Games.</p>
        <p>After a well-fought dame, Leeds beat Hunslet by eight points to three, and thus materially strengthened their position at the head of the League table. Rochdale Hornets secured a good victory over Broughton Rangers at the Cliffe by 21 points to 8, while Swinton, at hone, accounted for Oldham by ten clear points. St. Helens and St. Helens Recs. met for the fifth time this season, and the last-named secured their third victory by six points to three. Other results were :</p>
        <p>Rochdale H., 21 points; Broughton 11, 8 points Wigan, 9; Salford, 3. Swinton, 10; Oldham, 0. Dewsbury, 29; Bradford, 8. \Batley, 8; Halifax, 7. Leeds, 8; Hunslet, 7. Barrow, 18; Bramley, 0. Hull Kingston R., 3; Leigh, 0. St. Helens Rec., 6; St. Helens, 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>FISH FOR OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>FISH FOR OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Jack Fish, the old Warrington favourite, is turning out for 'Oldham on Saturday at Water- sheddings, against Leeds, along with another veteran—Arthur Lees, the Oldham half-back.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Two Exciting Games Played At Wilderspool On Saturday.</p>
        <p>Only two games in the Intermediate League were played on Saturday, and both were on the Wilderspool ground. Here two good matches were witnessed, and each was marked by distinctive features. In the St. Thomas' v. Parochial match four players were turned off by the referee, Mr. W. Bibby, and in the Rylands's v. Bolton match 34 points were scored between the rival'thirteens.</p>
        <p>St-. Mary's should have met Sacred Heart on Victoria Park, but the last named team failed to nut in an appearance, and the match was declred off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>PAROCHIAL'S FIERCE ENCOUNTER WITH</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL'S FIERCE ENCOUNTER WITH</p>
        <p>ST. THOMAS'.</p>
        <p>The match between St. Thomas' and Parochial was very exciting—too exciting, in fact, for good Rugby football. Each side had brought a host Of supporters, armed with rattles, &amp;c., and the crowd which assembled on the ground was certainly one of the largest of the season.. The feeling was naturally very acute. Parochial had only one defeat in its record, and that had been effected by the Stockton Heath team. And on Saturday they were determined to get their own back. Unfortunately, this fact, coupled with the noise of the crowd, made a few players lose their heads, with the consequence that Mr. Bibby, who is a referee who will strand no nonsense, had to give marching orders to four of them—three Parochial men and one "Heathen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>St. Thbmas' Early Promise.</head>
        <p>St. Thbmas' Early Promise.</p>
        <p>The only score of the match was a try by Parochial just before the interval, and the score about fitted the play. St. Thomas' looked like scoring very early. On a couple of occasions within the first ten minutes they were awarded free-kicks, in what might be termed fairly good positions, but goals were not forthcoming. . .</p>
        <p>Play was carried into midfield, and, eventu- ally, St. Thomas' had a hard task to defend their line. The game became fast and furious, and the weak spot in St. Thomas' team was apparent. Their threequarters often secured the ball, but hesitated, and were lost. On one occasion, instead of tackling his opponent, one of them turned his back on him, thereby enabling Parochial to register the only score which gave them the game. It was after the interval when the rough play began. Scrimmage after scrimmage was broken up by the eagerness of the Parochial forwards. One of them soon received his marching orders. Ire was followed two minutes later by one of his colleagues, accompanied• by one of .his opponents. A few minutes afterwards the fourth man was sent off, and he proved to be the last. The game continued, play being very oven, and finished as &lt;..a win for Parochial by • three points to nil.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S AND BOLTON SCORE THIRTY- _ FOUR POINTS.</p>
        <p>Both Rylands's and Bolton's teams were in good form for scoring on Saturday, and before the final whistle was blown, eight tries had been scored and five goals kicked, the scores being 21 points to thirteen in favour of Rylands's. The play, however, was very scrappy, kick and rush seeming to be the only tactics eiths.r team knew how to employ. These tacci..s seemed to be sucessful so far wi•nt, and Rylands's soon put the first five points on: Bolton were not long in bringing the score level, but before half-time were behind again, the teams changing over with the score eight points to five against Bolton. In the opening stages of the second half, Rylands's had by far the better of the play. and it was not long before they had increased their score to 16=5.</p>
        <p>Bolton seemed to wake up and redueed their lead by eight points, and even looked like win- ning. This was not to be, however, and just before the close of the game Rylauds's made the score 21 points to 13. - The scorers were as follows :—Rylands: Savoury .(12 points). Hill (3 points). Dumbell (2 points) and Molyneux (t points); Bolton, Bates (13 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>RESULTS UP TO AND INCLUDING JANUARY 29th.</head>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO AND INCLUDING JANUARY 29th.</p>
        <p>Points P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Parochial Heathsido Ryla nds's St. Thomas' Beamont Bolton Sacred Heart St. Mary's St. James'</p>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>Paiovhinl, 3 point; St. Thomas', nil . llylands's 21; Bolton, 13.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Bearoont V. Sacred Heart. Referee, G. Crozier. Kick-oft 2.30. . Bolton v. Hoathside. Referee J. 13oard.sa11. Kick-off 3.30. .</p>
        <p>On Victeria Park. St. Mary's v. Parochial. Referee, U. Cooper Kick-oft 3.0.</p>
        <p>At Gorscy-lane.</p>
        <p>Rylands's V. St. James'. Referee, R. Williams. Kick-off 3.0. The League meeting- will take place on Thurs- day next at 7.30, in the Warrington Pavilion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>At Go rsey-la ne.</p>
        <p>Kick off 3.0.</p>
        <p>Rylands's: T. Archer: Littlewood, J. Moly- neux, Britton and T. Bill; Savoury and Swanton; Dumbell, Barker, Sutton, Mulvey, Latimer and Butler. Reserves.—Thomas, Sloane and Bibby. Committee.—Butterworth and liorroek9.</p>
        <p>BEAMONT JUNIORS v. ST. JAMES' JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>Beamont.—J. Anderton; E. Makin, A. Mmon W. Smith (captain) and J. Higgins; J. C,ole. man and D. Siuclair;•Baguky, Hackett, Whit taker, Farrel, Johnson and Taker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>•:. ~:-:-:-:-:•.:.:-:•4•:.4-:..:-:-:•••:••:-:••:-:.,:••:*</head>
        <p>•:. ~:-:-:-:-:•.:.:-:•4•:.4-:..:-:-:•••:••:-:••:-:.,:••:*</p>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>SANKEY v. BEAMONT JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>This match, which w:x-, played at Sankey, was very interesting throughout. Sankey sus- tained their first defeat of the season, and the victory was well deserved by the winners. Bea- mont commenced in fine style, and after suc- cessive scrummages on the Sankey line Smith made a good mark, for Armitage to kick an easy goal. This aroused more vigour in the Sankey players, hut the Beamont lads repulsed their efforts by good combination from the pack, which ended in Makin scoring a grand try, for Sinclair to add the extra points. Rough play was witnessed in the second half. After Sankey had made good efforts to score they were awarded a try at the corner, the goal-kick failing. Mason scored a try for Bea- mont. Sankey never gave up hopes, and it was not long, before they recorded another try. This completed the scoring. Result: Beamont, 3 goals 2 tries (12 points); Sankey, 2 tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>South Liverpool The Better Side</p>
        <p>At Hood Lane.</p>
        <p>That there was disappointment around Hood- lane on Saturday after the match with South Liverpool goes without saying. Big things were expected.. A victory for the home side would mean increased support. There was ample reason' for these hopes and aspirations. Had not the new town club run the visitors to a goal at Liverpool' the previous week, after an exciting struggle, and were they not unlucky in not sharing the honours? But, alas, this confidence. in the ability of the home team to turn the tables was mis- placed, for, to be quite candid about Saturday's display, they were not quite class enough for the visitors. Even the most biassed supporter of Warrington was compelled to admit that the honours had fallen to the better side.</p>
        <p>Right throughout the game Warrington rarely approached the football standard the Liver- pool players. The many combined movements between Hall, Page and Cook were keenly appreciated by the spectators. Occasionally they erred in attempting too much finesse, thus giving the home defenders time to recover and repel the invasion. Millward and Leah, were kept continually on the 'move, and it was due to their harassing methods that Kite, the home goalkeeper, was not more frequently tested. Much of the best work of Hall was spoiled by his -method of running the ball to the extreme corner and shooting at awkward angles. Nevertheless, he was a dangerous man, aml required very careful watching. It was from one of his solo efforts in the second half that the goal which sealed 'Warrington's fate was seored.</p>
        <p>The Warrington forwards showed up some- what poorly in comparison with the Liverpool front line. The attempts at combination were very scrappy, whilst there seemed to be a lack of understanding between them and the half-backs when on the offensive. More ex- perience together, however, should help them. Of their marksmanship it must be admitted that there is room for improvement. Is it nerves or lack of the necessary skill? Perhaps a little ball practice would have the desired effect. Against Tranmere this weakness was apparent. Of the middle line trio, Bullen (prior to his injury) showed the best judgment in his placing of the ball to the forwards. Ditchfield and G. Whitfield were not nearly so accurate. On many occasions the Liverpool halves intercepted a pass intended for a Warrington player, and thus turned defence into attack. Another marked failing of the. Warrington defenders was that they were too prone to " balloon " the ball in- stead of keeping it low.</p>
        <p>Such methods only played into the hands of the visitors' full-backs, Page and Jenkinson. who revelled in their work. The former was the best back on the field, and his clearing always showed rare power and judgment. In the second half Warrington were handicapped owing to an accident .to Bullen, who was little more than a Messenger. Millward continues to justify the good things said of him. He played a really good game, although he had a warm handful in dealing with Hall. The Liverpool forwards found Kite a trustworthy keeper, and he could not be blamed for the goal which was scored. several times' he proved his ability by stopping difficult shots.</p>
        <p>Warrington have no reason to be disheartened at the results already attained in the League. Criticism is only made with a view to helping them to diminish their faults. Although victory is denied them. it will be readily admitted that their firSt four engagements have been fairly stiff ones.</p>
        <p>Saturday's crowd of 3,000 indicates a desire for Association football of good quality, and that there are .possibilities of this is shown by the matches already played.</p>
        <p>The "gate" was a very good one, and realised £3O. A great crush resulted in many getting in free, and some damage was done to the fences.</p>
        <p>To-day's team at Walkden will include Co- son, who who has been transferred from Tfonme, Rovers, at • back; and J. Daintith, the Warring- ton Rugby player, who is being tried at lira back. Millen, who was hurt in the first fire minutes of the match last Saturday, will be out.of the team for two pr three weeks. Despite .the criticisms made, rif "tip. Warrington- team play as well as last week they ;liould add points to their record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN v. SOUTH LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the Hood-lane ground last Saturday, in their return fixture. The following players turned cut:— Warrington Town: Kite; Leah and Millward; Ditehfield, CI. Whitfield and Bullen; Aspinall, Midge, Foster, T. Whitfield and Banner. • South Liverpool: Moore; J. Page .and iTenkin- son; Smith, Carlisle and Williams; Hall, Pages Cook, Rigsby and .Parr.</p>
        <p>_ The home captain won the toss and decided to defend the -lane goal. Cook set the ball rolling twenty minutes after the advertised time. After the opening exchanges Bullen passed out to Banner, only to see Page make a fine return, and, for a few minutes, play ,was in midfield. Then the visitors breathe more aggressive, and the game was conte,ted in the home half, and a free-kick against Bullen• was well placed by Smith, only to see Cook shoot: over the bar. From the goal-kick G. Whitfield set his for- wards going, but they failed to get past. the opposing hacks, and the leather was sent out to and Millward - forced him to play into touch. The visitors .returned to the attack and gained Ahe first corner-kick, which Was well cleareil by Kite, and Aspinall gained possession and centred •in front .of the visitors' : goal, Jenkinson punting away. *Page secured just in- side :his own half, and after a splendid indi- vidual effort, shot wide of the goal. • Home Team's Determined Attack.</p>
        <p>.... . After this the home team made a determined attack on the visitors' goal, and from a centre by liullen, Illidge sent the ball into the net, but was judged .to be offside. This gave relief. to the visitors, and play was soon at the home end, where'Kite.tipped the ball over the bar. The ensuing earner was futile, and then the home forwards attacked very strongly, and Moore made several tine saves. Play was again transferred to the home end, where the good custodianship proved fatal to the efforts of the visitors, and after a brief visit to the visitors' end, play was_in-midfield at the interval:</p>
        <p>Half-time: Warrington Town, 0; South Liver- pool, 0.</p>
        <p>_ The second half opened in a very spirited fashion, and a. raid on the home goal was 'frustrated by Mil!ward. The home forwards</p>
        <p>retaliated very strongly, Aspinall leading the attack. Ms final shot hit the crossbar and T. Whitfield met the rebound. Moore brought off a remarkable save with a crowd of players round him.</p>
        <p>Following this clearance • the home goal was attacked, and a lobbing centre from Page was headed just inside the post by Parr, giving the visitors the first goal. From the centre-kick Foster initiated an attack, when Page inter- vened in the nick of time, and a spell of mid- field ,play followed, when the exchanges were keenly contested. The home team were again put on the defensive, and Cook pissed a good opening inside the penalty area. Relief was obtained when Williams shot over the bar. After this the home team •made their strongest effort, and Illidge shot on the wrong side of the post, and from another of his centres Foster was wide with a high shot. fjenkinson conceded a corner, which was well-placed by Illidge, and Banner headed into goal, where Moore was again to the rescue. Towards the close, of the game the visitors pressed strongly, and gained several corner-kicks, and Cook - lost a, good chance of increasing the lead by hesitating to shoot. • Millward rushed across- and 'charged the shot down, and then 'good work by Leah trans- ferred play to midfield. Result :--South Liverpool, 1 goal; Warrington Town, 0. ,</p>
        <p>On Saturday the game between the Warring- ton Central and Hollins Green teams was declared to be one of the best witnessed in the Park this season. Owing to munition work the Central were not able to put their best team in the field, only one of their usual forwards being able to turn out, and owing to a cold, Knight was not able to keep goal. The visitors were very well represented, and deserved to win on the day's play, Green, Muskett, Clark and Mellor doing especially well. It was in the de- fence where the home team were seen to the best advantage, and Smith led many raids on the visitors' goal, but did not get sufficient assistance from his colleagues.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>NA IRRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LE VA E.</head>
        <p>NA IRRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LE VA E.</p>
        <p>TABLE TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Goals</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. P. St. Elph in's Howley United Fairfield United Res. 8 4 3 1 24 12 9 Stockton Heath Scouts . 9 4 4 1 19 17 9 Orford Juniors</p>
        <p>BeWsey A.F.C. *Penketh Juniors Mersey Juniors * Penketh Juniors have had two points de- ducted foi playing ineligible'pittyers</p>
        <p>A very keen gable was witnessed on the Arpley ground, where St. Elphin's were, at home to. Penketh Juniors. After a keen and evenly-con- tested first half the visitors held the lead, but on resuming. the " Saints " gave a very much improved display and, beating the Juniors at every point, succeeded in lowering their colours on three more occasions without any responSe from the visitors.</p>
        <p>On the Lovely-lane ground, tho game between the Howley United and Bewsey teams was not finished in a very sporting fashion. The first portion proved to be goalless,, and the second portion was tieing contested very keenly, the visitors .drawing first. blood. ' The. home team retaliated very strongly, but could not break through the opposing defence. The game had been in progress sixty-two minutes when the visitors scored their second goal, and the home team appealed against this goal being allowed on the grounds of a foul throw-in. The referee's decision was not in their favour, and they then refused to continue the game, and left the field.</p>
        <p>This, indeed; was a very unsportsmanlike action. For their benefit I will give that por- tion ,of the rule which refers to a foul throw, and will be found in Rule 5, Referees' Chart Book</p>
        <p>"An improper throw would be one delivereil over the shoulder, or with one hand giving the impetus and the other merely guiding the ball, or if the thrower had not some part of both feet on the touch-line at the moment of throw- ing; or. in other words, the law is complied with if the player has any part of both feet on the line when he throws the ball in."</p>
        <p>For to-day all the clubs in the League will be engaged, and some very interesting games should be played. The .most important is on the Arpley ground, where the St. Elphin's will engage the Howley United in their return fix- ture. This will be the tit-bit of /the afternoon. The Howley team declare they are going to be the first to lower the "Saints" colours, and the " Saints " have a double object in view, the first being to retain an unbeaten record, and the second, if they succeed in gaining two points to-day they will be sure of championship honours. None of the other clubs are able to overtake them in points, should defeat overtake them in their remaining fixtures.</p>
        <p>Fairfield Reserves and Penketh Juniors will be in opposition for the first time on the Rec- reation Ground, and a very keen struggle ,hould be witnessed, and the United Reserves will make a strong effort to regain their position ns runners-up, which they have tem- porarily lost.</p>
        <p>On their recent form the Orford Juniors should net have much difficulty on their own ground in gaining both points at the expense of Mersey Juniors.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Seonts have not been engaged in League warfare for the last three weeks, but to-day they visit the Lovely-lane ground to play Bewsey. An interesting game should be wit- nessed.</p>
        <p>The knock-out competition mentioned in these notes last week is being eager.y inquired about by the ,junior players. It will be discussed at the next League meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>ST. ELPHIN'S v. PENKETH JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. PENKETH JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>On the Arpley ground, before a good number of spectators.</p>
        <p>St. Elphini: Lockhart; Buckley and Gleave; Shelmerdine, Miller and Rothwell; Boyle, Shake Abaft, Rigby, Burns and Humphries. Penketh Juniors: Moss; Morris and Thomas- son; Swift, Sankey and Wright; Atkinson, Glover, Pearson, liVratson and Finch. The visitors made the first attack, but were repulsed by Buckley. From a goal-kick .the home team made their first attempt to lower the visitors' • colours, but their final • efforts were weak. After fifteen minutes' play Glover scored for the. visitors with a smart shot from twenty yards' range. Rothwell, Miller and Rigby, by good combination, transferred .play to the visitors' end, but were met by a good defence. Half-time :----Penketh Juniors, 1 goal; St. Elphin's, nil.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the "Saints "get up a very fast pace, and were quickly in front of the visitors' goal, • but were driven back by Morris and Thomasson. The "Saints ", again attacked strongly, Rigby, Shakeshaft and Boyle being prominent, and Rigby made the .scores equal. The " Saints " overplayed their. opponents, and were awarded a penalty-kick, from .which Boyle failed to score.. A few minutes later he made amends by dribbling past the backs and put- ting the "Saints" in front. The visitors made an attempt to get on equal terms and failed to score from a corner-kick. The "Saints now had the best of the exchanges, and Boyle scored again during a.scikimmage in front of the goal. The "Saints" continued to attack, and gained a corner-kick. This was well placed by Rothwell, and Humphries ,added the goal. Result :—St. Elphin's, 4 goals; Penketh Juniors, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY v. HOWLEY UNITED.</p>
        <p>At Lovely-lane, before a good crowd of sup- porters. The first half was very evenly con- tested, the defenders on each side coming out on top. At the interval no goals had been regis- tered.</p>
        <p>In the second portion the Howley lads were soon prominept with a smart attack, but they failed to make the best use of their opportuniti,,, and were 'gradually driven back.. At the. end of fifteen minutes'. play in the second half Hough opened the score .for the visitors with a fast shot. The visitors' defence would not yield to heavy pressure. Then Hough, receiving the balk from a throw-in, made a grand individual effort, beating five opponents and scoting the second goal. This was after sixty-two minutes' play, and at this juncture the home team refused to continue the game, alleging that the referee had allowed the play to proceed from ! a foul throw-in, which resulted in the second</p>
        <p>goal being scored. The score'at the time was:— Howley United, 2 goals; Bewsey,</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CENTRAL v. HOLLINS GREEN.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park, before a good crowd of spectators. Central: Warburton; Sankey and Glover; Brown, Gilbert and Stephens; Mason, Ashcroft, , Ellis, Catlow and Smith. 1 Hollins Green: Randle; Millington and Clarke; Mellor, Cleworth and Knight; Savoury, Green, Muskett, Buckley and Harrison. Smith made a good run on the left, but his final shot was put over the dead line. From the ' goal-kick the 'visitors made a raid on the home , goal, where Glover defended very well, and from his clearance Mason :placed in front of the visi- tors' goal, where Ellis had hard lines by heading inches on the wrong side of the upright. The visitors improved and good play by Green 'caused Warburton to save twice in quick suc- cession. Then from another attack Sankey handled inside the area and Muskett scored from the ensuing penalty-kick. Even play fol lowed, and at the interval the score was:— Hollins Green, 1 goal; Central nil. • The home team, with the slope in their favour, allowed the visitors to become danger- .ous,.Green leading a strong attack, which caused the home defence some trouble to clear. A visit to the opposite end saw Brown shoot past the post. Mellor, by resourceful play, again placed. his forwards in an aggressive position, but they could not break down the home de- fence: 'Gilbert was displaying fine judgment at !centre:half, and did • not allow Muskat to Shine.. Smith, receiving a good pass from ,Stephens, made a grand individual run on the left, beating several opponents, and centred well in the goal-mouth, where Mason had a grand chance to equalise, but he failed to accept the opening. This gave relief to the visitors' defence, and their forwards made a final effort to increase their lead, but again the home de- fence prevailed. Result :—Hollins Green, I goal; Warrington Central, nil.</p>
      </div>
