      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>Medical.</head>
        <p>Medical.</p>
        <p>Money.</p>
        <p>SOLDIER ABSENTEE DROWNED IN THE MERSEY.</p>
        <p>The circumstances of the death of Private Thomas Silk, of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, were investigated by Mr. H. C. Yates (coroner) at Stockton Heath Police Station on Thursday afternoon. The body of Silk was found in the River Mersey. John Welsby, of 11, Atherton-street, Warring- ton, said Silk used to be employed'at Crosfields, but left Warrington about three years ago for Scotland. When the war broke out he joined the Army. He understood that Silk had served in the South African War.</p>
        <p>Police-Sergeant William Wilcacksow said the deceased had in his possession a military pass, which entitled him to a furlough from 23rd December, 1915, to January Ist, 1916, and leave to proceed to Warrington from Edmonton. Silk had been gazetted an absentee. Sergeant Phillips, of the depot of the South Lancs. Regiment, said On the 11th November Silk was admitted into Edmonton Hospital suffering from myalgia. He was given a leave in December to proceed to 11, Atherton-street, War- rington, from the hospital. Verdict: "Found drowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>POTATOES FROM GERMAN CEMETERIES.</head>
        <p>POTATOES FROM GERMAN CEMETERIES.</p>
        <p>• SWISB newspapers learn from Berlin that the German Government has given instructions that all open spaces, including cemeteries, must be utilised this spring for the cultivation of pota- toes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 5, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>chuckle ,in it, every song is sparkling, and thersi are plenty'of.them, every dance a delight. The scenery and mounting is as handsome as- it is original, and the costumes are the finest to be seen on the stage at the present moment. It is just a play for the times. At the rise of the curtain all one's cares svill fall away, and when it is over happy memories of its jokes and its haunting melodies will linger for weeks to come. The company is a strong and excel- lent one. The cast includes Harry Playdora, Ruth Trafford, Marjorie Kennard, Harry John- stone, &amp;c. The play is drawing all London to the Gaiety, and will continue to do so for many months to come. For those who see "To-night's the Night," to-morrow night and the next night will be "the night" as well. The. piece is being splayed twice nightly, with matinee on Monday at 2.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Raynor's Company In Repertoire LONDON C ITY &amp; MIDLAND. Of Famous Plays. A better triumvirate of plays than those which Mr. Raynor and his company have 'leen pre senting this week need not be imagine.l. Each is by a writer who has passed away, 131 It &amp;t a iegacy of works, .which we are all pr,lnd ir. in- herit. "Moths," by Oujda, was given t•ii Mon- day and Tuesday, and is to be presented zgai.jn to-night. "A Woman of No Import inve: by Oscar Wilde, was presented on Wednes•Lsy and Thursday, and A Bunch of Violets," by 3ydney Grundy, on Friday. Each is full of dramatic power. though of a different classifica- tion. " Moths "is .sensation allayed by morals, and appears to be good. The wit of "A Woman of No Importance" surpasses either of the other two, but it was regrettable that the action of the play was confined merely to Hunstanton Chase. Really, the play should have two scenes —but travelling companies are so much on the move that they may be forgiven for not skip- ping from one residence to another when they get .settled. As regards the acting it was all very well done, but we tihould have liked to have seen Mr. Jack Melville a little more suave and mobile of countenance as Lord Illingworth. That was all that was. needed to Make his a per.: feet rendering. .11-e had.a.distinguished bearing,</p>
        <p>entitling him to be lionised by society. "A Bunch of Violets" was perhaps the best play Mr. Grundy ever wrote, and the audiences last night fully appreciated the presentation. "To-Night's The Night." For many years past the Gaiety has stood for all that was brightest and best on the English musical comedy stage. Of all the successes pro- duced at that famous house (and their name is legion) To-night's the Night," which is due at the Royal Court next week, is surely the greatest. The story is written by Fred Thomp- son,i and the music s by that famous composer, Paul A. Rubens. It has all the elements which went to make the old Gaiety plays the suc- cesses they were, and, what is more, it has an excellent farcical story .of its own. There is not a dull minute from start to finish. Every situation abounds in laughs, every line has a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>BANKERS OPTIMISTIe.</head>
        <p>BANKERS OPTIMISTIe.</p>
        <p>the bank has been in business, and never, once has it failed to make out of the year's profits a contribution to its Reserve Fund. Even this year we have maintained the honourable tra- dition and transferred &lt;25,000 from the year's profits to the Reserve Fund. There is hardly a bank in the kingdom that has such a record. We can go still further. We have never until now drawn anything from that fund. That also is almost a unique position to be in. Now, when the whole world is disorganised by the war, it is only proper that we should make use of the fund, provided for the purpose, and draw from it £lOO,OOO for depreciation of securities, caused by the war. I would call to your memory also the fact that all the depre- ciation of investments BEFORE the war was pro- vided out of profit's without touching the Reserve Fund, or affecting the shareholders' dividends at all. The report refers to our inner reserve, and I repeat that it is ample for any eventualities which we can foresee, in fact we have been very prudent indeed, and the bank is quite as good.as the report shows.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>Increased Accounts.</head>
        <p>Increased Accounts.</p>
        <p>"As for the balance sheet, it looks well, al- though £lOO,OOO is taken off. the Reserve Fund. You will notice that our current and deposit accounts have increased by almost £1,000,000., Most of this is in current accounts and although current account balances are of course valuable„ they ,are not of the same per- manent character as deposits proper, which usually remain with us a considerable time. Naturally the current account balances fluc- tuate with the activity of trade. This is a point that many writers do not seem to have grasped. "On the other side of the statement you see cash and short money, 142,768,000, which is a very respectable total. The investments as compared with last year do not disclose, the total of our subscriptions to the War Loan, as, during the year, many of our short date secu- rities fell due and were repaid, and other American bonds, &amp;c., were realised on satis- factory terms. £3,000,000 War Loan.</p>
        <p>"Our holding of war stock is actually £3,170,000. Bills of Exchange are much re- duced from the total of last year. The explan- ation is quite simple. Having put so much money into war stock we have employed less in the London Discount Market. Our advances, as we saY in the report, give us every satis- faction."</p>
        <p>The Chairman concluded by inoving the adoption of the report, which was carried un- animously.</p>
        <p>LORD EMMOTT'S EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>In moving the re-election of Lord Emmott as a director, Sir Frederick H. Smith said he was sure they would all be glad to see Lord •Emmott a director of the bank. Connected as he was with the great cotton industry of Lan- cashire, and with his knowledge of public affairs, he was eminently fitted for the position of a director of the Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank. Mr. James Kenyon seconded the resolution and said he felt sure Lord Emmott's experience at the Board of Trade would make his ser- vices of peculiar value to the bank. Lord minott's full title was Lord Emmott ctf Old- ham, and his being so well known in that district should prove a very important factor in the interests of the bank. The resolution was carried unanimously. Lord Emmott said he hoped before yery long to be able to give very close attention to the work of the bank.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Whitehead, Mr. Comber and Sir F. H. Smith were re-elected directors. Mr. David Smith and Mr. J. P. Garnett were re-elected as auditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>NATIONAL PROVINCIAL.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL PROVINCIAL.</p>
        <p>Lord inchcape And A Beaten Germany.</p>
        <p>Lord Inchcape presiding at the annual meet- ing of the National Provincial Bank of England. expressed the opinion that Germany was o t.easly irretrievably beaten. "No one," bc Ili lei!. "knows this better than she does herself. She is conducting this war like a very devil let loose from hell. Her inhuman and murderous methods, which would disgrace barbarism, will not be forgotten while the world lasts, and must effectually prevent friendly relations between her and the Allies, either social or commercial, for generations to come?'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>Sacrifice For Freedom.</head>
        <p>Sacrifice For Freedom.</p>
        <p>Despite our enormous national expenditure necessitated by the war, he felt perfectly satis- fied that we should come out victorious in finance, as we should undoubtedly do both on land and ,sea., Even if we ended up the war with a debt of 5,000 millions that Would only mean, with a 5 per cent. interest and, 1 per cent. sinking fund, an annual charge of 300 millions. To this had to be added our pm-war annual expenditure of 200 millions; pensions might be put at another 100 Millions, bringing the total up to 600 millions a year. Our present taxa- tion was 'bringing in nearly 400 millions a year, so that we should have to provide another 200 millions yearly, and this we could do by additional taxe;4, or to some extent by a reduc- tion in that rather extravagant expenditure, not only private but -public, in which we indulged before the war. The standard of living in the country would have to go down, a much smaller amount would have to be spent on luxuries, and the nation would have to work harder and spend less; but the energy and the industry of the people would see us through, and the -country would make any sacrifice to preserve its inde- pendence and freedom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>" Adventuraiss " Banks.</head>
        <p>" Adventuraiss " Banks.</p>
        <p>In looking at the 'position. of 'Abe National Provincial Bank, • its deposits, its loans, and its investments, it was satisfactory to think that they had no cause for anxiety in regard to the .advances made to the constituents • of the bank, amounting to close on 40 millions sterling. lie was surprised to see it suggested in the House of Commons not very long ago that the bankers of this country should be more adventurous, and that they should follow the example of the Deutsche Bank' by financing and taking shares. in industrial enterprises all over the world. 'He was entirely opposed to any such departure on the part of the banks. It would be a distinct betrayal of their trust if they were to invest the funds of their depositors in undertakings of an industrial or speculative character with a view to earning larger divi- dends for themselves. All their deposits were at call or at very short notice, and they would run grave risk of being unable to meet their engage- ments if they were to tie up their resources in the manner suggested. The business of the bank had proved sound and satisfactory during 1915, which spoke well for the position • of the country's trade generally: The deposits, which ho mentioned at the last meeting were a record in its history, were now 61 millions in excess of what they were a year ago. The depreciation in their securities, for which ample provision„ had been made, had been considerable, but 'the. now 'stood in the books at a figure which yielded a larger average return of interest. The number of customers had again increased con- siderably, and the earnings were the highest in the history of the hank. The •report was unanimously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Headquarters, Enville-street, Latchford. Ordets for week ending Februar:v,-. 12th, by J. B. Starkey, Officer Commanding.</p>
        <p>Tuesday. Fall in at Bridge-street Rooms at • 7.30 prompt. Physical and Company Drill. Semaphore Class 8 p.m. to 9 p.m. Thursday. Uniform. Fall in at Bridge-street, 7.30 Prompt. Class Firing.</p>
        <p>Band Practice at Headquarters, 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>Saturday. Parade as strong as possiblo at Mad. quaters, Enville-street, for Route March to Appleton, - 2 p.m. prompt. Dress Marching Order. Water bottles to be tilled, and food carried in haversacks. By biller. J. B. STARKEY. The Corns is open for bovs from 14 to 18 years of age. Particulars may be had from Quarter- master, at 1, St. Mary-street, any evening after?.</p>
        <p>UI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>" Sinbad The Sailor."</p>
        <p>" Sinbad the Sailor" has many artistes in the cast from "the land o' cakes and thistles.," judging from their dialect, and it is unmistak- • ably a Scottish production, for it i,s one of Mr. Harry McKelvie's, and is written by Mr. R. Waldon, of the Royal Princess's Theatre, Glas- gow. It is a show of considerable note, and one which is being highly appreciated by War- rington audiences.</p>
        <p>The two Sisters Cora take.the principal parts as Sinbad and Polly respectively, and portray their numerous items in splendid style. Their popular number is especially "Keep the home fires burning," and causes the audiences to take up the refrain with surprising gusto. Mr. Jack Bamber, as Ra-scull the math, is very funny, his impersonation of the celebrated Charlie Chap- lin being clever and entertaining. His make- up is an exact replica of the famous cinema player, and in features he is not at all unlike this people's favourite. Mr. Harry Elliston, as Sinbad's mother, is successfully humorous. An- other good artiste is Mr. Hugh Ogilvie, who per. forms the part of Tinbad the Tailor. He is seen at his best in scene 3, as the pawnbroker's assistant. The chorus and ballet are well trained, and although some of the gags in the show are by no means new, the production should have a good tour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>" Tit-bits From Opera."</head>
        <p>" Tit-bits From Opera."</p>
        <p>• Miss Olive Westwood and Sir. Albert Bower, lately of the Moody-Manners Opera Co., are to give "Tit-hits twin Opera' at the Him,- drome neit week and, undoubtedly, their " turn " will be good, for indifferent singers are never included in the Moody-Manners Co. Judging from their titles, all the other items which make up the programme will be worth seeing. These are I'. J. Gillen's comedy sketch "Rogers -; the Crascoynes's presentation of their miniature theatre and the "Four Original Storks." Three humorists—lda Re'nita, Jim Nolan and Bert Weston—will also con- tribute to the programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>"THE ROSARY" AT THE KINEMA.</head>
        <p>"THE ROSARY" AT THE KINEMA.</p>
        <p>A picture of rare quality has been showing this week at the Star Kinema. it is called "The Rosary "—for such is the picture— adapted from the play of that name, and show- ing the life of Father Kelly and featuring Kathlyn Wililams. It is shown in five parts and during the process Madame Armstrong sings the well-known song of the name. A further episode of the "Exploits of Elaine," in two parts, proved—as usual—quite exciting, 'while the, " Pathe , War Gazette" depicted several interesting scenes. ' Since Thursday, in addition to "The Rosary," there has been shown a host of other good pic- tures, which should all go to make up a fine programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Next Week.</head>
        <p>Next Week.</p>
        <p>During the first three days next week, premier position will be held by "The Exploits of Elaine," and during the latter half by "The Eternal City."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>GEM PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>GEM PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The films " reeled " this i'eek at the Gem Pic- ture House are of high-class quality, and well worth a visit by those who like the flickers of the magic lamp. The new management are sparing no expense or efforts in providing healthy amuse- ment for young and old alike. During the first part of the week the premier film was one en- titled "The Black King," and was very sensa- tional, although not• too far-fetched. Another splendid reel was "The Magic Monocle," this film creating much hilarity and mirth. The latter part of the week is equally as good as the former, "The Mysterious Hand," a film of a famous producing house, being very absorbing and weird. The pictures truly are "reel gems."</p>
        <p>They Knew. • After service: "What was that sentence the choir repeated .0 often during the Litany F" "As near as I could make out, it was, We are all miserable singers.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>Sir Edward golden's Pointed Questions For Germany.</head>
        <p>Sir Edward golden's Pointed Questions For Germany.</p>
        <p>The general meeting of the shareholders of the London City and Midland Bank, Ltd., was held at the Cannon-street Hotel, London, E.C., last Friday. Sir Edward H. Holden, Bart., the chairman, presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>GERMAN AND BRITISH RESOURCES</head>
        <p>GERMAN AND BRITISH RESOURCES</p>
        <p>The Chairman recalled the fact that last year a German banker said that every mark would be squeezed until it shrieked; but he (the Chairman) thought we should presently see that the mark had already been so squeezed that there was not even a squeak left in it. Up to the present time London had been admitted to be the financial centre of the whole world, but it was predicted by some that after the war we might lose that position. In his opinion we should not only not lose our financial position, but we should greatly improve, it, and our banking institutions would stand much higher in the 'estimation of the financial world than ever before. It was essential for us to retain such an amount of labour as was necessary to maintain our exports, otherwise the exchanges would fall and our gold would go. Of the Allies, the credit of Great Britain stood the highest and had been used to assist the credit of the other allied countries. International Finance.</p>
        <p>The exchanges which had given us the most trouble were the Dutch and American, and the trouble in both cases had been caused by in- creased imports. The most important exchange we had was that between New York and Lon- don. Having compared the rate for the years 1910 up to September, 1915, when it dropped to 4.50 he observed that several reasons had contributed to the drop. . American purchases of exchange on account of " invisibles " were curtailed, the finance bills which usually matured in August were practically non- existent, the American cotton crop and the record wheat crop were beginning to move for- ward, and heavy payments for the increasing purchases by the British Government were being made. Imports Must Be Reduced.</p>
        <p>The fluctuations of these exchanges were brought about mainly by the excessive amount of imports over exports. As we were incon- venienced so much in consequence of our in- creased importations, it was regrettable that our purchases were not reduced. We must im- press the need for economy more emphatically on the minds of the men and women of this country, and remind them that there were only a few ways of paying for our imports. Questions For Germany.</p>
        <p>At the present time the exchangeable value of the .2 sterling in New York showed a depre- ciation of only 1.9 per cent., whereas the ex- changeable value of the mark in New York showed a depreciation of 21.5 per cent. Ile would like to put tlm: following questions to Dr. lielfferich, the German Finance Minister: When the war broke out Germany had credit balances abroad against which they might sell exchange. Were those balances existing at the present time, or were they all exhausted?</p>
        <p>- Had the exports of Germany not seriously diminished, thereby placing the country in a, difficult position for drawing and selling ex- change to provide for imports? Assuming their balances had been exhausted and their exports diininished, was it not a fact that the German people had sold nearly all their American, Swiss, Dutch, Scandinavian, and even their Russian securities? Was it a fact that other continental countries had been selling sterling to purchase florins, and selling those florins for marks, .in order to Create a "drawing post" in Holland? Did Germany pay gold for the Rumanian wheat, and, if so, why was this operation not shown in the Reischbank statement?</p>
        <p>Was it not a fact that nearly all her foreign saleable securities had now been exhausted, thus placing her in a, poSition of great diffi- culty in paying for her imports? As the Reichsbank Note had already been rendered inconvertible by the Law of August, 1914, and the sale of gold forbidden in Novem- ber of that year, and as the notes of. the Loan Banks, War Credit Banks, War Aid Banks, Mortgage Banks, Insurance Banks, Private Banks, and Municipal Corpora- tions had no gold against them at all, did he not think that the words " Mene, mene, tekel, upharsin," should rather be written on this towering pyramid of paper, for when all these notes had to be redeemed 'Germany would in- deed be "found wanting." Would Dr. Irelfferich give a categorical answer to the following query: Is the gold shown in the Reiehsbank statement", actually held in that institution, or are portions of it held in any institution outside Germany? Would he explain, in view of the statement that Germany had a sufficiency of food, the report of riots in Berlin, and the shooting down by the soldiers of poor women crying out for bread?</p>
        <p>Finally, he would ask him if he had forgotten that in his former speeches he had led his people to believe that they would not be taxed in respect to the cost of the war, for on about December 20th, 1915, when interrogated by the Socialists, he had to admit in the Reichstag that the war, under all circumstances, would bring the Germans face to face with colossal taxation burdens. The Bank's Affairs.</p>
        <p>Sir Edward, moving the adoption of the annual report, made a running commentary on the leading items in the balance sheet of the Bank. The profits for the year, he said, amounted to .t 1,130,976 against £1,106,808 last year. They were paying in dividends £745,803, being at the rate of 18 per cent. per annum'; they transferred to Investments Account .2642,860, writing down all securities to the prices ruling on 31st December, 1915, excepting War Loans, which. were taken at cost; they transferred to *Bank Premises' Redemption Fund £30,000, and to Officers' Pension Fund £20,000 carrying forward to next aceount .2113,597.</p>
        <p>About 1,850 of the staff had already joined the Forces, and they anticipated a further considerable number would follow them. So far as they knew 46 had lost their lives in defending their country, and he felt quite sure that the relatives of these brave men would have their sincere sympathy. The report was unanimously carried. The usual complimentary resolutions to the Chairman, Board of Directors, Managers and Staff having been passed, the meeting termin- ated with a vote of thanks to Sir Edward Holden for conduct in the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>LANCASHIRE &amp; YORKSHIRE</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE &amp; YORKSHIRE</p>
        <p>_ The annual gen,val meeting of the share- holders of the abbre bank was held in Man. Acster last Friday. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>SUPPORTING THE WAR.</head>
        <p>SUPPORTING THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas B. Moxon, who presided, claimed that it was something, that at the end of the year they • were able to say, with absolute truth, that the position of the bank gave them every satisfaction and that the number of its customers had again increased. "During the past year," he said; "we have made liberal subscriptions to the 'War Loan, besides giving substantial assistance 'to various schemes for improving the American exchange. This we have done cheerfully, and to a larger extent that the public are aware. You may be surprised to hear that the banks of the kingdom have all together subscribed fully one-third of each of the War Loans that has been brought out, in addition to the large amount of Treasury Bills they have taken. This is quite apart from the amounts taken up .by their customers, so that the banks have certainly done their share in providing, means for the war, and the confi- dence the public , has placed in them has been fully justified Profits and Dividend.</p>
        <p>"As you will see from the report, the net profits of the bank for the year have been £153,415 18s. Id., plus £21,648 3s, 3d. brought for- ward from last year. This enables us to con- tinue our dividend of 16s. a share, less income tax, to add £5,000 to our reserve fund, and to increase the amount of undivided profit carried forward by the sum of 410,400. For 43 years</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>FUNERALS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>FUNERALS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>MR. REGINALD CORBETT.</p>
        <p>Tribute To A Good Man's Life.</p>
        <p>Before the cortege proceeded, to Tilston, near Tarporley, where the burial took place on Saturday, a service was held at the residence of the deceased in Wilson-Patten-street, Warring- ton. It was conducted by the Rev. W. T. Derry. Several Warrington friends attended the inter- ment. The Rev. W. A. Beckett, who is now stationed .at Crewe, but who was lately pastor at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, where Mr. Corbett worshipped, also attended. Wreaths were sent by the congregation and Wesley Guild. During Sunday evening's service at Bewsey- road Wesleyan Church, the Rev. W. T. Derry spoke of Mr. Corbett's devout character. He was a. man who took the Word of God as the rule of his conduct, and to whom prayer was the breath of life. He walked with God and worked with God. He saw that it was far easier to in- fluence young people than those who are older, and gave himself with the utmost devotion to the spiritual welfare of the children aid young men of the school and congregation. His great aim in life was to win them for Christ and train them to be whole-hearted Christians. The preacher continued, "If Mr. Corbett stood here to-night, he would say to you who are facing life, E When you come to the end of your day, you will find that nothing matters save your relation and loyalty to your Lord. Acknowledge the claims of Christ, gladly give Him your alle- giance, and then fellow Him without swerving, and serve Him witl your might.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>MR. 'WILLIAM GILL</head>
        <p>MR. 'WILLIAM GILL</p>
        <p>Representatives Of Many Institutions</p>
        <p>Present.</p>
        <p>Many ladies and gentlemen attended the funeral at the Cemetery on Monday of Mr. William Gill, who had passed away in his 78th year at his residence, 20, Winmarleigh-street. Before proceeding to the burial ground a short service was held in Holy Trinity Church, con- ducted by the Revs. E. C. B. Carleton, E. L. J. Roberts, and T. P. Owen. The mourners were: Mrs. Gill (widow), Mr. and Mrs. George Gill (son and daughter-in-law), Major and Mrs. Gar- side (son-in-law and daughter), Mr. and Mrs. Tilston (son-in-law and daughter), Miss Gill (daughter), Mr. Roy Garside (nephew), Miss Gill-and Mrs. Dickson (sisters), Mr. and Mrs. 'Borthwick (brother-in-law and sister). Among the 'represetitatives were Alderman Arthur Bennett (Conservative Association), Mr. Thomas Mason (Conservati e Club Buildings Company),Rev. A. M. Mitchell and Mr. A. Bot- tomley (Warrington Board of Guardians, of which Mr. Gill was once a member), Messrs. John Hesketh, T. Welsby, L. R. Rowson, James Harding, E. Dyke, J. Hignett, T. Domville, W. D. Jeans, jun., E. Rose, Laws, and j. Jefferson (Holy Trinity Church, at which Mr. Gill was vicar's warden). Others present included: Dr. Langdale, Dr. Bennett, Messrs. J. Bernard, T. Brookes Hand- ley, John Fairclough, J. Rymer Young, S. P. Silcoek, John Mainwaring, Arthur Browne, A. M. Ker, J. H. Dawson, T. H. Ashcroft, W. Roberts, A. Stanley Wright, F. Milling, 11. Rogers, J. Gandy, C. Seward, C. 11. Wood, John Fair (Padgate),' John Johnson (Walton), and Foster Jackson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>RESPECTED WIREDRAWER.</head>
        <p>RESPECTED WIREDRAWER.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. Thomas Middleton And His Fellow-Workmen.</p>
        <p>Amidst scenes of .manifest regret, .the remains of Mr. Thomas Middleton, 76, Padgate4ane; a highly esteemed employe in the wiredrawing department of Messrs. Rylands Brothers, were laid to rest at the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday. As a special mark of respect a large, number .of wireclrawers were present, their com- mittee being represented by Messrs. F. Bake.: (chairman), J. Wrench, T. Seed, F. Mills J. 13ennion, Winstanley, W. Birch, H. WI-tit- field, W. Butterworth, J. Woods, R. Colley and H. Hicks. Among others present were: Mr. T. Wadsworth (general manager), Messrs. H. Ma!' dock, W. Webb and B. Farrar (department:it managers). The coffin was borne to the grave by the following wiredrawers : Messrs. Bonov,-, Tby- lor, Wrench, Ratcliffe, Mason, Hill, Sykes aild Haslam.</p>
        <p>The Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) officiated. and the mourners were: Mrs. Middleton and daughters, Mr. and Mrs. Middleton (Halifax), Mr. Royle (Altrincham), Mr. Womersley, Mr. Cook and Mr. W. Haigh. Beautiful floral tributes were received from: "His wife and children," Mr. and Mrs. Middle- ton (Halifax), " Aunt Mary and Jennie," "Ernest and Daisy," Mr. and Mrs. Ashton, Mr. and Mrs. Womersley, Mr. and Mrs. .Cook, Mr. Royle . (Fliaton), Mr, and Mrs. Havieur, "The Wiredrawers' Committee" "His 'fellow work- men rrom Rylands's." Mr. Middleton, who was a native of Denton, and was 52 years of age, had worked at Rylands's for 26 years. He lost his eldest daughter, Ada, an assistant at the Library, about ten months since. He had lived in Padgate-lane for 23 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>MR. E. E. PESTER.</head>
        <p>MR. E. E. PESTER.</p>
        <p>Members Of Oddfellows Head Tlw Cortege.</p>
        <p>On leaving Orford Church on Saturday, where a service was conducted by the Rev. G. Vale Owen, the cortege was headed by members of the National Independent Order of Oddfellows and staff and employes of the " Guardian " Office, where the late Mr. Pester was employed for many years. The Oddfellows also held a short service at the house. The mourners were: Mrs. • Pester, and 'family, Mr. and Mrs. A. Leech (brother-in-law and sister-in-law), Miss Ada Pester (sister), Mrs. Rothwell (sister-in-law), Ernest, William and Edmond Rothwell (nephews), Mrs. Hunt (niece), Misses J. and F. Boothman, Mrs. Dickenson and Mrs. Foden (cousins), Mr. H. Cherratt, Miss Cunningham, Mr., Mrs. and Miss Whitby, and Miss Roberts. Floral tributes were received from: Mrs. Pester and family, Mr. and Mrs. A. Leech, Mrs Rothwell and. family, Mrs. Foden and Mrs. Dickenson; staff and employes .." Guardian." Office, National Independent Order of Oddfellows (" Garibaldi" Lodge),- National Independent Order of Oddfellows (district), Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street; St. Luke's Cycling Club, "Hubert and Maggie," Mr. J. Clarke, Mrs. Dean and son, Mr., Mrs. and Miss C. Whitby, Misses Boothman (Middleton), Signaller T. Ellison, R.F.A., France; Gunner H. Perkins, and Miss Harrop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>LATE MR. DANIEL HAMBLETT.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. DANIEL HAMBLETT.</p>
        <p>A 'Well-known Stockton Heath Family. •</p>
        <p>Much regret will be felt at the death of Mr. Daniel Hamblett, who passed away on January .28th at his residence, 17. Egerton-street. Mr. Hamblett, who was in his 47th year, was the on of Mr. Joseph Hamblett, of Stockton Heath, whose family for a long time controlled the Stockton Heath St)ade Works. Almost his entire working life was spent in the service of the Bridgewater Canal Co., afterwards the Manchester Ship Canal Co. In his youth he rode the packet horse to Manchester and in later years served as a carter. His early reli- gious training was received at Providence Sun- day School, but as a young man he became attached to the Baptist Church at Wash-lane, of which he has been a member (luring the past nineteen years. His loss will be keenly felt, and the large number of friends who assembled at the funeral was eloquent of the esteem in which he was held.</p>
        <p>The interment took place on Tuesday at the Hill Cliffe Burial Ground. The services were conducted by the Rev. J. F. Matthews, who travelled from Sheffield to fulfil the duty of reading the burial service at the funeral of an old and esteemed friend, and who paid an eloquent tribute to his character and worth. ,A mid .much, said the reverend gentleman, that was strong and rugged in the life that had passed away, there was a strong undercurrent of calm, for hi ti nature was ever sustained by the "peace that passeth all understanding." Among those present were Mrs. Hamblett (widow), Arthur Hamblett (only son), Mr. Richard Hamblett and Mr. James Hamblett (brothers), Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Heyes, Mrs. Leach, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Heyes, Mr. awl</p>
        <p>... \ , , F. i; tt</p>
        <p>N</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Robert Reyes, Mr. and Mrs. John Heyes, Mr. Jesse Leach, Mrs. Richard Hamblett, Mrs. James Hainblett, Mr. Cecil 'Pritchard, Mrs. Newton and Mrs. Grounds.</p>
        <p>The body was carried by Mr. J. R. Conway, Mr. J. B. Wilkinson, Mr. Jos. Unsworth, Mr. J. Appleton, Mr. H. L. Homey awd Mr. P. Grounds, members of the Sunday School Bible Class at Wash-lane.</p>
        <p>The Ship Canal Co. and N.C.R. Workers were represented by Mr. F. Barker, Mr. J. Fisher and Mr. W. Adams.</p>
        <p>'The Independent Order of ' Reehabites were represented 1)3, Mr. William Wright and Mr. S. Fernley.</p>
        <p>:A number of the members of the Wash-lane Church were present, amongSt, whom %cure: Mr: and Mrs. W. F.-Wilkinson,' Mr. • and Mrs. Robert Jones, Miss E. Jones, Mr.. and Mrs.S. - Albinson, Mr. I. Wilkinson, Mrs. F. 'W. Grounds. Mrs. 'a Heald, Mrs. Adams, Mrs. P. Cliffe, Mk:- A. Lightfoot, Mrs.- J. Fairhurst, Mrs. F. Pearson, Mrs. J. Buckley, Mrs. 3. Muskett. Miss H. E. Smith, Miss A. Caldwell., Miss A. Wills, Mrs. P. Harrison, Mrs. A. New- ton, Mrs. R. Hartsman, Mrs. E. Kenwright. Mrs. Peter Gripe, and Miss IT. E. Grounds. At the graveyard were also Mr. W. Cooper, Mr. R. Turner, Mr: C. Hamblett (Royal Flying Corps), and Mr. A. B. Marsh. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER, RUSSELL AND CO.'S v. STOCKTON HEATH VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Teams, representing the above met at the range of the "former on Tuesday evening.. Con- ditions: 15 a side, 12 to count; 10 rounds at 25 yards; orthoptic 'sights. The result was in favour of the home team. Scores:—</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell's: C. Legge 99, J. F. Fer- gusson 97, 3. Towers 93, H. 0. Bennett 91, F. Hare 91, R. Turner 91, J. Phillips 90, J. Corlett 90, G. MePhie 90, W. Banks 89, H. Nicholson 85, J. DieDermid 85; 1091; W. Leach 84, P. Brownridge 75; total for 14, 1,250.</p>
        <p>„ Stockton Heath• Volunteers: M. A. Piercy 95, A. Shepherd 94, R. Swinton 94, T. Fletcher 92, W. A. Thompson 89, L. R. Rowson 86, A. Ilulme 83, J. H. Fletcher 82, L. Fairclough 81, H. S. Page 80, B. W. -Clough 72, P. Chadwick 67; 1,015; W. Howard 66, J. Kelly 65, G. H. Eccles 55; total for 15, 1,201.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-05_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>4th SOUTH LANCS. v. APPLETON RIFLE</head>
        <p>4th SOUTH LANCS. v. APPLETON RIFLE</p>
        <p>CLU B.</p>
        <p>An, interesting match took place at the Drill Halt -on.. Wednesday between the Staff :of the Administrative Centre 4th Battalion South Lancs. Regiment and a team selected from the Appleton Rifle Club. The conditions were la shots at a S.M.R.C. 25 yards' match target with orthoptic sights, and 10 shots at a S.M.R.C. .50 yards' target with open sights. The:Aoldiers are, of course, more at. home, with the service rifle than the miniature " match " rifles, and consequently suffered defeat on the aggregate by 23 points, although they Were seven points to the, good on the open eights total. Scores Appleton. Orth. Open • Sights. Sights. Total.. R. W. D. Cool; F. Chadwick E. Dyke - BO, • 81 161! J. W. Elliott J. It. Pletcher ' F. L. Garside A. Ithlme - M. A. Picrcy. 93 85 178* E. L. Robison - 97 187" .</p>
